      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>SELECTED READINGS.</head>
        <p>SELECTED READINGS.</p>
        <p>JUDICIOUS RECILEATION.—The father or the mother of a family may easily feel a little puzz'ed ! as to what special form of recreation the members thereof should choose and they (the parents)should encourage. For although pleasurable exercise and amusement are wonderfully successful in keeping the doctor's chariot away from the door, they must be well timed and judiciously carried out. The old saying, "All work and no play makes Jack a dull boy," is exceedingly true. But, on the other hand, recreatian must not be carried to excess either physically or morally. Physically, recreation can be overdone. It matters nothing what outdoor game be chosen or commends itself to ore— whether cr:cket, tennis, football bowls, or golf • or what kind of pleasurable exercise—whether boat- ing, riding, walking, or cycling, but, while enjoying It, we must never approach tco closely to the I boundary-line of exhausting fatigue. The same may be said for indoor games and exercises; but even those that do not entail muscular excitor, but are better described as pastimes, should never be continued until they have become a weariness. It may not be generally known that so apparently trivial an act as that of yawning betrays a tired heart, and is an effort of Nature to stimu- late that organ to the getting rid of venous blood :by pumping it into the lungs. What is commonly known by the term "stretching one's self" (and this, by the way, is usually accompanied by yawning) is another sign of fatigue, and has for its object, like the yawn, the stimulating or a tired heart. Those who lead sedentary lives sitting long with head and back bent over the desk, acd probably thinking hard all the while, are subject. to fits of stretching and yawning. Out they ought to go, into the fresh air, as soon as nature vouch- safes these warnings. . . . These are really not trivial matter, for, whatever we do, we should not ' age the &amp;art. It is not that heart-cliseass in a valvilar or organic shape is to be greatly feared, but something as bad in the long run, though ! the symptoms may never at any time be very urgent. What we mean is this: if we 1 neglect recreation, if we work too hard and ; sit too loug at desk work, the heart becomes, feeble, and virtually old. If, on the other hand, ! we carry exercise to excess; if we row too hard, if we constantly "spurt" when cycling, if we are in ! the habit of lifting weights unsuited to our strength, then we are apt to stretch the right side of the heart. And the life of one who has either a , feeble heart or an enlarged right-heart is not to be envied. It is mostly a weariness, and one of:hated , thus is generally nervous, and hardly ever knows what it is to be otherwise than tired. . . . But one may be guilty of excess in recreative exercises ! or amusements fro:n even a moral point of view. The converse of the old saying about Jack and play IS equally true; all play and little work entirely lunfits Jack for the battle of life, and he soon falls ! to the rear, and is heard of nevermore.—From ! Cassell's Book of the household. THE LIBERTIES OF MATURITY.—Among the fond fancies of children is the belief that when . i 'grown up" there will be no more lessons to learn, no more commands to obey, no more scolding to , endure. They will be men and women, no longer children in the nursery—pupils in the schoolroom; ! therefore they will be free, independent, above rebuke and beyond coercion. It is a helpful belief, lending them the aid of hope wherewith to assist patience during the dark days of the actual, in ! expectation of the cloudless skies of the ideal. . .</p>
        <p>k.Alid it is about as baselefs as the mist wreaths of the morning, as vain as the dreams which float . through the gate of ivory to weave their lovely lies c about the head of the smiling sleeper. It is a fancy ! that is twin brother to the pumpkin coach of Cinderella, which certain young enthusiasts believe ' could be recreated for their behoof, when elder ' sisters go out a pleasuring in their best frocks and they are left behind in garden hats and their old patched boots. Poor credulous enthusiasts 1 poor I deluded dreamers! nourishing themselves on impos- ; Sible hopes and visionary expectations which have ' but one merit—that of making the long road of the present seem shorter by the light they shed into' the future that of lessening the actual burden by visions of the ideal release. As if we were ever free from rebuke, lessons, com- mand, coercion—as if that high-sounding phrase, "AU men are born free sad equal" had one poor farthing's worth of common seuse in it I True is it that the child has one form to which he must . nb- mit, and the man another. The method is not Identical, but the spirit is the same. The liberties and immunities, dreamed of in youth as inherent to the grown up, fade away in the light of experience as those dreams themselves fade when the morning 42 fay Fothe and "getting up time" has come With it. We must change the dress but not the substance—the venue but not the suit. We are still under the harrow of circumstance, if not under the direct lash of a short-tempered master. Fortune nubs us if our Nanna no longer spanks. Our dis- appointments are of a graver kind than those which once agonised our childish heart and cast desolation into our soul. The Grownups Cave not got it at all their own way, and fate and ' fortune still hold them in subjection. But one grand glory they do possess—the joy of struggle —the grandeur of resistance to an evil fate—the superb rejoicing when the man's power has over- come, and the hydra of difficulty, of adversity, lies dead on the broad plain.—The Queen. WHILE Erasmus was in the monastery of Tarpon, there was in the garden a pear-tree bearing fruit, of which the father superior was very fond, and which he wished to preserve for his own use. Erasmus, who was, in this respect, of the same taste with his superior, rose several days running, very early in the morning, to pluck some of it, without anyone know- Ang. .-This obliged the superior, who perceived that the number of the pears was diminishing, to watch one morning at the window of his cell, in order to</p>
        <p>discover the robber, whom he had at been able otherwise to detect. It must be remarked, that one of the monks in the convent was lame. Well, one day while the superior was upon guard, he discerned upon the tree a monk busy gathering pears; but as it was not yet much light, he tesolved to wait a little, without saying I anything, in order to discover with certainty 1 who it was. He made some noise, however, which lErasmus heard; and for fear of being found out, he descended without delay, and returned limping towards the convent. The superior, who believed I he was now sure of the pear-stealer, thought it best to be silent in the meantime, and to wait till day, light, that he might censure him in full assembly. As soon as he could call the monks together, the</p>
        <p>„ superior, after making many choice remarks upon saintly obeojience, turned towards the lame brother, • and accused him of having violated it in a manner the most flagrant in the world, by stealing the pears from the garden, notwithstanding his repeated pro- bibitions. It was in vain for the poor brother to Maintain his innocence; this only augmented the wrath of the superior, who imagined he had recog- mised the culprit by a mark that could not be mistaken. He was accordinzly condemned to a baavy nenance, in spite of all hi. 7—QuAcirERY. --A South Midas quack-doctor, ^occulting a farmer near Preston, the other day, said be had been sent by Government to cure the potato disease. At his request the farmer produced a diseased potato, which he halved, and scattered upon it a few grains of carbonate of soda and tar. Uric acid. When it effervesced, he said "That _ _ _</p>
        <p>Is the disease ; you see it comes out at once.'" Tak- ing next a sound potato, he scattered a few grains of soda alone, and no effervescence being caused the farmer accepted it as a proof that it was unaffected, and, being anxious to possess the mira- culous powder, he bought the professor's stock, at 73. 6d. a packet, and received instruction "to fill A tub with water, throw the powder into it, and dip the potato-sets carefully before planting." A ROGUE OUTWITTED.—The New York Reveille shows how a Yankee grocer lately trapped a thief. The latter, after purchasing some wool, seized a moment when he thought the grocer's back turned, to put a large cheese into his bag. The grocer saw what had happened, gave the change, and then took hold of the bag, exclaiming :—" Why bless me, I must have reckoned the weight wrong." —" Oh, no," said the other, "you may be sure you have not, for I counted with you."—" Well,. Well, we won't dispute the matter, it is easily tried," said Mr. S., putting the bag into the scales, 4ain. "There." said he," I told you so—knew I was right—made a mistake of nearly twenty rounds ; however, if you don't want the whole, you needn't have take part of it out."— •" No, no!" said the other, "I guess I will take the whole." And this he did, after paying for his dishonesty by receiving the skim-milk cheese at the nriCe of wool. GENTLEMAN went up to an apple stall, and asked the old woman if the apples were sweet. With a wise wag of her head, she replied— " Wet ranted, sir—st)len !" Nononv but a loving mother could weep.bitter tears over a lost child, and then spank it ener- getic:al y for rel MRS. WITUESBY : "What cud your cook do? Go off in a huff 1" Mrs. Planklugton ; "No. She went off in my tailor-made gown."</p>
        <p>" CAPITAL " CHAT.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the missionary committee of his denomination on Thursday complaints were made that he had never ceased to criticise its policy, and an ex-president roundly declared that such critic- ism ought in decency, if persisted in at all, to come from the outside. It was further intimated to him that exception would be taken at this week's meet- ing of the district Synod to his connection with the SWiss re-union holiday scheme,under the regulation that no Wesleyan minister must engage in trade. Dr. Lunn accepted the challenge,and at once sent in his resignation.</p>
        <p>An amusing incident occurred while the Parlia- mentry terrace was crowded on Monday afternoon with members and friends waiting to see Captain Boyton walking on the water. A steamer crowded with executionists was passing, and some wag on board attracted the attention of the occupants of the terrace with a stentorian " Hey." Having thus riveted legislative eves on his personality the ir- reverent joker yelled out, " Hurry up with that Home Rule Bill." An old proverb assures us that there is many a true word said in joke,and beyond all question it would be a good thing if the House took the suggestion of the river humorist seriously to heart. --- A good story is told of the Rev. Josiah Bateman, rector of Southchurch, near Southend, who has just died in his 91st year. lie was a strong supporter of the Low Church party, and much to his own dis- pleasure, had a son also beneficed, who favoured Ritualistic practices. The son often asked his father to occupy his pulpit, but the father for a long time refused. Eventually the latter acceded to the wishes of his son,ascended the pulpit,and announced as his text, "Lord have mercy on my son; for he is a lunatick." (S. Matt.,xvii.,ls.) On this was based a vigorous attack on his son's Ritualistic practices, whose feelings it is kinder to imagine than describe.</p>
        <p>Mr. Gladstone came into the House on Monday evening just as Mr. Storey was concluding his speech announcing his determination to vote for Mr. Victor Cavendish's amendment. The look of surprise that came over Mr. Gladstone's face when Mr. Morley informed him of this unexpected deser- tion set some of the younger Tories sniggering. Mr. Disraeli under such circumstances would have be- come even more sphinx-like than usual, but Mr. Gladstone's face faithfully reflects every passing emotion of his mind. And young Tories may be re- minded that frankness and suavity of countenance are preferred by most honest Englishmen to studied Oriental impassivity.</p>
        <p>One of the few clergymen of the Church of Eng- land who were in favour of disestablishment and disendowment has been removed by the death of the Rev. Henry Anthony Noel, for the past twelve years rector of St. Clement's, Longsight, Manches- ter. A Broad Churchman and a steadfast disciple of Stanley, of Kingsley, and of Maurice, he took a deep interest in all social and labour questions,shop as,istants and railway workers in particular finding in him a friend who did his utmost to better their condition. He was of the advanced Radicals till the disruption in 1885, when he went with the se- cessionists, but he did not cut himself off from his old friends, and ho looked for reunion. Cremation found in him a steadfast supporter, and by his par- ticular injunction his remains will be cremated.</p>
        <p>There is a widely-prevailing belief which is shared by many of the parliamentary freshmen that when the House of Commons is in Committee the Speaker is at liberty to amuse himself as he thinks fit. Nothing could be further from the truth. Ttie temporary delegation of the presidency to Mr. Mellor enables Mr. Peel to seek the seclusion of his own apartments, and renders possible the substi- tution of a eet dinner for the hasty chop which constitutes his evening meal when the mace is on the table; but, inasmuch, as he is liable to be sent for at any moment to settle a controverted point, or to deal with a contumacious member, he can- not leave the precincts of the building. When Mr. Mellor is in the chair, the Speaker is usually to be found in his library before dinner. Some- times he takes a brief constitutional on the terrace.</p>
        <p>The Rev. C. H. Kelly, the ex-President thus primarily responsible for the removal of Dr. Lunn from the Methodist ministry, is a sexagenarian who is in charge of the publishing department of his church ,under the designation of the rlook Steward. He has the prompt masterful instincts a martinet, which stood him in good stead as an army chaplain, which he first became as far back as the year 1859. He was instrumental, in conjunction with that ultra-Protestant, the late Dr. Rule, in winning for Wesleyan soldiers many important religious privi- leges,and he had a share also in the somewhat diffi- cult task of inducing the Admiralty to concede to Methodist ministers the right to visit members of their communion in the Royal Navy. The number of men in the united services affected by these regulations now exceeds twenty thousand. Mr. Kelly would be taken by a stranger for a rubicund white- whisleredmajor,whosecorns itwas important toavoid</p>
        <p>Mr. Rowland Whitehead, who is to enter the bonds of wedlock with Miss Ethel Rathbone,daugh- ter of one of the' merchant princes " of Liverpool, is the second son of Sir James Whithead, Bart., M.P. Ile is a barrister in Lincoln's Inn, and though quite a young man he is already in the enjoyment of a lucrative and increasing practice. His eldest brother, Mr. George Whitehead, is a stock broker in the City (Whitehead, Halsted, and Chown) ; and being, like his father, an enthusiastic politician, he is President of the Bromley Liberal Association, and has been several times pressed to contest vari- OM constituencies. During Sir James Wnitehearl's year of office as Lord Mayor he wasmarried to Miss Ascroft, daughter of a well-known Lancashire magistrate, and he lives in a charming villa at Bromley. Another brother is proprietor of the art color printing works in Baldwin-gardens (Gilbert Whitehead and Co.), whilst a fourth is pursuing a promising career at Oxford. The three eldest graduated there with distinction, and all of them are on the right side of thirty,</p>
        <p>The Moot Hall, Malden, where a Bench of Essex magistrates has been sitting to inquire into the death of Police-Sergeant Eves, is a fine old fifteenth century building, with a square, time-worn tower of a dark red colour, forming a very prominent feature in the main street. For many hundreds of years it has served as a town hall, court house, and prison to the ancient little borough which has its mayor, aldermen, and town clerk, its commission of the peace, and its court of quarter seseions. In the Munireent Room there is u goodly display of red- sealed charters signed by .royal hands, from Henry downwards. Some of the charters relieved Maldon freemen from many curious items of feudal service, such as "hydnge. scutage, stallage, dangeld, and hostage." It was under Mary's Charter of 1533 that the Corporation was called upon to provide a prison in the Moot Hall, and within the liberty tumbrel, pillory, and gallows." These last have passed away, but there is still a display of polished manacles just inside the door.</p>
        <p>The visit of the Duke of Devonshire to Ireland calls to mind the story of how the Devonshire ' family came to have the estates in that country. During the reign of Elizabeth, Sir Walter Raleiah got some forty-two thousand acres, exclusive of waste, hog, and mountain;out of the plunder of the Earl of Desmond's territories. About the same time a London judge's clerk, named Richard Boyle, who had graduated in Ireland as a forger and horse thief, was raised to the Deputy-Escheatorship of Munster. When Raleigh was imprisoned, Boyle offered him ..£5,010 for his forty-two thousand acres. The offer was accepted, and £5OO paid, but that was all Boyle ever did pay. Later on Boyle became Earl of Cork, and obtained from James I. patents for all las plunder which, in addition to the forty- two thousand acres, included rights over sea and land granted by King John to the monks of Lis- more. Boyle married his children into English aristocratic families, and that is how the Devon- shire family became possessed of their county Cork and Waterford estates, which are said to be worth .530,000 per year in these days.</p>
        <p>Dr. IL S. Lunn, who on Saturday placed his re- signation as a Wesleyan minister in the bands of Dr. Rigg, is a young Irishman, who took a medical degree with the intention of proceeding to India as a medical missionary. He was in India barely a twelvemonth when his constitution wassoimpaired by the climate that he was compelled to relinquish his intended career and return to London. Hem be became an assistant to Mr. Price Hughes at the West London Mission, and it was not long before he published in Mr. Hughes' paper an attack upon the miqAionary policy of his church. After some controversy a small commission was appointed to investigate these charges, Mr. Henry Fowler being a member of it,and the report of the commissioners was to the effect that Dr. Lunn's charges had not been supported by the evidence. Hereupon be was permitted to retain his status as a minister without</p>
        <p>s 4 ,0,?%0rN1 THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 20, 1893. LADIES' LETTER</p>
        <p>+</p>
        <p>DEARES? NELLIE, As I have already stated, the blouse is still a favourite article of dress and the splendid weather of the past few weeks have brought them out in great variety. Although it is lo4aee-Etting it must be cut out with the greatest precision end fitted abcurately, and unless the figure of the wearer is faultless, it is a mistake to buy a ready-made blouse, for if the waist is too long it bunches up, eeCially when put into a yoke. As to the large sleeve, they require as much care in enttlng out as do the bodices, although to the inexperienced eye they have such a free and easy air and look so easy to manipulate.</p>
        <p>One of the newest and most original blouses of the season is that forming crossed folds by the fronts gathered on the shoulders and cut with long sloped ends, which, after crossing the chest, pass round to the back, and fasten with a bow or rosette of the material; or the ends are cut extra long, and are drawn round again to the front, and furnish with a bow in front. In this pretty blouse with the ends brought round to the front a nice effect can he obtained by mixing contrasting materials, or by using a combination, such as velvet and wool, or silk and cropon. Where the blouse is of foulard or shot silk, a velvet plastron and collar i exceedingly pretty, or the one material can be striped with narrow jet or gimp, or have rows of. itavti op. laid over it. A lovely Atibilardrea-dn be made of starch. blue crepon with a small brown silk spot. Romid the skirt hem is a five-inch flounce of brown guipure hem run through with a double row of pale blue b4be ribbon. The full bodice is gathered into a broad folded waistband of dark brown satin, and is ornamented with, graduated shoulder frills of brown guipure run through with ribbon. This g-oWii day be rgad9 in satin, delaine, Pongee silk, or washing mate-ritifi, and may be trimmed with ecru lace instead of the brown guipure. The skirt is of the bell or circu'ar shape. The foot trimming may, if preferred, be made of a flounce of self material. The bodice is cut with a short round blasque, to be worn inside the skirt. The front of bodice can ho covered with full kids of the. material, gathered round the neck, and the plain band collar lined with sarccnet and interlined with canvas. The fashionable leg-of-mutton-sleeves should be cut in both lining and material, gathered on the shoulder, but plain and tight-fitting from elbow to wrist.</p>
        <p>One of this season's daintiest productions is a hat composed of alternate rows of fine black chip and coarse green straw, ornamented with full upstanding bows of shot brown and green silk ribbon, while two handsome black ostrich feathers curl upwards on the left side, and are clasped with a handsome buckle of dark green enamel. Moire and watered silks are again coming to the fore. These are not really expensive, and make up smartly for an entire dress or used as trimmings only. A delightful gown of dark blue diagonal striped serge is made with the skirt finished with fine bias bands of black moire, each band piped at either side with a coarse cord of lichen-green satin. In the bodice the sleeves and back only are of blue serge. There is a short, loose coat in front, from the side seams of moire, and above this a full frilled collar and revers of similar material out- lined with green satin. The vest is of gathered green satin, with a band of Russia c embroidery in fawn, green and bla# across the Nice, which it not frilled or gathered, and again across the basque, which is an inch or so below 'the waist, and circular iz shape.</p>
        <p>A costume fertile in useful suggestions- is made of velvet, but it -tan also be of woollen, or of silk. The bodice has an applique of cream guipure in front under a short jacket pleated to, show the lace. Jet fringe edges the curve of the jacket, and trims it rightly. The collar and girdle are of satin of the same blue shade. The sleeves are without trimming. The full skirt, slightly long at the back, has three narrow flounces headed by a flat fold. Blouse bodices in various styles are now being worn by little girls and boys and can either be composed of silk, or a thin unshrinkible flannel. A new pattern, and one which is most useful, has a stiff band collar, to which a half yoke is added at the back ; the fronts are slightly full, and fastened down the centre of a wide hem with pearl buttoms, the remaining portion of the back is gathered to the yoke, and meets the fronts under the arms. This blouse is completed with gathesed shirt sleeves, and is intended to be worn under the skirt or knickerbockers ; a sash, is then fastened round the waist. Yours very sincerely, JENNIE</p>
        <p>American Dough Nuts. Take one pint of sour milk, one cup (large) of soft sugar. half a cup of lard or cooking butter, two eggs,and one teaspoonful of carbonate of soda ; mix to a moderate stiffness, roll half an inch thick, cut into nuts, and fry in boiling lard till a light brown. This will make a large quantity. • '</p>
        <p>' Jordan Cake. Two teacupfuls flour, 1 teacupful sugar, 1i teaspoon- fuls baking powder, a pinch of salt, 2ozs. lard, 1 egg, and a little milk to mix to a stiff batter. Mix the flour, sugar, salt, and baking powder, rub in the lard. and add the egg well beaten and the milk. Pour in a well-but- tered dripping tin, and be in a brisk oven till a needle, pricked in the centre, will come out clean. Let it stand a little, then cut it up into squares, split open, and spread with raspberry jam, and put the two pieces to- gether again.</p>
        <p>top put a teaspoonful of the following mixture :—One egg, its weight in butter, sugar, and flour, and a tea- spoonful of baking powder. Beat the butter and sugar to a cream, add by degrees the egg well beaten an the flour alternately, and then the grated rind of half a lemon. Add the baking powder last of all. Bake in a quick oven for 20 minutes ; turn on to a sieve and sift sugar over.</p>
        <p>About Kitchen Tables.</p>
        <p>Housekeepers who have never had a tin-covered table for kitchen use are still unacquainted with coo of the most valuable articles of domestic economy. An ordin- ary kitchen table takes kindly to the metal cover. jit a sheet of tin on the table and perforate the edge -for tacking. The tin should cover the thickness of the board top, that it may be tacked on the underside of the table. A table so covered needs no scrubbing, is impervious to hot kettles, sheds grease as the proverbial duck's back does water, and, in fact, cheers the heart of the kitchen- maid more than anything else except her wages and her "company." The woman who does her own work should not let another dawn find her without one, said a wise family man : "Anything which simplifies the domestic labour of the household should be regarded as of distinct benefit to mankind," and he was speaking of a tin- covered kitchen table, upon whose virtues his wife was , dilating. A fair and cheap substitute is found by cover- ing tables with marble cloth sold sometimes for wash- stands and children's bibs. It was Mephistopheles him- self who designed kitchen tables out of soft pine boards. With their absorbent andspreading qualities a tiny drop of grease is quickly convered into an unsightly blotch, and a hot handle or kettle sears its iudeliable mark across their surface.</p>
