      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>WARRINGTON JUNIOR, RUGBY LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON JUNIOR, RUGBY LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>PRESENTATION OF CUP AND MEDALS.</p>
        <p>The presentation of the Challenge Cup and Medals to the Parish Church football team, who have won the championship of the Warrington Junior Rugby League, took place on Monday night at the National School. There was a good number of t he followers of the Parish Church team present, and also the Rector (Rev. F. W. Willis), Rev. F. C. Timmins, Mr. Percy Davies (the donor of the cup and medals), Mr. H. Ashton (captain of the Warr- ington team), &amp;c. The Rector, in introducing Mr. 1 Davies, expressed his feeling of gratitude to him for the noble cup which he had given. He had great sympathy with the game of football, and it gave him great plea- sure that the two clubs in his parish had won a cup and medals.He hoped that they would win still greater honours lin the future. He was glad that there was a stronger 'feeling against the use of improper language on the fcot- ball field. In conclusion he congratulated the team on I their weli deserved hcneurs. (Applause.)—Mr. Davies said that it was a great pleasure to him to give the cup an medals. He was president of the Warrington F.C., and was proud of it,as there was nothing political about it, and he was not a politican. He was also proud to mention that in the three teams that represented War- rington in Rugby football, everyone of the players was Warrington born. (Applause.) In the case of the clubs such as Swinton and Association teams,they would find that a number of the players were strangers to the town, they played for. With regard to the local challenge Cup he bad intended to give it to the winners of the compe- tition, but a number of the committee would not listen to it. hen they saw the cup was such a handsome one, they thought it was too good for only one year,and therefore it would be given to the winning team each year. He hoped to see the play carried on without any roughness, and he never saw any misbehaviour on the football field by Warringtonians. It was always the fol- lowers of the visiting teams who had misbehaved, and then the Warrington team had to suffer for it. Mr. Davies then presented the Cup to the Rev. F. C. Tim- mins, president of the Parish Church club, amidst loud applause. The cup is a very handsome one, and bears the inscription " Warrington Junior Rugby Football League Competition, presented by Mr. Percy Davies, president of the Warrington football club."—Rev. F. C. Timmins, in reply,thankeel Mr. Davies for his generosity in presenting them with the magnificent cup. He would thank him on behalf of the club of which he was presi- dent,and he thought they had deserved to win it as they had got 12 points out of a possible 16, and also scored 6 goals 12 tries, against 1 goal 4 tries. He thought that Rugby football was a very fine game, although it was becoming a little unpopular. He thought that the fault did not lie in the players but in the spectators, who would not cheer good play by the opposite side, but were always ready to cheer even rough play by their own side. He also thought that there should be no betting on the football field. He did not say this so much as a clergyman, as a sportsman. It was betting that had spoiled horseracing. There were three good points in connection with the Rugby game—it was a manly game, it taught one to keep his temper, and it was an unsel- fish game.—Mr. Davies then presented a medal with the player's and Mr. Davies's name upon it to the following members of the team :—Messrs. Houghton (captain), Boldsworth, T. Welsby, Blenkinsop, S. Mason, Daniels, Woodward, A. Miller, Howard, T. Gallop, P. Carey, Frieth, Lawton,Nevina and Garrity. The meeting closed with three cheers for Mr. Davies.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>CHOLERA AGAIN. _- A DEATH OFFICIALLY CERTIFIED.</head>
        <p>THE CHOLERA AGAIN. _- A DEATH OFFICIALLY CERTIFIED.</p>
        <p>NVANDSSECK, SCIILESWIG, TIJESBAY.—A workman, who has been suffering from illness presenting choleraic symptoms, died yesterday in the town of Schiffbek, in Holstein. Dr. Cremer, representing the chief magistrate of the province, and Dr. Flunnius, district physician, have opened an inquiry. Twelve workmen who lived in the same house as deceased have been removed to tem- porary buildings, where they will be completely isolated. Most stringent precautionary measures have been adopted to prevent the spread of the diseve.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>NEW FORM OF LOTTERY.</head>
        <p>A NEW FORM OF LOTTERY.</p>
        <p>AN ENTERPRISING PUBLICAN.</p>
        <p>The Stipendary Magistrate of Salford was on Wednes- day called upon to deal with a novel form of lottery which had been established by a local publican for the extension of his trade. The offence consisted in dis- tributing tickets to customers each of which entitled the holder to participate in a draw for prizes. Tickets to the number of 9,000 or more were distributed in the space of a fortnight. For the defendant it was stated that he acted in ignorance of the law, and that so soon as public attention had been called to the distribution and its illegality pointed out, he put an end to it. The magistrate, in these circumstances, took a lenient view of the offence, and fined the man 20s. and costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>LORD SALISBURY'S VISIT TO BELFAST.</head>
        <p>LORD SALISBURY'S VISIT TO BELFAST.</p>
        <p>In addition to the other engagements connected with the Marquis of Salisbury's forthcoming visit to Ulster, it has now been arranged to add a new feature in the shape of a procession on a large scale, which is to take place on Saturday next. The processionists who are engaged to number between 20,000 and 40,000. will march past a platform erected in front of the Mayor's residence in Clatendon-street. Londonderry, when Lord Salisbury will witness the assemblage. It is understood that contingents will arrive from Fermanagh, Tyrone, Donegal, and all parts of the county of Londonderry. Laird Salisbury will address a great meeting in Belfast, on Wednesday evening, and will next day proceed to Barons Court as the guest of the Duke of Abercorn. On Friday Lord Salisbury will speak in the Guildhall, Derry, a building capable of accommodating 3,500 persons.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>LORD ROBERTS AND HIS PATENT OF PEERAGE. .</head>
        <p>LORD ROBERTS AND HIS PATENT OF PEERAGE. .</p>
        <p>EXTRAORDINARY INCIDENT AT THE HOUSE OF</p>
        <p>LORDS.</p>
        <p>Lord Roberts went to the House of Lords on Tuesday evening to be sworn a peer of Parliament. Be has not been in England since he was raised to the Upper House. On arriving at the house he was asked to pro- duce his patent of nobility, but he had not brought it with him and in fact it transpired that he had left it in India. This excluded the noble lord for the moment. The question whether their lordships would be within their rights in dispensing with the production of the patent on special motion is (says correspondent) under- stood to be engaging the attention of constitutional authorities in the Upper House. If the exception can be made there is no doubt that it will be done in favour of Lord Roberts.</p>
        <p>The Royal Laundresses say Critchley's " STARCH GLOSS a unequalled for imparting a beautifnl finish to starched linen, !cc., either cold or boiling water starch. Mr. —, Corn- wall, says My wife will not finish eur linen without your " Starch Gloss." From Mrs. —, Bradford : "Dear sh lam pleased to inform you that I have tried many so-called Starch Glazes and Polishes, but find none eq-aal to yours." It should be used in every house. Sold at Id., 3d., had 6d., by most stanch sellers. If not in stock will procure it. T. CHITCHLRY. Blackburn. Proprietor.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Mr. Johnson, farmer, Draycott, North Staffordshire, died on Wednesday from blood poisoning caused by attentag a cow which waa suffering from anthrax.</p>
        <p>Mr- S. Smith, M.P., writes that there is no truth in the report that he was about to retire from the repre- sentation of Flintshire.</p>
