      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>DARKWOO D. A TALE OF LONEMOOR.</head>
        <p>DARKWOO D. A TALE OF LONEMOOR.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER' XXXII. A FOUL DEED.</p>
        <p>Lord Dark wood staggered back, horrified and appalled, before that strange, unearthly figure in the yellow stream of light in the open window of the library.</p>
        <p>A niortal sickness seemed to have come upon him. Leaning against a table for support, trembling as with palsy, his eyes starting, his face livid and ghastly, and with that cry of agonised terror still quivering upon his white lips, the marquis presented a pitiable spectacle. And still that figure in the window stared at him with a strange steadfast gaze. Lord Darkwood groped behind him with one shaking hand and found a chair. He sank into it heavily, and put both hands before his face. A dead silence succeeded, during which his lord- ship's excited senses noted the loud ticking of the little Sevres clock, the dropping of a coal in the grate, and were tortured by the sounds.</p>
        <p>Lord Darkwood was not a superstitious man. He had no faith in the supernatural, yet his incredulity was lost in this moment of supreme terror. It seemed to him that the spectre was drawing nearer to him with noiseless steps, and with new terror be dropped his bands. The figure had entered the window and pauge4 just within it, silent, stern, and motionless. There was something of wander in those deep eyes, some- thing of mocker' and amusement in the expression of the mouth.</p>
        <p>Trembling in every limb, straggling with the awful hoi'rOt that held him as in iron fetters, fearing lest the spec tip should come nearer to him, the marquis faltered out tz a hollow voice, holding up both his hands as if to ward off an attack— " Stand back I What do you want 9" No answer came from the visitor, who drew a step nearer. -</p>
        <p>"Back, I say "cried the marquis, excitedly, cower- ing in his chair. "Do not touch me r The stranger halted, and the mocking smile on his mouth deepened. "Why are you here ?" whispered Lard Darkwood. The stranger looked amazed. Then he spoke, and the voice was surely human. "You seem to take me for a ghost, Captain Toll- ish."</p>
        <p>Lord Darkwood started, and the ghastliness of his countenance was horrible to witness,</p>
        <p>" Don't come nearer !" be said again, in that hollow whisper. "Do you not recognize me as human, Captain Tollish ?" inquired the stranger. "You cannot seriously believe me a ghost! This is an odd greet- ing for a man who returns to his own house after shipwreck and an illness almost unto death." A strange cry broke from Lord Darkwood's lips. What! was the figure not spectral ? He had rather it had been a ghost than human. Better, a million times, his terror of the supernatural than, have to yield up title and place to another. " Alive I" he gasped. Alive !" "You see that I am alive, Captain Torn*" he said, looking at the marquis with a cool scorn. "put away your petty, childish terrors, and face the truth, for I am alive, and come back to my own." Lord Darkwood could not speak. Slowly a com- prehension of the truth, so fatal to himself, pierced his bewildered mind. And with that comprehension, came an awful despair that was bitterer than death.. "Lord Darkwood," he exclaimed, huskily, scarcely knowing what he said, " they—theY said vou were dead 1"</p>
        <p>"I had a narrow escape of it, I own," said the Intruder, quietly, his blue eyes bright with amuse- ment still, his mouth quivering in a faint smile under his fair moustache. "I suppose you beard the story of the wreck from Foster? Naturally, you supposed me drowned." Lord Darkwood nodded assent. "My escape from drowning was not at all marvel- lous," said the 'visitor, calmly. "When the storm came on and the yacht became unmanageable, I went below and buckled on my life preserver. I had barely returned to the deck when the Sylphide went over on her beam ends, and I was swept into the sea. I heard the cries of the sailors—then the darkness, and the waters came and swallowed us up. I floated, of course, for hours, exerting myself at times to swim, in order that I might not become chilled. At daybreak I was picked up by a little Corsican vessel. I was then exhausted. I was taken to Corsica, nursed through the fever that followea my exposure, and as soon as I was sufficiently recovered, I started for home. lam scarcely strong yet," he added. By this time Lord Darkwood—or, as we will call him, Captain Tollish— had comprehended the truth in all its, ,to him awful bitterness. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>Lord Darkwood was alive. Lord Darkwood, whom he had believed dead, and whom he had succeeded in the title and estates, was standing here in his own library, under his own castle roof, and Captain Tollish was Captain Tollish again, with his debts and privations and old struggling life. The agony of that moment was like unto nothing he had ever endured.</p>
        <p>"At Marseilles," continued Lord Darkwood, "I read in the French newspapers of my supposed death, and of your accession to the title. The fancy took me to give you a surprise. I travelled home under the name of Mr. Charteris. I would not even stop in London to see Mr. Sutton. lest some ill wind should inform you of my return. On arriving at Shrewsbury, I muffled myself thoroughly, strolled into a taproom, and made inquiries concerning the new Lord Darkwood. I learned how you enjoyed your new honours, and I resolved to visit you unheralded. I walked all the distance from Shrewsbury, turned into the park, and approached the castle, without having been recognized. I saw you at the library window. I came up and looked in upon you. You know the rest. But was I not successful? Was not your surprise complete 2" Ay, it had been Indeed. Captain Tollish groaned In his anguish._ ' •" '</p>
        <p>"You -and I have been bitter enemies for seven- teen years, Captain Tollish," said the marquis. "If I had ever planned to be revenged upon you I could have so deeper revenge than I have now. You were poor, in debt, harassed, with expensive tastes, but without means to gratify them. You have been placed in a position of dignity, power, and luxury. You have flourished for some brief weeks as a peer of the realm, with a princely income, with servants and dependants, and everything you hold dear, Now you must go forth to your former life. The king has come into his own again." There was no triumph in Lord Darkwood's manner. He stated the fact simply and without exultation, but Captain Tollish received his words as a guilty man receives his sentence of death.</p>
        <p>These two—kinsmen as they were— had been bitter enemies for many long years, and Lord Dark- wood would have been more than human had he not in his heart been pleased at the terrible humiliation his return entailed upon Captain Toll- ish.</p>
        <p>"This is the first time we have spoken to each other in seventeen years," continued Lord Darkwood, a gloomy expression darkening his eyes. "You per- formed an act of treachery at that time which I can never forget nor forgive. So long as the memory of Clare Markham dwells in our hearts so long must you and I be foes." A lurid gleam appeared in Captain Tollish's hag- gard eyes—a gleam of hatred and defiance. "You are amply revenged, Lord Darkwood," he said. "This hour pays you for all you suffered then. I go from Dunholm Castle a beggar, but even you cannot rob me of the happy memories of the long ego. Clare Markham loved me • Flu-left all to foilaw me; she brightened my Melt 1, few 31orious months." Lord Darkwood turned noon Is.stb Tollish with the quickness of lightning. ' "Cease your boasts !" he cried, sternly. "She loved you, but you killed her! Murderer! Leave this house! I will summon the household and. have you cast out!" lie moved towards the bell.</p>
        <p>A murderous fury filled the soul of Captain His eyes blazed. His face glowed witb white heat. He seized a bronze taper-stand that stood at his elbow and bounded towards Lord Darkwood, pouncing upon him like a beast of pry. The marquis made a wild effort to self-defeuee, but he might as well have struggled with a rand- man.</p>
        <p>Captain Tollish hurled him to,the floor, flung him- self upon him, and struck hi a forehead with the bronze taper-stand a heavy aud resounding blow. Lord Darkwood, as he had said, had not tecovered his full strength since his illness, but he struggled desperately to fling off the weight of his enemy, his one thought and hope being to reazh the bell- pull. But Captain Tollish's ponderous frame weighed him down, fairly crushing him. Captain Tollish's steely eyes glared murderously into his own; and the heavy bronze taper-stand again crushed against his skull.</p>
        <p>He gave an odd gasp and closed his eyes, a stream of blood trickling down his face. lie grew suddenl7</p>
        <p>Inert;, and Captain Tollish arose slowly and loolad with savage exultation, and muttered— "He's dead now—dead as Julius Cmsar. There's no mistake about it this time. He's dead." He dropped the bronze figure, and stooped and felt Lord Darkwood's pulse and heart. How cold big lordship was growing. How ghostly was that blood- stained face. Captain Tollish sat down growing weak. Ile held up his hands and looked them over. There was no blood upon them. He stared at the rug.• A little stream of blood was trickling into it foul the victim's face. He turned the head over and sat down again. "He's dead!" he repeated; and the words Nye° sweetest music to his ears. "And, being dead, I'r3 his heir. I'm Lord Darkwood again, master of Duna- holm Castle, proprietor of rentroll, servants, ae tenantry. I had a big scare, but it's come out all right." He wiped his wet face with his handkerchief. lI features were distorted, looking like those of a 83' vanized corpse. He breathed hard, as if he had he°</p>
        <p>running. "It is fortunate that he told no one in Londo2 or in Shrewsbury, his name and identity," he musc "Fortunate for me, for I should have killed hire the same if he had had a troop of friends outsioe. I believe. Fate has befriended me as usual." Ile lookedlong and intently at the prostrate• egur,eo; the white, stained face, ic .air, dabbled tigi.l.of life came from his victim. A red gla."- firelight 101 l upon the ghastly features of Darkwood, and served to increase their look 0 marble-like rigidity. For some minutes Captain Tollish, or as syo again call him Lord Darkwood, sat immovable ital his chair, watching the object on the floor, ati listening for some adjoining rooms. "They are all asleep in the house," he "and yet I could swear that I heard some moving stealthily. Can any servant have Wl' witness to this night's work 7" The very possibility brought new terrors to 111°1'4. He arose and crept softly to the door. It svp'l,l locked. He turned the bolt very gently, oPell-;f the door with startling swiftness, and found Wins," face to face with Pietro, his valet. muttcrea;</p>
        <p>He reeled backward in his amazement. The sleek Maltese slipped into the librarY relocked the door. Thee, with a strange smile an." without any look of horror, he glided Fst master, and bent over the figure on the heartbrug. Murder was r-Jot the same thing to the revenfiefl crafty Pietro that it was to. his colder-bloc(.0 master. It seemei natural to him to rid himself oL_ an enemy by violence, and he thought the better ol Lord Darkwood for is wickedness, Confederates as they had long been in lesser crimes, the master would have preferred to kel this hideous secret to himself. He was angry the man fcr his surveillance, but he dared not sil°- it.</p>
        <p>"How long have you been at the door, pictro he asked uneasily. 6.1 "Ever since he camel" replied the Maltese. Was passing through the hall, when I heard ott strange sound ot someone in distress. It vex Yogi erying out that you saw a ghost. This was ve ghost Lord Darkwood himself. You btl murdered him !" The master shuddered. "That's an ugly word," said he, nervously- don't like it !." ".He's dead !" said the valet. "Yes, he's dead !"</p>
        <p>NI</p>
        <p>" What are you going to do with him? "I have not thought yet." tate "It's time to think. It's going on towards - in the morning," said Pietro. "In two or three hours more the dawn will come. What ardY°: going to give me to keep your secret and helP to get this thing"—and he pointed to the bodY "out of the way ?" "'I am in no mood for bargaining. Hele Teoety': Pietro, and I will do anything reasonable. if Yo,„:1 go back on me, you gain nothing, and lose a g place. If you help nte, you may be sure that Ye shall be rewarded."'</p>
        <p>" Iwill help you," said Pietro, briefly. He went to a little cabinet, of which he kne the contents, and brought forth a decanter brandy and a glass. He poured a large portion int his master and took as much for himself. d " "That will stiffen us. for the work M hen„:,t said the Maltese,. "And now to work. He ll' be buried." "Yes, and where no labourer will find his body said Lord Darkwood. "We must go through tlic ruins," said pietre• "There's a private passage leading from this cel. room into the ancient castle. Take up a Tg-yie One of our wax candles on, the shelf is wile' want. I'll carry the body. Now, come !" The marquis took, up the lighted candle, ~04 opened a door cleverly and thoroughly conceit:li% by a bookcase which swung outward when ne door was opened, and.advaneed into a dark Et° passage belonging to the ancient castle. Pietro gathered up the body of Lord Darkw°4l in his arms, and staggering under his burdete followed after his master. And in this 05°;ctnt little procession entered the- ruins of the sue— castle.</p>
