        <p>EARLESTOWN MAY QUEEN FESTIVAL</p>
        <p>1</p>
        <p>•</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>IMPOSING SPECTACLE.</head>
        <p>IMPOSING SPECTACLE.</p>
        <p>tilorious weather favoured the second annual May Queen festival, which has been promoted by Mr. Thonsas Molyneux, for the purpose of raising funds for the erection of public swimming baths, on Saturday. The whole of the week had been beau- tifully fine, but on Friday a change came over the atmosphere, and a cold wind blew from the North, which raised some misgivings in the minds of the organisers, and of those vviso intended taking part in the festival. Saturday morning however broke with a clear blue sky, and "4kl Sol" shone down pleas- antly, yet, as the day advanced, pitilessly upon the town. There was not 'even the accompaniment of a breeze, still although its cooling influence was not felt, its absence prevented the unpleasant whirling of dust. The inhabitants were early astir, and preparations for the successful accomplishment of the long talked of proces- sion, were speedifyin evidence. The interest displayed by everyone was sincere, and soon from the windows of private houses Might be seen flags and banners of all des- criptions floating in the air. Across Market-street, Bridge-street, and Earle-street, were stretched festoons of flags and bannerettes with various inscriptions, appli- cable to the occasion, whilst evergreens and seasonable towers added a pleasing relief. It was intended that the procession should not travel se Queen-street, and in con- sequence no decorations were to be seen, except at the shop of Mr. Attfield, who had a gay show of bannerettes and other things incidental to his business. Some dis- satisfaction was however expressed, and at the at moment 'Queen-street was included in the route. It was freely mooted that the display in the procession would be on a grand scale, and the result provcd that the rumours were not far afield. As previously stated, the Committee went to some expense in purchasing the cos- tumes, and other accessories which were used in connec- tion with the Lowton Rose Queen festival, and there can- not be two opinions as to the wisdom of the speculation. Last year being the inauguration of the affair, the pro- cession which was not on a very large scale, was formed at the Mission Hall, but when the Committee became aware of the dimensions which this year's festival would assume they were faced with the difficulty of securing a suitable place in which to marshal( the procession. A field near the Sugar Works belonging to Mr. Chambers was selected, and on that gentleman being approached he very kindly gave the committee permission to use it,and a more suitable rendezvous could not have been chosen. Two o'clock was the time appointed for mustering, but so anxious were the children, that many of them wended their way there long before that hour. Characters of all descriptions could be seen making for the Vitriol, and a stranger, unaware of what was in progress, might well stare with surprise to see clowns, wild cowboys, Indian chiefs, minstrels and pretty flower girls, all hurrying in one direction. The scene at the field was of a lively description, and kaleidoscopic in character, so many and varied were the characters and dresses. The assistance required in the shape of lorries and other conveyances was readily and cheerfully afforded by the tradesmen, and in this way they helped forward a project which is at once worthy and needful. By two o'clock, nearly all had assembled, and vast crowds had already taken up points of vantage along the route, and in order to obtain a good sight were willing to stand for some time exposed to the broiling heat sf the sun. Large numbers of people arrived from outside districts, and Warrington sent a large contingent in wagonnettes. The apprriaches to the field were kept by a staff of police in charge of Inspector Kelly, but the crowd considering its dimensions was most orderly. Some little delay took place in the marshalling, but when it was considered that nearly 1,500 children were present, who had to be arranged in something like con- formity with the programme, any delay was excusable. About 2.45 a start was made, Mr. Thomas Hamer offici- ating at the head as marshal, attired in the uniform of the Duke of Lancaster's • Yeomanry and mounted on a restless horse, which successfully prevented any obstruc- tion from the spectators. Following him came the fife and drum band of the Warrington Ragged School, head- ing a large contingent of prettily attired flower girls. Behind these was a miniature representation of an El ton gun on a gun carriage, drawn by lads attired as man-of- war's men, and sitting astride the gun was a very youth- ful representative of Her Majesty's navy. More tower girls followed, with bakers, red riding hoods, and colliers in quick succession, and a more motley and natural group of eolliers could not have been got together. At this stage a lorry belonging to Messrs. Healey and Hun- ter intervened, on the side of which was seated a giant, armed with an immense spiked club, his get-up eliciting much amusement. Dainty little hospital nurses followed with butchers in close proximity, and on their heels were lady cricketers, and joiners with two comical characters representing travelling jews. The painters were represented by a lorry on which Mr. Starkie exhibited the arts and implements of his trade, and this we might say was a really tasteful and complete exhibition. The Golborne brass band here led sp the second part of the procession, the first representation of which was" Little 80-peep" (Miss Waddington), "Boy Blue" (Master T. Morris), and " Little Miss Muffits " (Misses Davies). This was remarkably pretty, the illustration and the costumes being of a chaste descrip- tion ; Shepherd and Shepherdess (Master and Miss Jones), followed in a conveyance, with a sheep, which gazed wonderingly at the crowd. A company of soldiers came next and following were bricklayers and labourers, shoemakers, blacksmiths and strikers, barbers, milk- maids dressmakers and waitresses. The Gipsy Queen, (Miss milk- maids, in an improvised tent on wheels drawn by a donkey was followed by other gipsies, gaudily, yet prettily attired, and after a few cricketers came a conveyance containing Queen Rosebud (Miss Elemmons), who sat in state, a perfect bevy of flowers, attended by her maids of honour. In striking contrast was an evil looking Indian chief, mounted on horseback, and a slave driver (Mr. Spencer), whilst the never fail- ing Buffalo Bill (Master Hamblett), accompanied by Mexican Joe (Master W. Ellison), and attended by'Covr Eeys were in evidence. A small car, drawn by a goat, and containing two very young clowns was present, as also were hunters, hunting girls, old men and women, and a lorry containing greengrocery belonging to Mr. J. Riley. Fruit girls preceded a lorry on which Mr. ColEnge showed some of the implements connected with thensanufacture of marmalade. Musicians, Italian girls, SWir..B girls, American girls,and Nancy Lees were followed by Cinderella (Miss Wylde), and the Prince, while a spleuffid depiction of an Albanian (Master Barrow) kept themeompany. A lorry containing Spring, Summer, AI4I.IFLEI and Father Christmas, with Rip Van Winkle (Master,Davies) and boys representing games of lawn tennis, cricket, boating and football, was followed by another lorry on which sitting on a large globe was " Britannia" (Miss Barcroft), surrounded by England, Ireland, •Snotland and Wales, the British Army and Navy, with British possessions, India, Australia, Canada and Africa. This was a really magnificently got up representation, and elicited much favourable comment. After a number of clowns, minstrels, and mashers, came the Earlestawn brass band and following that was the May Pole dancers, who created a-pleasing impression, their attire ,being pretty and uniform. A company of smartly dressed Highland soldiers showed they had paid attention to the training they received from Sergeant McCoy, and their steady marching was a treat. A small policeman Al. (Master Green) preceded four daintily attired fairies, and then came the sword bearer (Master Green), sceptre-bearer (Master F. Butter), and a repre- sentation of an Archbishop (Mr. W. Crowther) carrying the crown. The carriage, drawn by two horses, contain- ing the Queen ,(Mies Bessie Davies) followed. She was attired in a dress of cream ponge silk, trimmed with gold lace, her train being composed of ruby velvet, trimmed with ermine, and on her head she wore a wreath of flowers and a veil. She was tire daughter of Mr. Joseph Davies, and very well she bore her queenly honours. With her were the pages, Masters Phillips and Parry, and the train bearers, Miss May Macklin and Miss M. Flough, while behind followed the maids of honour Misses Marsh, Grimshase, Penketh, Neild, Judge, Ellis, Jones and Banks. A carriage with the ex-Queen came next (Miss Luke) and she was attended by her train bearers, Masters C. Howgate and Conway, and bringing up the rear of the procession, which took about 20 minutes to pass any point, was a van containing future May Queens. Several tradesmen sent conveyances, which also took part in the cavalcade. The route followed was by way of Queen-st., Fairclough-et., Bridge-at., Market-Bt., Legh-st., Sankey-at., Regent-st., Viaduct-St., Wood-st., Earle-st., Market-st., to the Race Course, where the ceremony of crowning the queen took place. The large brick stand was thrown open and this was fairly well filled with spectators, the show being immedi- ately in front, in one of the enclosures. The throne was placed at one side in the centre and the usual ceremony was gone through, Mrs. Ormand completing the corona- tion, and presenting the Queen with nicely bound prayer and hymn books. A slight hitch occurred here, owing to the Rev. T. R. Matthews mounting the steps of the throne, and making a speech, before the sceptre had been presented to the Queen 4e)ut this, we believe, was due to some misunderstanding. Otherwise everything passed off well. The Maypole dancers under the charge of Mr. G. Shun performed their task exceedingly well and they deserve every praise. The dancers trained by Mr. J. Davies also acquitted them- selves to the satisfaction of everybody. Entertainments were given by the May Day Minstrels, and the ground was shortly afterwards given up to the general public for dancing. Everything passed off most successfully and every credit is due to the Committee for the excel- lent manner in which they carried out what must have been a most arduous and anxious task. The following is a list of the Committee of Manage- ment :—Chairman, Rey. T. R. Matthews ; Messrs. G. Butler, G. Payne, J. Barcroft, J. Davies, B. Lees, G. Shinn, N. Morris, H. Crowther, W. Lanwarne, J. Hatton, W.Banton, J.Shuffiebottom, J. Wickham, W.Kenning, J. Clarke, J. Burrows, T. Milnes, E. Bedworth, J. Baston, W. Leatherbarrow, S. Openshaw, J. Gee, W. Evans, J. Lowe, R. Watkinson, J. Tu rton, J. Nuttall, W. Maims ; Mrs. J. Waddington, Mrs. W. Kenning, Mrs. J. Davies, Mrs. PI. Morris ; Mr. T. Molyneux, hon-treasurer ; Mr. J. Waddington, hon. secretary. Masters of Ceremonies : Messrs. G. Shinn, J. Davies, J. Shuffiebottom, amid J. Wickham.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>111 E EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 20, 1893</head>
        <p>111 E EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 20, 1893</p>
        <p>child carried a bunch of flowers, was formed in the chapel grounds, and then marched into the chapel and down the first aisle, singing the hymn "All things bright and beautiful." Near the pulpit a large flower stand had been erected, and as each child passed on to the platform the flawers were taken by Miss little Bennett and Miss Edith Richardson, and placed on the stand. The children sang special hymns during the service,and the Rev. John Gunnell delivered an address especially relating to flowers. Referring to the study of botany,he said it was a most beautiful study, and if it were not for the barbarous Latin names given to some of the most common English flowers many more would take up the study. Special hymns were sung by the choir and chil- dren during the day,and great praise is due to Mr. James Bennett, of the Bewsey-road (Warrington) Wesleyan Chapel choir, for his untiring energies training both the choir and the children for the occasion. Miss Bennett, of Lyndhurst, presided at the organ. Collections were taken during the day in aid of the funds of the Sunday school and amounted to £24 14s. 20.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>AT OUR LOCAL CaURCHES,</head>
        <p>AT OUR LOCAL CaURCHES,</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>CAIO.ST. SUNDAY SCHOOL ANNIVERSARY.</p>
        <p>lOn Sunday, the Sunday school anniversary sermons wem preached by the Rev. W. H. Drummond, B.A. In 4 the afternoon a children's service was held when special hymns and anthems were sung, There were good con- gregations at each service. Collections were made in aid of the Sunday school. The Rev, gentleman took for his text in the morning " Of such is the kingdom of heaven "Matthew xix 14. He said looking through the eyes of Christ, the world had seen the spiritual beauty of childhood. His words about children few as they were, contained a whole gospel in them- selves. Those of them who had visited some of the great continental galleries must have felt how strongly the redemption side of Christianity appealed to the imagination of past generations. They had there the " Mater Dolorosa," the thorn-crowned Man of Sorrows, with a monotony which would become oppressive, did it not speak of great human realities, and were it not broken at times by the figure of some bright-eyed child, radient with gladness, purity and sweet human love. This emphasis which might in some aspects seem un- natural, sprang no doubt in part from the teaching of the church, without a deep reflection upon its meaning. It was also in part, the fruit of the political conditions of the time, with widespread sense of insecurity, which gave undue prominence to the distressful featut es of life. But it pointed ultimately to something far more significant and radical than either of these causes. It was the expres- sion in art of the redemptive message of Christianity to the surging passions, the enslaving habits, the voice- less sorrows of human life. But this was not all we needed. There were chords of feeling which it did not touch. Beside the figure of the Man of Sorrows we would put the picture of Christ and the children, for it was not less divine_ Perhaps they might think its mean- ing was less hard to learn. Like flowers and sunshine and the song of birds it came to them unsought for, and won their admiration by the simple charm of its beauty.. But, like the flower, too in this, it brought them to the threshold of one of the great mysteries of God, and it was only the awakened spirit that could draw from it the message that lay hidden in its heart. Was Jesus thinking of his own childhood when he spoke these words? Perhaps it was only fancy which found in them still some of the sweetness of His mother's love, some of the influence of home, with its quiet joys and faithful prayers, some of the freshness of nature, amid which His boyhood's days were passed. That all these things had entered into His soul, and there become part of His faith in the divine fatherhood they knew from many of the delicate hints and touches which shine through His recorded words. If ever It was true that " The child is father to the man "it must have been true of him. Such simplicity of religious utterance is only given to those who ha-m entered into the heart of tne things of which they speak. Childhood was the period of trust. Here Jesus found the best symbol of man, the child, to God his father. Trust is a perman- ent factor in life, the whole of society is built up upon it. Two practical suggestions flowed from what he had said that morning. The first concerned the religion of childhood, and our duty in regard to it. There was no more serious and responsible question which parents could ask themselves than "of what kind is the religion of my child to be ?" It involved more important issues than any other for the answer which was given to it would largely determine the temper and spirit which animated the whole of life. The second thought was one which he would leave with them as at once the primary lesson of the words of Christ and a primary quality in all helpful personal religion. Christ spoke a blessing over the eager wondering heart of the child, and then he said in words which Christendom has often either strangely perverted or forgotten " Except ye be turned and become as little children, ye cannot enter into the kingdom of heaven." It was the dream of the genera- tions, their dream to-day, the kingdom of heaven. They prayed for it, and it was in their midst all the time dwelling in the pure and childlike heart.</p>
        <p>WYCLIFFE COCKHEDGE MISSION. The chapel anniversary services in connection with the above-named Mission were held on Sunday. The Rev. T. Ross, Wesleyan minister, preached in the afternoon. The attendance was good, and the remarks of the rev. gentleman were much appreciated. In the evening Miss Green, of the Railway Men's Mission, conducted the ser• vice, and preached from the text " What think ye of Christ ?" and was listened to with close attention. The choir, under the leadership of Mr. R. Travis, sang the anthems " Behold how good and joyful," and " Arise, shine," accompanied by a portion of the Wycliffe Or- chestral Band. Miss Kent sang as a solo "Glory to Thee my God this night." Collectioas were taken on behalf of the mission fund.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>P.S.A. AT WYCLIFFE.</head>
        <p>THE P.S.A. AT WYCLIFFE.</p>
        <p>Despite extra outdoor attractions on Sunday after- noon there was a good attendance at the Pleasant Sun- day afternoon meeting held in Wycliffe Church. Mr. Thomas Morris, F.G.S., preAded, and in a short, practi- cal address gave some good advice to those present, basing his remarks on a scripture text which, by the way, he announced at the close, "A soft answer turneth away wrath." Mr. C. E. Parker was the speaker for the afternoon. He took for his subject "Christ multiplying the five loaves and two fishes." Mr. Parker very gra- phically described the scene of the miracle, also the manners, customs, and the occupations of the people in the time of Christ, pointing out as he proceeded, the various lessons to be learned from the incident. The address was a very interesting and profitable one. Miss Burnett, of Salford, sang as a solo "There is a green hill faraway" in an excellent manner. The Rev. J. Youge announced that the membership was still increasing, 19 having that day joined the society. Mr. A. Birch also took part in the service. ROMAN CATHOLIC CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>At both St. Mary's Roman Catholic Church, and St. Alban's Roman Catholic Church, on Sunday evening, the annual procession in honour of the Blessed Virgin Mary took place, there being a crowded congregation at each place. The processions were, as usual, of a most impos- ing nature. First came a youth bearing a massive cross followed by two other youths, carrying lighted candles. Then followed a number of girls and young women , dressed in spotless white, with white veils and blue 1 sashes, and carrying bunches of choice flowers and ban- ners. Four of these young women bore on their should- ers a statue of the Blessed Virgin, the base of the figure being surrounded by a beautiful wreath of various coloured flowers. Special music was rendered by the choirs. The magnificent ecclesiastical sculpture at St. Mary's has been further beautified by the addition of another small group of carved stone recesses in the interior of the Church. The ,figures represented are Christ speaking to the women of Jerusalem. It is the work of Messrs. Boulton and Sons, art sculptors,Ciselten- ham, and is a beautiful specimen of figure sculpture. There are still six recesses to be filled, at a cost of ten guineas each, eight now being completed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>SERMONS AT PADGATE.</head>
        <p>SERMONS AT PADGATE.</p>
        <p>The annual sermons in connection with the Padgate Wesleyan Sunday School were held on Sunday,the pulpit being occupied morning and evening by the Rev. F. B. Lockyer,B.A.,of Manchester. In the afternoon a special flower service for children was held the Rev. John Gun- nell, of Penketh, presiding. A procession,in which each</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>RAILWAY FACILITIES AT ST. HELENS. THE POSITION OF THE COMPANY'S SERVANTS.</head>
        <p>RAILWAY FACILITIES AT ST. HELENS. THE POSITION OF THE COMPANY'S SERVANTS.</p>
        <p>-- The London and North-Western Company in their arrangements for a better and more punctual train service from St. Helens, to other large towns have made a thorough revision of the hours of work and the duties of their servants throughout that district. There are 38 signal boxes in the St. Helens district, the most important being Raven-street, where the Warrington, Widnes and Manchester lines join the main from Liverpool, south of St. Helens Station ; that at St. Helens Junction on the main line from Liver- pool to Manchester ; Sutton Oak, an important mineral and passenger centre ; Pocket Nook, where numerous works sidings join the main trunk ; and at Carr Mill, where to meet requirements the lines have been doubled. The hours in these boxes are eight per turn,the 24 hours being divided into three shifts. At Ravenhead, Sharp- at., Clock Face and Garswood, the hours are ten, and at 2$ other boxes the hours are 12. The wages vary from 20e. to 303. per week. There are eight foremen shunters and about 20 other shunters in this district. They were all put on the ten hours' system in October' 1892, but in March, 1893, with the exception of the foremen and three others, they were all again put back to the twelve hours. The wages are from 233. to 303., and foremen 325. 6d. ; time and a half for Sundays in each case Goods guards and brakesmen are paid by the trip system ; also a majority of the engine men. They are rated at ten hours a day ; all over ten hours paid for ; limit 12 hours ; time and a half for Sundays. Passenger guards are rated at ten hours a day, or 60 hours per week ; wages 253. to 303. Passenger porters are rated at 12 hours a day, with one and a half hours for 1 meals ; wages 17s. to 203. per week ; no Sunday pa y-1 merit, but work every alternate Sunday. In the work- ing of goods and minerals the trains have recently been retimed, so that all men engaged will complete their trips in ten hours unless very seriously delayed. No man is allowed to work more than 12 hours, no matter when or where he may be ; nor is he allowed to resume' duty again until he has been off duty eight hours. This, we understand, has caused more trains to be put on to do the work formerly done by the other trains working longer hours.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>HEARTLESS CRUELTY TO A DOG AT</head>
        <p>HEARTLESS CRUELTY TO A DOG AT</p>
        <p>HAYDOCK. A COLLIER BREAKS ITS LEGS.</p>
        <p>At the Newton Petty Sessions on Saturday, Mr. T. J. Gillespie in the chair, a collier named Wm. Travis, of Haydock, was charged with cruelly ill- treating a dog on the 2nd of May. The animal be- longed to another collier named Thomas Topping. Defendant pleaded not guilty. The prosecution was conducted by Inspector Dowty, of the R.S.P.C.A. It was stated by Thomas Topping that while he was in a I room at the Ram's Head public-house, on the 2nd May, , with his dog, defendant came in and entered into con- versation with him about the animal. It was a racing "Snip dog" he said, and when asked what kind of a dog I that was, he told the Bench that it was a 200 yards I runner, whereupon Inspector Dowty came to the rescue and said the animal was a racing greyhound. At any rate, the defendant very shortly said that the dog belonged to him, and he was not going to part with it alive. He then took the dog up and held it by its two fore legs. He was asked by Topping to free the deg and leave it alone, but upon saying that he would do as he pleased with it, he received a blow from Topping which felled him. When on the floor, still holding the dog, he ex- claimed "Now we'll see," and he 'then deliberately pulled the dog's fore legs apart until they were wrenched from their" sockets," and the joint bones protruded I from the skin. The dog was suffering BD much pain that I Topping drowned it shortly afterwards. These facts were corroborated by Thomas Corn, after which P.C. Green stated that when he heard of the affair, he made inquiries and found that a William Winstantley had drowned the dog in a pit at Haydock. He tried to re- cover the carcase to show it to Inspector Dowty, but found that someone, he suspected the defendant, had been there before him, for the carcase had been re- moved. An erratic speech in his own defence was next made by the defendant. He said the dog had been stolen from him about a month ago, and he considered that if the complainant would not let him take his own dog away,tie was justified in doing what he pleased with it. He should do it again if it was his own dog. He denied that the dog's legs were broken. The Bench said they considered the case amply proved ; it was one of the cruelest they had heard, and they fined the defend- ant 20s. and costs.</p>
        <p>WA RRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians was held at the Town Hall, on Wednesday, when there were present—Mr. G. Ratcliffe (in the chair) Rev. G. F. Roberts, and Messrs. T. Smith, A. Jolley, G. Pilling, J. Clarke, J. Dignum, J. Houghton, T, Mather, W. Houghton, E. Aukland, S. Davies, Wm. Gill, R. Heaton, J. Hill, A. J. Jolley, 11. J. Longton, J. Muir, and W. J. Shaw. GAS METER RENTS. Mr. Straw drew attention to an item in the accounts, submitted for approval, respecting the gas bill. He said he noticed that at Scotland-road where the bill was 6s. 11d., they had to pay is. 6d. rent for gas meter, while at Bewsey Chambers where the bill came to £1 is. 9d., they only had to pay ninepence. He thought that something must be wrong there, and asked for an explanation.—Mr. PILLING said that the extra charge at Scotland-road evidently occurred because the meter was larger.—Mr. Strew said he did not think so ; but after some discussion Mr. Pilling's explanation was accepted. THE CHARGE AGAINST A PORTER.—A WARD FOR DRUNKEN</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>MORE BANK FAILURES IN AUSTRALIA.</head>
        <p>MORE BANK FAILURES IN AUSTRALIA.</p>
        <p>-- Very serious news was received on Monday from New South Wales and Queensland. At Sydney the Commer- cial Bank closed its doors. The liabilities are very heavy. The announcement of this disaster had been preceded by news of the suspension of the Queensland National Bank and the Bank of North Queensland. The two failures of the Queensland banks did not produce much effect in Loudon, but when it was found that the Commercial Bank of Sydney had suspended—" pending reconstruction," of course,—the Stock Exchange fell into a panicky condition. The Government of New South Wales has prcclaimed the bank's notes a legal tender within the colony. The Queensland National Bank has received large sums of British money. There, as in New South Wales, the Government stepped in to assist the bank by giving a conditional guarantee to the notes in circulation. " Pending reconstruction," the City of Melbourne Bank, Limited, closed its doors on Tuesday. It holds about two millions of Scotch money. From Sydney we learn that the " reconstruction " scheme of the Commer- cial Bank proposes that the payment of the deposits shall be spread over a period cf eight years. The Govern- ment of New South Wales announces that it is satisfied that the position of the Bank of New South Wales is sound, and that it will assist that institution to the ful- lest extent.</p>
