      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>MEN.</head>
        <p>MEN.</p>
        <p>The minutes of the House Committee which were read contained a minute of a meeting of the Committee at which the charge of illtreatment against a porter was investigated. When all the evidence had been gone through the Committee exonerated the porter from all blame.—Mr. Devms, in proposing the adoption of the minutes, said that the Committee were unanimously of the opinion that there was no ground for complaint against the porter or the men who assisted him. On the contrary they were of opinion that the man said to have been injured ought to feel very thankful indeed that he I had got off with such mild treatment. Some people thought the porter or some other official should be at the gate all night to receive any rough character who might present himself but he wished it to be clearly understood that while the Committee were most anxious that tramps should receive proper treatment, the Work- house authorities should not hold themselves responsible, or rezcive any blame. Their poiter only acted as a kind of policeman and what policeman in the Warrington force would stand idly by whilst his head was being battered in ? He wished the outside public to know that a much larger number of tramps passed through their wards than was supposed. No fewer than 50 passed through the previous night and had to have a bath and their clothes disinfected. He thought it would be a good thing if they could get tramps treated in a different ward, and not have them coming in the middle of the night and disturbing the infirm and sick inmates. (Hear, hear.)—Mr. SHAW seconded the adoption of the minutes, and Mr. Wm.Houghton in sup- porting them, drew attention to the fact that he had advocated that tramps should receivequalifiiiations from the police before they came to the workhouse, and then they would not be troubled with any kind of drunken characters.—Mr. DAVIES said that the man in question was suffering from delirium tremens, and ought to have been detained by the police, and not sent to the Work- house.—Mr. HraToN said something ought to be done to prevent the place becoming a receptacle for drunken men.—The CLERK stated that their union was troubled much more than most others by tramps, and he put before them for consideration the fact that numbers of Unions kept a separate and distinct place with special officers for the treatment of tramps.—The miuutes were adopted.</p>
        <p>SCHOOL ATTENDANCE OFFICERS. The CLERK read a letter from the Local Government Board approving of the appointment of Mr. B. Shearer and Mr. F. Wright as school attendance officers.</p>
        <p>THE PRESS AND THE BOARD..</p>
        <p>Mr. DAVIES at the conclusion of the business said that at the last meeting of the Board, during a discussion res- pecting the allegations against one of their porters, the reporters got up in a body and left the room. He could not understand such a proceeding taking place, but he was informed that they had been privately advised that the proceedings were private, and they were asked to retire. He had yet to learn that those proceedings were private. Their Board was a public body elected by the ratepayers, and the ratepayers through the medium of the press had a right to know what was going on. One of the news- papers had a full report of the proceedings, and the others had to go about and pick up a few details. He would like to know upon whose authority the press had been asked to retire.—The CLERK said that it did not require any order to be issued. It was a standing order of their Board which had existed since he had been con- nected with it, that all inquiries respecting their own officers were to be treated, Ira the first instance, as private. It would be very unjust if they did not do 89 as statements might be made which might give pain to an innocent official without having a chance to defend himself. Of course if they wished they could alter the custom, but he hoped they would not do so. Of course as to the circumstances of a member of the Board re- porting the matter himself he had no control over it, and he did not think that the Board could control it.— Mr. W. HOUGHTON : It is the same as the Board going into Committee.—The CLERK: Just so.</p>
        <p>In reporting the minutes of the Padgate Schools Com- mittee which were submitted to the previous meeting of the Board of Guardians it was stated that a brick- setter had been engaged at Elid per hour. We are informed that it was a bricksatter's labourer who was engaged at that rate per hour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>SHOCKING FATALITY NEAR ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>SHOCKING FATALITY NEAR ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>Henry Partington, of Bickerstaffe-st., St. Helens a guard in the employ of the London and North- Western Railway Company, met with a fearful death on Thursday night last week. He was work- ing the train which left Lime-st. station, Liverpool for St. Helens, at half-past nine. On the arrival of the train at St. Helens at three minutes past ten, it was found, after the passengers had cleared the platform, that the door of the van was shut, and the guard was missing. A telephonic message was immediately sent to the station master at Thatto Heath Station, who replied that Partington had got into his van all right. The sta- tion master then walked down the line, and when he reached the bridge opposite Greengate brickworks, he found Partington lying face downwards in the ditch, a few yards on the St. Helens side of the bridge, quite dead. His cap was lying underneath the bridge. His skull was smashed in, and death must have been instan- taneous. It is evident that he had been looking out, but had not noticed his near approach to the bridge,with the result that he was caught by the buttress of the bridge and dragged out of the van. The body was then conveyed to St. Helens, and thence to his late home. He leaves a widow and one son to mourn his loss. Part- ington, who was about thirty-five years of age, has been in the employ of the company since his youth, and from I the driver of a parcel office van he became a clerk in the I office, and by his integrity and attention rose to the position of guard. Be was well known and highly respected.</p>
        <p>CADBURY'S COCOA is absolutely pure.—The Publics Ana 'vats have submitted CADBURY'S COCOA LO close tests and the result has always proved its great value as a sustain trig totd nourishing beverage.--Advt.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON HIGHWAY BOARD.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of this Board was held in the office of the Board,Masonic Buildings,on Wednes‘' ay, when Captain T. B. Withington presided. There were also present, Messrs. W. Gough, J. Wright, J. Clare, 11. Bate, J. Stanier, P. I. Bate,lll. Ormond, J. Millington, W. Royston, R. T. Lowe, It. Owen, J. Houghton, and J. Grounds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>4...'''';' 7 ' ,- k `..*, *5-;•: '.2ii.'; 4ck 0 r-1, it , ...</head>
        <p>4...'''';' 7 ' ,- k `..*, *5-;•: '.2ii.'; 4ck 0 r-1, it , ...</p>
        <p>ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>Captain T. E. Withington was re-elected chairman, Mr. W. Royston was re-elected senior vice-chairman, and Mr. James Houghton jun. vice-chairman, for the year. The Finance Committee was re-appointed, the only alteration being the appointment of Mr. Joeeph Clare in the place of Mr. Thomas Blackburn. THE ATTENDANCE OF MEMBERS. The following is a list of the attendances of members at the seven meetings of the Board held during the year :—W. Gough, three ; J. Wright, two ; T. Black- burn, one; H. Bate, four; J. Stanier, none ; P.l.' Bate, five; S. Bent, one; J. Millington, seven ; W. Royston, three; R. T. Lowe, four; Reg. Owen jun., four; J. Kerfoot, six; J. Houghton, seven; J. Grounds, five, and T. E. Wallington, five.</p>
        <p>I . . IMPROVEMENTS AT PENKETH. The SURVEYOR recommended that the road opposite Penketh Lodge be widened if the land could be obtained from the Penketh Tanning Company, and if Hot, that 1 Chapel-lane bridge be widened. Mr. Millington thought it was time the work was done, and it had been discus- sed for two or three years, and nothing had been accom- plished yet. He also pointed out that a public water- ing place for cattle had been stopped near Halton bridge by the Board placing there a large mound of rubbish. The Surveyor was instructed to have the rubbish re- moved.</p>
        <p>MR. OWEN 31 ARES A COMPLAINT. Mr. R. OWEN, Croft, said he had been approached by several leading ratepayers in his township, who said that something was being done by the Highway Board in the township which ought not to be done by them. They referred to some improvements that had been made to the back approach to Mr. Munse's premises. The ratepayers complained at the township meeting about this matter, and it had been said that he supported its being done. The surveyor had never brought the matter before the Board and he thought that each waywarden of a township should know what was being doing by the Board in the township he represented. The surveyor said a com- plaint was made to him about the level crossing. It was most dangerous. It was always customary to mend these places when complaints were made about them. He had the deep gutter dammed up and the crossing properly made. The cost would be about £5 altogether, which would be about ss. for the township of Croft.—Mr. GWEN said it was not the cost he complained about but the principle. He felt he had been sat upon. After further discussion Mr. OWEN moved a resolutisn that every waywarden be informed on every matter re- lating to his township. Mr. Millington seconded and the tnotion was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>MISCELLANEOUS.</head>
        <p>MISCELLANEOUS.</p>
        <p>The CLERK said he had received a communication from the Warrington Rural Sanitary Authority, asking the Board to adopt tha Private Street Works Act of 1892. It affected the Board in private streets in the Warrington extra municipal district. The Clerk was instructed to write to the clerk of the Rural Sanitary Authority ask. ing him to give fuller particulars of what they intended to pave the private streets with, before the Board gave their consent. It was decided that the rate should be Bd. to t £ which would realise £4.091 3s. 4d., which with a balance of £135 9s. 53. from last year would al- most meet the expenditure which was £4,228 14s.—The Town Clerk of Warrington wrote respecting the tenancy of the Board's room, stating that he believed the Corpor- ation would be willing to continue the tenancy of the Highway Board. This concluded the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>SUICIDE OF A LIVERPOOL MAN</head>
        <p>SUICIDE OF A LIVERPOOL MAN</p>
        <p>AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>A respectably-dressed young man, supposed to be William Lloyd, of 134, Tunnel-road, Edge-hill, Liver- pool, was found dead in a retiring room at St. Helens Station on Monday. The man was lying on his side on the floor. The body was warm but dead. It was car- ried to the waiting-room, and Dr. Jackson, the com- pany's local surgeon, sent for. The doctor stated that deceased had died from poisoning. On inquiry at 134, Tunnel-road, Edge-hill, it was found that the deceased's name was William Lloyd. aged twenty-one, an appren- tice at Messrs. Rockliffe Bros., stationers, Castle-st., Liverpool. Mr. and Mrs. Power reside at the above address, and deceased had lived with and been brought up by them from a child. He is described as having been a quiet youth, and nothing can be stated as to the reason for the condition in which he was found. He was at business on Monday morning, but left about eleven o'clock, and his friends heard nothing of him until about three o'clock on Tuesday morning when a railway official came to make inquiries. He has a mother living at Formby.</p>
        <p>At St. Helens Town Hall,on Wednesday, Mr.Brighouse held an inquest on the body of William Lloyd.—Dr.Robt. Jackson, who was called to the station' said he found that Lloyd was dead. He examined the body, and came to the conclusion that in all probability he had died from carbolic acid poisoning.—Police-constable John Hall, who took chalge of the body, produced a number of papers which were in the clothing.—The Coroner examined them' and said there was a paper called the " Racing World," and attached to it was a reckoner to show the prices; a list of horses cut from a newspaper, and a list which he seemed to have made in his own handwriting of horses and their ages ; then there wan a list cf "horses worth following," and the names of their trainers. There was also a pocket book with sums of money entered to horses, and a paper which seemed to be a tip from someone who knew all the winners. It looked yew much as if he was given to betting.—Mr. James Power, of 134, Tunnel-road, Edgehill, said the deceased had lived with him for about ten years. He (witness) was a compositor at Rockcliffe Brothers. The deceased was at work up to dinner time on Monday, but he did not return in the afternoon. The deceased was a quiet young fellow. Witness did not know he was given to betting. He did not know that he was in monetary difficulties. The deceased never mentioned anything about betting to him.—The Coroner having summed up, the jury returned a verdict that Lloyd committed sui- cide whilst in an unsound state of mind.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
