        <p>DARING BURGLA.RY AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>A THIEF GETS A NEW RIG OUT.</p>
        <p>The lock-up 'premises of Mr. George Jackson, pawnbroker, Winwick-st., were broken into on Tuesday night, and property to the value of about £2O was stolen. The thief or thieves affected an en- trance first to the premises of Mr. Kenyon. oil and varnish manufacturer, whose premises adjoin Mr. Jackson's,and climbed between the joists and then burst a hole in the ceiling of Mr. Jackson's. The till in the front shop connter was opened, but luckily for the pro- prietor, only eightpence had been left in, and this of course was taken. The jewellery in the window was left untouched, but some jewellery that had been left on the top of the safe was taken, and also some silk handker- chiefs. The thief also helped himself to a suit of clothes leaving his old ones behind. The full extent of the robberies is not at present known, but the proprietor considers abdut £2O worth of goods have been taken. Several tools which are burglary appliances were left on the premises. The police ore taking active steps to cap- ture the delinquents. The same shop was broken Into about three or four years ago, and the burglar who was then caught, is still in gaol, le being sentenced to five years' penal servitude.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>FIRE AT THE WARRINGTON FEVER</head>
        <p>FIRE AT THE WARRINGTON FEVER</p>
        <p>HOSPITAL.</p>
        <p>TIE NEW STRUCTURE BURNT DOWN.</p>
        <p>A fire broke out on Thursday noon,in the new fever hospital, near Aikin-st., which was originally in- tended for the smallpox patients. The place has been used for serious cases of typhoid fever, but for the past few months, it has not been in requisition, although it contained twenty beds. The tire was first noticed by someone in the houses near the fever hospital, who at once informed the matron (Miss Walker), and Mr. Bloomfield, caretaker. Mr. Bloom- field rush:xi to the building which is some distance from the fever wards, but on opening the door, the flames burst into his face, and one el the gas meters also burst. He turned the gas eff at, the main meter and prevented the other meter bursting. He then telephoned for the fire engine. The fire brigade under the command of Superintendent Tureer arrived about 12.15, and the hose was at once brought to bear upon the burning building, the Workhouse, and the houses round about. The fire in the building was, hc ;corer, beyond control, as the interior was almost entirely made of wood, and in five minutes after the arrival of the fire engine, the roof of the building fell with a crash. About this i time a severe thunderstorm passed over, and the rain fell n torrents, materially helping to extinguish the fire.. The building was completely gutted, there being scarcely anything left with the exception of the corrugated iron in which it was encased. Among those present who rendered some assistance were Drs. Peacock, Fox, Adams, and Cannel!, Alderman Sutton, Councillors Bonnell, Shaw,Smethurst, and other Corporation officials. The news of the tirespread rapidly through the town,and a large crowd of people quickly filled Aikin-st., and a number of the relatives and friends of the inmates, ow- ing to a false report that the rest of the wards were on. fire, clamoured at the gates for admittance. They were not allowed to come inside the gates, but, their fears were allayed when they saw the true state of affairs. The damage is estimated at about £7OO. It is supposed that the cause of the fire, was a defective flue in the heating apparatus. An ample supply of water was obtained from three hydrants, one on the premises, one in the Workhouse yard, and another near the gates in Aikin-street. We understand that the building was not insured.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>OPENING OF THE NEW PARK,'</head>
        <p>OPENING OF THE NEW PARK,'</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>On Thursday aternoon the new Eccleston-park was formally presented to the borough of St. Helens by Mr. Samuel Taylor, J.P., after which the Mayor (Mr. A. Sinclair) declared the magnificent new breathing space open to the public. There was a large number present in the park to survey the tract of land, Thursday afternoon being the recog- nised half-holiday in the tow.i. The members of the Corporation drove to the Tollbar in carriages, and marched in procession along the Rhododendron-drive to the spot reserved in the park for the meeting, where the formal presentation of the estate took place, and where Mr. Taylor was presentO with an address thanking him for the handsome gift. The estate is situated on the western side of the borough, and is en an eminence com- manding a wide view of the country, extending to the Rivington Hills on the one side and Ortnsktrk on the other. The total area of the park is 48 acres 2 roods and 1 pole. The land area embraces 31 acres and 1 rood, the remaining 17 acres being made up of tire big dam, 12 acres 2 roods and 2 poles ; the little darn, 1 acre 2 roods ; and the waterworks resery oir,3g acresacresd 4 a andand 39 poles. The Victoria-park consists of of whiche s, and a about Thatto seventeen ll Heath-park, tahe -rpe as rokn, he Eeele purposes, o n Corporation are going to spend rk 3 000 in laying ou only acresre useda n fdo polo, pTa making it suite P P therestesta t e beingand marekserved for suitable d laanrdk t tuhr. regarded as a moss acceptable ipnof:ecat, e d aTbai yt al no rts' s y- acceptable one to the smoke-begrred and chemicall ofnothbelehgoirfotuisg.i.</p>
