        <p>1.7 is not so long ago that Mr. lian'e Rule CHAMBERLIAN declared that Home, Since that et Rule was as dead as Queen Anne.' atement was made few men have done more than Mr. CHAMBERLAIN himself to lehtn. onstrate how ill-founded was the statement. It, "dead *, question has almost completely onopclised his oratory, and of his speeches there ilaaor end. it might have been thought that his polit!)f success in anticipating even the immediate taideavlisfaubtiulirtey7Euulsd in hgave impressed upon him in extreme moderation Prophetic talent he happens to be endowed But modesty is not one of Mr. Cliental:it- ;ll-iN s most pronounced qualities, and it is not at I,2arPrising to find him again predicting the sZtlre with his customary assurance. In an_ rtteSs which he gave to the Grand Committee of nixtioghara Liberal Unionist Association on eonesday he practically settled all that is to Zu4le cloPlace at the next General Election. That IN‘maiby the way, is,according to Mr. CHAMBE- Li ,O t electi akR e place in a "few months." For the it is hhe says, every Unionist is longing, yet Si th'ard to trace any desire to hasten the appeal ' %ea: acticms of the Unionists in the House of to the'44lB for they are there forging amendments n measure which we were told was as "dead rao'lleen Anne " that will take more than a few la4lthe to discuss, judging by the progress of the the i.vreek or two. When the election does come by aZiherals are to be decimated,if not annihilated, hs.s b Perated electorate. Mr. CHAMBERLAIN 46/1 at the tie o,erl furnished with strictly private informa- people are at last awakening to the qtAcinitntY of the proposals made by Mr. Pir -1181°1`Ts for the disintegration of the Ern- , C`he Scotch Liberals, in particular, are gillbil'ng to "awaken." Mr. CHAMBERLAIN, It aili• ddedy does not live in Scotland but in of :au . -gal One of the hypothetical incidents e/41,18 hnothetical General Election in which ir. -;',4aLAIN seems to find particular delightm s ke.;eJection of Mr. GLADSTONE by the electors of He assures us that the PRIME has not the slightest chance of being esti-tried again for that constituency. But,then,we llsver forget that Mr.CHAMBERLAIN just as con- ,Que4tlY assur ed us that Home Rule was "as dead as 4111ae," and yet a Home Rule Bill has passed 8-Coml reading in the House of Commons, and Pro Visions are even now the theme of every vo,witican from Mr. CHAMBERLAIN downwards-Tor a.-cirdB. From the ill-natured prognostications eolle ..4aandie!scl partizanship of Mr. CHAMBERLAIN, .10 bnras with pleasure to a picture of Mr. GLAD- himself I'l4 conducting i the debate n t eHouse Ny. e tarn° t:ed nrs nonh ity that into which he has and which, in spite of a 41°0t unblushing obstruction, is surely adv. anc- n',„ to the final scene. "The most striking NriPt73loll of the sitting was," writes the graphic a Parliamentary correspondent, "the pre- ‘• Ccinance of Mr. GLADSTONE. From ...TSt to last Was the life and soul of the debate. Not only ol.e, Most able man in the House, but the most ea,rer, and attentive. Time after time he e- as on his feet, each time throwing off a little bteell• Alone and single-handed he carries his forward, alternately fighting, persuading, ainatiog'• Ilis colleagues, supporters, or Irish 114•Iies now an again come to his aid but seldom oelP the matter fotward. Looking on the scores 4'Qulber:i around, and then turning to the</p>
