        <p>straight, lithe figure, so vigorous in gesture, so full of youth, bubbling over with humour, the brilliant intellect simply skipping round and round the comparatively slow moving wits of some of the cleverest hcads in England, how incredible does it seem that the feebleness of this frame, the obscuring of this intellect, have been confidently calculated upon as a factor in the situation."</p>
        <p>THIRTY-TWO thousand pounds Profitable Piano-Playing. for playing the piano I That is the pretty considerable fortuie I, which M. PADEREWSKI, the Prince of Pianists, has " netted" by his tour in America. He gave fifty- nine concerts, and, therefore, if the figures quoted be correct, he must have cleared over five hundred pounds at each one of them. But then, are the figures correct? American newspapers take such a delight in playful exaggeration that one can neyor be absolutely sure about the information they impart to us, especially when the " al mighty dollar" is the theme. Even the New Yolk " Nation" has begun to remonstrate against the unveracity of its contemporaries. American news- papers are very enterprising in their methods of gathering news, and, we are told, that their columns are filled with occurrences and stories of every description, true or false, which are likely to "sell the paper." Bogus interviews are said to be "now so common as hardly to excite remark even from the victims, and burlesque drollery in the guise of descriptions of actual occurrences is so much in fashion that it is with great difficulty a New Yorker to-day can get from a morn- ing paper a reliable account of any impor- tant event of the day in his own city." M. PADEREWISKI has himself had some expe- rience of the ingenious freedom of comment in which the American pressman indulges. The popular pianist's visit to the World's Fair at Chicago was the occasion of a lively newspaper controversy. Both the official director of music at the Fair and PADEREWSKI were accused of being in the pay of a large piano-making firm, and the latter was described in one journal as a mere " shindy-box " thumper, while another newspaper declared in a leader that "The fluffy poll of the artless PADEREWSK/, even to its last golden strand, is a paid advt." In praise of the Chicago young ladies it is,however, to be said that they displayed more self-restraint than their sisters of New York. They did not kiss PADEREWSKI"S face, nor did they mumble over his long white fingers. The gushing—or shall we say, maudlin—enthusiasm of some of the New York young women led them into , both of these forms of excess.</p>
        <p>Mn. E. WitaixsoN, one of the " Positively , assistant commissioners in connec- Satisfactory 2 tion with the Labour Commission, is evidently a gentleman who is easily pleased, or, at all events, he is one who thinks that the agricul- tural labourer should not be difficult to satisfy. In a report which he has presented to :the Labour Commission on certain districts in Derbyshire, Lincolnshire, Staffordshire, and Yorkshire, he declares that in the majority ®f cases the condi- tion of the labourer is "positively satisfactory." That is his opinion, but what are his facts? In Lincolnshire the wages of these labourers whose condition is " positively satisfactory "range from thirteen shillings and sixpence to fifteen shillings a week. Extra work at harvest time brings in a little extra remuneration, but allowing for these " ortras," which, let it not be forgotten, are not given but have to be earned by exhausting and strenuous labour, the average wages of the year do not tonount to sixteen shillings a week. And, then, as to the houses in which the labourers live, we are told that " among the old cottages it is rare to find one, with three bedrooms, and there are great numbers with only one." In connection with this state- ment it is worth while keeping in mind that in many country districts nearly all the cottages are old—very old some of them ; and that the agricul- tural labourer is not unfrequently the head of a numerous family. The Assistant Commissioner further tells us that in one district upon which he reports—and it is fairly typical of a good many others—no draining is attempted. The sewage is allowed to gather in shallow holes in the ground, and, furthermore, the water is generally not good. It is hard to see how conditions such as these can be suitably described as "positively satisfactory." It is certainly to be hoped that while the members of the Labour Commission duly note the facts in the reports that are supplied them, they will not take Cask opinions from too optimistic Assistant Commissioners.</p>
        <p>STOPPAGE OF HAYDOCK MINERS. Taking advantage of the Whitsuntide holidays, next week, and in consequence of dullness in trade, Messrs. Evans and Co., have decided to close their pits at Haydock, Parr, Golborne, and other places, from Thursday night, for five days. This will mean the enforced idleness of nearly 4,000 miners. SEE OUR NEW STOCK Of Disis Materials, Dress Prints, Delaines, IC., EVANS, 17, Saakey-st., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>_ THE PURCHASE OF THE BOLD HALL ESTATE. Sonte of the men on the Bold Hall Estate, near St. Helens, which is being purchased for mining extension, were paid off on Saturday, so as to cur- tail expenses on the estate as much as possible. Various speculations have been made as to who the purchasers are, but great secrecy is being main- tained. UMBRELLAS UECOVERED.—.T.. and W. DUTTON Sankey-SI., Wairtnaton.—Advt. THE HOME IRON TRADE. Matters continue very quiet in the English iron market. Crude iron is in dull enquiry generally, and there still little in the demand for finished iron. Tinplate-makers, on the whole are well occupied. The steel trade gives promise of increasing activity. Shipbuilding exhibits a little amelioration; but engineers and iroafounders are not in receipt of many fresh orders. The coal trade remains quiet.— Iron. iMPERIAL Flax EXTINGUISHIERS.—DARLAND, ITORMOBger, Wturbagton. AFFAIRS OF A RUNCORN TRADESMAN. At the Warrington Bankruptcy Court, yesterday, James Alfred Chesters, 3, Chapel-road, Runcorn, soda water dealer, appeared for the public examina- tion of his accounts. His liabilities were £4Bl and assets £23, leaving a deficit of £458. The alleged ' cause of failure was losses through deaths of horses, and ill health. There was a borrowing liability of £395 and a trade liability of £156 19s. 11d. The debtor said he had kept no books, and therefore was unable to produce any accounts. The examination was closed. THE ST. HELENS CANAL : STOPPAGE OF TRAFFIC. The prolonged drought this spring has had its effect on tho St. Helens Canal which runs from St. Helens to Widnes. The L. and N.-W. Railway Company, to which it belongs gave notice at the end of 4ast week that owing to the continued dry weather there would not be a sufficient supply of water to keep the canal navigable for traffic on and after Monday, May 15. The canal is supplied with water from Eccleston Dam and Carr .Mill near St. Ilelens. These ocdupy an area of about 18 acres when foil,: and are 19 feet deep, but as there has been so little rain since Christmas, they were at the beginning of the week only a little over five feet deep. There is accordinglf an insufficient supply of water for the upper reaches of the canal. For the pond between Bewsey Locks and Widnes there Is a pumping engine at Widnes which supplies that section of the canal with river water. This section may yet be open for traffic, but until there is a larger rainfall, the traffic from St. Helens will be closed. There has never been less water for the canal since 1887,</p>
        <p>=IRS</p>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>THE PROPOSED SUNDAY_ SCIOOL DEMONSTRATION IN</p>
        <p>WIDNES.</p>
        <p>The date of this demonstration has been changed from August Bank Holiday to August 12th.</p>
        <p>WIDNES COUNTY COURT.</p>
        <p>His Honour Judge Shand held a sitting of this court yesterday. The list comprised two hundred cases, Uut none of them were of public interest. POOR RATS FOR NORTON. The Runcorn magistrates on Monday sanctioned the levying of a poor rate of Is. id. in the £ for the ensuing half year. The rate is estimated to produce £368 Bs. 2d. - •</p>
        <p>Go to PRITCHARD'S, 51.1110 i-street, Iluncern, for the be3t FIVE GUINEA WATCH a workingman can have.—Advt. ST. EDWARD'S CATHOLIC FREE SCHOOLS, RUNCORN. In aid of the funds of the free day schoola of St. Edward's, Runcorn, and St. John the Evangelist,Weston, the Rev. Monsignor Slaughter preached to sermons in St. Edward's Chapel, on Sunday. There were good con- gregations. If you want your wiTea REPAIRED by elalle 0 workmen take it to PRITCHARD'S.—Adrt THE ALLEGED KICKING OUTRAGE AT RUNCORN. James Finegan, lodging-house keeper, Merscv-st., will to-day be tried by the justices of Runcorn Police Court, on a charge of unlawfully wounding Frank Lyden.a Ship Canal navvy, by kickiPg him and breaking his 'jaw in Penketh-lane,on the 29th April. Finegan is at liberty on bail.</p>
        <p>FBITCUAED'S have now E. toad the test fcr fifty years, and quality and fir6t-elass workmanship are 6 till guaranteed.— _Advt.</p>
        <p>PRIMITIVE METHODIST SUNDAY SCHOOL, worn:. The annual sermons were preached in Greenv ay-road Chapel, on Sunday, by the Rev.T.Guttery,of Southport. In the afternoon the Rev. W. Arrowsmith, Wesleyan minister' delivered a discourse. The scholars and choir rendered special music at each service, under the leader- ship of Mr. Hillidge. Mr. W. Gittens, jun., was at the organ. The collections amounted to .2.23 18s. Bd. The number of scholars on the roll is 263. MR. BRUNNER, M.P., AND THE WEAVER BILL. Mr. Brunner, M.P., intends on the second reading of the Weaver Bill before the House of Commons to move that it be referred to a Hybrid Committee, which shall have power to inquire into and report upon the constitu- tion of the Trust, and also that the traders shall be allowed to be heard before the committee as to the Trustees having further powers to borrow money.</p>
        <p>jOSEPH 3.-RITCHARD, 51, 11igh-strett, Runcorn, keeps SPECTACLES t,O suit all sights, from a shilling a air—Add. SYSTEMATIC THEFT OF COAL AT WIDNES. On Monday morningat the Widnes Police Court,befora Messrs. H. W. Deacon and G.Pilkington,two lads named Henry Murphy, ten years of age, Nelson-st.,and Thomas Mainwaring, ten years, Nelson-., were charged with stealing a quantity of coals from Mort Liddals Chemical Works, the property of the United Alkali Company. The boys admitted to Superintendent Keighley that they had been stealing coal all week and selling it for 31. a bag.— The Bench ordered each lad to receive six strokes with a birch rod. When in want of a CIIISTE an 1 -ICravi.i.Nr WEDDING or BIRTEIDAT PRESENT go to PEITCIIIIaS, 51, if ig</p>
        <p>COMMISSION OF INQUIRY INTO THE CHEMICAL TRADE. Mr. Asquith has appointed a committee consisting of Mr. A. E. Fletcher, chief inspector of Alkali Works ; Mr. J. Henderson, chief superintending inspector of factories and workshops; Mr. H. M. Richard, inspector of factories and workshops ; Professor Simpson, and Dr. O'Neil of Farnworth, to inquire into the injuries to health alleged to be caused to operatives in Chemical Works, and the desirability of special rules being drawn up f9r their prevention. Ties VISIT OF RUNCORNIANS TO THE EAST. There has been a melancholy termination to the pleasant tour in the East in the case of one of the com. panions of the Rev. Dr. Preston. He was accompanied by the Rev. F. Hoare, of Derby, and they returned togs- ' tiler as far as Venice. The latter desired to proceed homeward in a leisurely manner, and got to Milan. Here his journey was brought to a fatal ending. He has died in that city. Much sympathy is felt by his late fellow-travellers and .his friends for the bereaved family, who looked forward to a happy re-union. JOSEPH PRITCHARD, Watchmaker and Jeweller, 51, High- s, Ruicorn.—Adet. SUDDEN DEATH FROM SMALLPDX AT RUNCORN. At half-past ten on Monday morning a girl named Mary Ann Jukes, aged seven years' daughter of Luke Jukes, labourer of back Mersey-st., died saldenly after a brief illness. The girl was taken ill on Saturday and was put to bed by her mother who did not believe thet illness to be serious. On Monday morning she became I worse and Dr. MacKenzie was sent for but she died 1 before his arrival. In the doctor's opinion death was due to smallpox, and the Coroner deemed it unnecessary I to hold an inquest. THE BRUTAL ASSAULT ON A SISTER AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>„ At Liverpool Assizes on Monday, Christopher Daly, (36), labourer, was charged with having, on the 15th April, at Widnes, feloniously wounded his sister, Annie Daly, with intent tc do her grevious lo)dily harm. Mr. T. swift prosecuted. On the night in question screams and cries of” murder" were heard proceeding from prisoner's house. Some neighbours entered and found Annie Daly, about 34 years of age, lying on the floor, with blood flowing freely from wounds about the head. It seemed that he had struck her with a chair, kicked her, and jumped and danced on her.- 'Mr. Swift said that the sister had nothing to do with this prosecution, but it had been taken up in the interests of the public. Prisoner was found guilty of unlawfully wounding, and sentenced to eight months' imprisonment with hard labour. THE ROLLING-PIN OUTHAGE,.AT WIDNES._</p>
        <p>At Widnes Police Court. on Monday, before Messrs H. Wade Deacon and G. Pilklngton, Catherine Burns, 34 years of age, Major Cross-st., and her son, Patrick Burns were both charged on remand with wounding James Burke, 49, Alford-st., on the 6th inst. It was alleged that the prosecutor took one of the female prisoner's sons home the worse for drink. Mrs. Burns accused Burke of making her son drunk, and afterwards, it is alleged, she struck him a violent blow on the head with a rolling-Pin, while the male prisoner struck him about the head with his belt, several wounds being inflicted.— The case was remanded for a week on the application of Mr. J. W. Knowles, who defended, owing to the alisnce of an important witness for the defence. A DESPICABLE THIEF IT RAINITILL. At Widnes Police Court, on Monday before Messrs. H. Wade Deacon and 0. Pilkingtou, a teamster named Arthur Scragg, Cronton-lane, Rainhill, was charged with stealing a hen's egg, the property of his employer, John Cartwright, farmer, Rainhill, on Saturday morning. The prosecutor said that for the last two years he had missed a great number of eggs and other farm produce. Hs had discharged several of his servants owing to the prosecutor snaking accusation:: against them. His sus- picions against the prisoner were aroused, and lie put the matter in the hands-of the police. On Saturday morning Police-constable Palmer secreted himself in the loft, and saw the prisoner take the egg but of the hen. cote. The prisoner pleaded guilty, and he was fined 20s. with the alternative ef fourteen day's imprisonment with hard labour. SERIOUS CHARGE AGAINST A WIDNES PROPERTY OWNER At Widnes Petty Sessions on Thursday, before Mr. H. Gaskell, junr., Henry Dagnall, labourer, of Bryn-street, and James Edwardson, forgeman, at the Tharsis Copper Works, who is a large property owner in the town, were placed in the dock charged with stealing a quantity of timber valued £3 from the Timber Yard of Thomas Sad- ler, builder, Widnes-road. Mr. J. W. Knowles defended Edwardson.—The prosecutor stated that Da gnall was in his employ, and about five o'clock that morning he saw him go to the yard and unlock the door. After lie had been in the yard a few minutes, the prisoner looked down the road and then another man came lin with a handcart, which after it was filled with tirobar and mortar, was wheeled away tgy the prisoner and the other man, whom the prosecutor could not identify. Infor- mation was given to the police, and P.C. Evans went to the backof Midwood-street where he found the handcart containing the tinter, which the prosecutor identified as his. The conatablii charged Edwardson with receiving timber, well knowing it to have been stolen, and he re- plied that he had bought it froin Degnall.—Mr. Knowles contended that there was no evidence against Edward- son, but en the application of Superintendent KeighleY, both ptifenvri, were remanded for a week bail being allowed.</p>
