      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 20, 1893.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 20, 1893.</p>
        <p>I Durin</p>
        <p>RUNCORN FREE LIBRARY. Lst ithllo</p>
        <p>issued. from</p>
        <p>—,,rimg the past mont 1,798 books were this library. The Committee have decided to add 198 new voluincs, WHITSUNTIDR AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The usual demonstration of Friendly Societies and scholars will take place on Whit Monday. The only societies expected to parade are, "Star of Freedom," and " Lily of the Valley." WIDNES PARISH CHURCH ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL CLUB. The above club have appointed the following officers for next season :—First team captain, J. W. Clibborn ; sub-captain, T. Davies ; secretary J. Nestor ; and trea- surer, A. E. Parr. RuNcorix UNION SCHOOL ATTENDANCE COMMITTER.</p>
        <p>The first meeting of the newly-appointed committee was held on ruesday, at Dutton Workhouse. The Rev. J. W. N. Tanner was unanimously re-appointed chair- man, and the various local committees were appointed without discussion. ANOTHER ACCIDENT ON THE SHIP CANAL WORKS.</p>
        <p>At an early hour on Thursday morning, at Saltport, George Hughes, aged 18 years, engaged on the Manches- ter Ship Canal Works as pointsman, met with a serious accident. Whilst engaged at the points for a train to pass, the handle rebounded with great force and struck him on the abodomen. His conEtion was somewhat serious, and he was placed upon rai engine and conveyed to the hospital at Latchford. On being medically exam- ined it was found that he was suffering from shock owing to the blow. DRAWING-ROOM MEETING &amp;T FARNWORTH.</p>
        <p>- Ii ij yr the funds of qq Dinesan C.E.T.S. Police Curt and Prison date Mission, a drawing•room meeting was held at Farnworth Vicarage on Thursday afternoon. Addresses were delivered by the Rev. Richard Fostance, M.A., vicar of St. George's, Everton, Rev. Charles Courteney, M.A., vicar of Emmaauel Church, Everton , the Rev. J. Must, diocesan secretary, Mr. Henry Taylor, dh.cesan C.E.T.S. police court missionary for Widnes, Warrington, and Newton, and Mr. H. Wade Deacon, J.P., C.C. The Vicar, the Rev, J. Wright Williams presided and in conjunction with Mrs. Williams enter- tained their numerous guests to light refreshments.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>THREATENED STRIKE OF JOINERS AT WIDNES.</head>
        <p>THREATENED STRIKE OF JOINERS AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>The joiners of Widnes have given notice to the master builders that they will cease work on June Ist unless they agree to the following code of rules " Ordinary working hours, 55 per week in summer, in winter 50 in the shop, where artificial light is provided, and 471 ix other cases. Wages Bd. per hour, except where artificial light is provided, when 811 shall be paid, overtime, time and a quarter from 5.30 to 10 p.m., and time and a half from that time to 6 a.m. Sondays double time. No piece work to be allowed on any cotilderation. No reply has been received from the masters. The chief grievance is in regard to overtime for which the inmi. ters pay what they think fit. The movement does not effect the large works.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>INSPECTION OF POUCH AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>INSPECTION OF POUCH AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday afternoon the annual Government in- spection of the Runcorn Division of Constabulary took place at the Police Station, by Sir Edward Croft, Bart., her Majesty's inspfxtor. There were on parade Super- intendent Atherton, Inspector Cooper, five serFeants, and 25 constables, the other members of the division being engaged on duty. The clothing and accoutre- ments were inspeated, and found to be satisfactory. Th. men were afterwards put through a courso of drill, and passed through the trial with great credit. The books, offices, rooms, cells, Sze. were thoroughly examined. After making a strict utvestigation, the Inspector ex- pressed himself well satisfied with what he had seen, and complimented the members of the division upon their smart and intelligent appearance. ALLEGED SHOCKIN4 OUTRAGE ON GIRLS AT WIDNES. At Widnes Police Court, on Wednesday, an elderly man named Edward Warren, a chairmaker, living at li, Midwood-st.' was charged under the Criminal Law Amendment Act, with assaulting two girls named Norah Lloyd, aged 12, of 42, Kent-st., and Jessie McCracken, aged 10, of 7 Elliot-st. About half-past seven on Sat- urday evening the girls went to the prisoner's home to inquire about a chair he was repairing for the McCracken's, and it is alleged that the prisoner com- pelled them to stay in the house all night until two o'clock on Sunday afternoon. The doors were kept locked during the whole of the time.—The girls stated that they slept in the same bed as the prisoner and he assaulted both of them.—The prisoner was committed to the Assizes for trial. WIDNES ATHLETIC CLUB SPORTS. - - -</p>
        <p>These sports are fixed to take place on Friday, June 9th, and June 10th, and no effort is being spared to make them the success which is usually associated with this athletic fixture. The reputation of being the best meeting in the north of England is to be kept up. It is a noteworthy fact that the entrance fee for the local events has been reduced to 2s. e9ch event. Alderman Gossage, the Mayor, who is president of the club, as usual, is giving a first prize of five guineas.which on this occasion will be appropriated to the 120 yards local handicap. The United Alkali Co. have generously pro- mised £lO 103. to be divided into three prizes, and these are to be for the half mile flat race handicap, confined to the employes of the company. A new feature amongst the open events on the Saturday is the three-quarter mile steeplechase with water jump. Entrance for the tug-of-war is free to teams of six employes in any one works, club, er other association.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN AT</head>
        <p>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN AT</p>
        <p>RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>BY AN OCCASIONAL CoItRESYONDENT.</p>
        <p>Passing through Halton this week, I was pleased to notice that the path, being a short cut from Halton Brook to the village, had been thoroughly repaired and put in good condition Tbe footpath had got in adreadful, not to say condition, state, and as it was nobody's business to repair it, the township having no power and the Highway Board declining, I was curious to know who were the benefactors to pedestrians. I learned that the expense of the work had been borne by Mrs. Cox, Dr. Carruthers, Mr. Thomas Wright, and Mr. John Lea.</p>
        <p>Mr. Joseph Stokes, of Rocksavage Farm, Runcorn, appears to have created a record in potato grow- ing. He gathered potatoes from the open field on the 11th inst. They were " regents " of good size and splendid quality. It is highly probable that no other Cheshire farmer gathered potatoes so early. About 30 years ago, the father of Mr. Stokes, who was a noted potato grower lifted potatoes on May 12th.</p>
        <p>It is expected that in the coarse of next week Runcorn Docks, which have been closed since July last, will be re-opened for traffic. Highly pleased as the traders will be at this news, the directors of the Ship Canal Company will be equally delighted. The traffic has been conducted through Weston Point at enormous cost and much inconvenience.</p>
        <p>The smallpox epidemic appears to "ebb and flow" very strangely. A fortnight ago the disease was speeding rapidly, it now appears to be again on the decline. As the disinfector at Moore has not so far been used by the Board of Commissioners, one is curious to know how the disinfecting of clothing, &amp;c., is being carrid on, but it is difficult to obtain any reliable information. Is the disease spread through the went of a proper system of disinfec- tion ?</p>
        <p>The directors of the United Alkali Co. evidently take a deep interest in athletics. Thay have recently provided good medals for a football com- petition ; at the forthcoming athletic sports at Widnes, they are providing the first, second and third prizes for one of the local events, and it is stated that they are in negociation with the Leigh trustees to purchase land for a recreation ground to be the home of cricket, football, Scc.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Saunders Gilliat. M,P., for Widnes division has presented a petition in the House of Commons from the residents of liuyton, protesting against the Welsh Suspensory Bill.</p>
        <p>The numerous friends of the Rev. W. Stanton Jones will be glad to learn that an arrangement has been made for him to continue his good work in Widnes. He had accepted an appointmentat Ince, but the parishioners of ‘4idnes were so loath to lose one to whom they were deeply attached that the new vicar was approached on the subject, and he at once invited Mr. Jones to stay, and that, gentleman eventually accepted the senior curacy of Widnes,. notwithstanding that from a pecuniary point of view the Ir.ce appointment was much butter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>FRODSITA-I‘.r.</head>
        <p>FRODSITA-I‘.r.</p>
        <p>Wrirrsurrnin.—flie festivities here will be on som.e- what similar lines to those of twiner years. On Whit- Mt-May there mill be processions of the Foresters, Druids, Oddfellows, aryl Dachabite Friendly Societies. TRINITY CUATTL. —On Monday afternoon, a sermon was preached in the abare chapel by the Rev. Pcter Mackenzie. A public tva meeting WAS hei aftcrwatds in the schoplroom adjoinimz. In the evenimr the rev. aentleman delivered tii paPular en" The Desert Prophet, hl4 mihistry and m trtyrdom." proceeds of the day were in aid of thy trust fund.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>RUNCORN WIFE DESERTION CASE,</head>
        <p>A RUNCORN WIFE DESERTION CASE,</p>
        <p>On Wednesday morning, at the Runcorn Police Court, before Dr. Steele, and Mr. P. Speakman, a summons against Frederick Ellison, blacksmith, Stretford, for deserting his wife, Ellen Ellison, was heard. Mr. E. Wynne Humphreys appeared for complainant. and Mr. Batty (Manchester), repre- sented defendant. Mr. Humphreys rested his con- tention on the alleged desertion taking place 11 months since, and the cessation of maintenance four years ago. The parties had been married 33 years, and although their earlier wedded life might not have been of the hap- piest description, their quarrels did not assume an acute form until four years ago. Then they lived on the Nor- ton estate, near Runcorn, and with a view to adding to his income, defendant took a beerhouse at Nantwich, purchased the ingoings, had the license in his name, and placed his wife in charge of it. On March 10th, 1892, he &amp;sorted her. His conduct was unbearable, he was scarcely ever sober, and lie was constantly hurling foul epithets at her. She told hiin he ought to support her, but he plainly expressed his intention not to contribute to her maintenance, as he had " one woman to keep, which was quite enough." Mrs. Ellison fi9und the beer- house could not be made to pay, and the place was con- sequently closed. Adultery was alleged against him. Mr. Batty, for the defence, denied the alleged adultery, and said witnesses would prove that lie was lodging at the house wife!' he was seen to visit. He contended that his client paid over £4O for the ingoing of the public. house, and the receipts were taken by his wife. He urged that the separation was made by mutual consent. As to language and conduct there was blame on both sides, Fqrther, there had Veen I.)c:pf qfer made by the defendant to provide a home for his wife. . . _</p>
        <p>The defendant -bore out this statement, and deposed to his wife having assaulted him many a time. He would not say she was of drunken habits, but she liked a "sops." (Laughter.)—BV Mr. Humphreys : His wife was as dear to him as his own heart, but he had never tried to find her since they parted. (Laughter.) He did not knock four teeth out, for as a matter of fact she had had no teeth in her head for the last 20 years. (Laughter.) He emphatically denied committing adul- tery. He had never offered marriage to another woman, and never would marry again. (Laughter.) A large number of witnesses were examined. The Bench considered the charge of desertion had been proved and made an order against the defendant for the payment of 103. a week towards his wife's maintenance. He would also have to pay the costs including advocate's fee, amounting to £4 17s. 61. They refused the applica- tion of Mr. Batty to state a case for a superior court.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>RUNCORN FOOTBALL CLUB. ANNUAL MEETING.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN FOOTBALL CLUB. ANNUAL MEETING.</p>
        <p>The walla' meeting of the members of Runcorn Football Club, was held at Mr. Riley's Coffee Cafe. Mr. P. Speakman, J.P,,C.C.,presided and there was a large attendance. The Secretary ( Mr. James Langshaw) read the report. TUE SECRETARY'S REPORT. Mr. Langshaw, the secretary, in his report saia . _I am sure we can congratulate ourselves all round on u.. work of the season ended on the 29th April last. Before proceeding to statistics we must all deplore the loss the Club has sustained since our last meeting ef one who was then present. 1 refer to the death of Mr. T. 11. Davies, who for a considerable time had been connected with the club, and by his genial, kindly disposition had endeared himself to a host of friends in Runcorn and in football circles throughout the country.—The Secretary then passed in review each match during the season,and referring to the celebrated Warrington match said, next was the now famous Warrington match, which has caused quite a flutter in that dovecote. \\e won after having the best of the game by a try. The rough play of Warrington was followed by the committee cancelling all future engagements, which action was endorsed by a full meeting of the Lancashire County Committee. Our totals ars : \Von 20, lost 8, drawn 5. Runcorn scored 24 goals, 43 tries, to their opponents 11 goals, 13 tries. In points Runcorn stood 201, against their op- ponents 84. The tries were scored by Crompton, ten ; Morris, seven ; J. Faulkner, seven ; G. Robieson, five ; G. 11. Kingston, five ; J. Langley, three ; Abrams, T. Robinson, H. Williams, S. Houghton, C. Luya, H. Far- mer, A. Hampson, all two each ; A. Taylor, P. Riley, J. Evans, F. Gayter, IV. Faulkner, Darbyshire, Gayter, Warder, Davies, Hughes, and Antrobus, one each. Goals from free kicks were obtained—Crompton, two; W. Faulkner, one ; dropped gals, T. %Varier, two ; Crompton, one ; penalty goals,!'. Warder, two: Cromp- ton. one ; penalty goals, Warder, one. Cheshire county again called heavily upon us, and many times we were pressed to the utmost. S. Houghton and W. Faulkner gained their North caps. Houghton was also reserve to the English team. Crompton and Faulkner also played in the Best of England v. Yorkshire match. The treat- ment of our captain, Crompton, in the Eagland v. Scot- land match, is still fresh in our memories. It seems a strange decision to pass their own own selected reserve centre, and play a wing man in that position. The list of attendances shows that players attended very closely to their duties, and have done remarkably well. The Treasurer's report pointed to the fact that a heavy de- ficit has been practically wiped out. Seeing. the bad season, rain every week, it was only at the beginning of March we were able to pull together and get the club free from liabilities.</p>
        <p>The second team played 25 games, won 20, drew one, lost four. The following have obtained the tries :—T. Antrobus, four; J. Faulkner, three; 0. Dunbavand, nine ; T. Warder, three ; J. Green, six; A. Limb, one ; Hayes, one ; Jordan, two ; F.Darlington, two ; G. Welbe, five ; J. Nixon, ten ; T. Robinson, four ; W. Evans, evro; F. Gayter, two ;J. Molyneux, three; A. Clarke, two ; J. Coleshill, one ;R. Harrison, one; T. Climes, two ;G. Moores, one ;A. Abel, two; T. Woodward, one; Butterworth, one. Dropped goals: Antrobus, one ;0. Dunbavand, one; T. Warder, three. The Chairman moved the adoption of the report.—Mr. Shore seconded. —The motion was unanimously adopted.</p>
        <p>TEE TREASURER S REPORT.</p>
        <p>Mr. C. Pownall read tlin treasurer's statement of accounts f—Tla&amp; receipt side showed—Subscriptions, £54 ; gate money, £598 17a. 3d. ; insurance, £55; rent, £lO ' • Parr's Bank overdraft, £l5 14s. 10d. ; total. £733 12s. id. The payments were—Parr's Bank over- draft last season, £74 mi. 11d. ; hotel, travelling expen- ses and refreshments, £196 3s. 81.; rates and taxes, £35 Bs. 6d. ; jerseys, boots, &amp;c., £64 Os. sd. ; donation Davies' fund, £l5 ; insurance of players, £B2 ; doctor's fees, embrocation, &amp;c., £2O 14s. ; hire of conveyances, £l4 18s. 7d. ; printing, stationary, and advertising, £67 2s. 10d. ; gatemen, police, referees, postages, tele- grams, &amp;c., £156 12s. 6d. ; cash in treasurer's hands, £6 93. Bd. ; total, £733 12s. id. The assets were £133 ss. 11d., and the liabilities £4O 14s. 10J., the balance in favour of the club being £92 11s. Id. against £42 9s. 4d., last season. In the liabilities provision had been made for a memorial stone for the late Mr. T. H. Davies, in the Runcorn Cemetery, which was in courso of execution. The gate receipts were £46 in excess of last year, and the expenses of outfits, travelling and refreshments, £B9 less than in 1891-2. On the motion of. the Chairman seconded by Mr. S. Houghton the report was adopted. VOTE OF THANES, - _</p>
        <p>Mr. John Clark moved that the best thanks of the club be tendered to the players, officials, and committee for their services during the past year. From the re- ports they had every reason to be satisfied with the past year's record. The players had kept up the reputation of the club, and he believed they would do the same in the future, and that that meeting ought to give them the heartiest encouragement.—Mr. F. Hancock seconded the motion, which was carried.</p>
        <p>- THE PRESIDENT. The Secretary announced that Mr. S. Taylor had with- drawn his nomination for the position of president, and he propesed that Mr. Speakman again be elected to the position.—Mr. John Wilson seconded, and the proposi- tion was carried—The President in acknewledging the compliment, said it was now many years since the mem- bers gave him their confidence by electing him president of the club,and he should have been very willing to have gracefully given way fer any one the dub might have appointed. His interest in the club would have been the same. (Applause.) He did not think he should ever be a football player,but he was certainly more enthusiastic in football now than he was when he first became con- nected with them. He joined the club because he thought that there was not sufficient interest being taken in the hard-headed footballers of the town who were re- nowned wherever football was known. Be travelled a great deal, and Runcorn team was always spoken of in the hiellest praise. He hoped they would give him credit for what he said,when he told them that he did not come amongst them to patronise them, but to be one of them, and his aim was to do what he could to bring the art of football to a place of higher merit. He should be very • sorry to think that Runcorn ' team was , like some teams he could mention. He should hays liked to have mentioned one team but he did not , know whether it would be wise. There was one team I played at Runcorn a short time ago that gave him very great pain, and he was very pleased indeed that the officers of the club had decided not to play that team I again. (Applause.) It was not to the interest of foot- ball to play an unmanly game, and he was pleased to I think that Runcorn played such a game that whither 1 they won or !oat they could shake hands. They had the reputation in Runeorn of playing a gentlemanly game, and he hoped that they would uphold that reputation in the future. (Applause.) The president then referred in touching terms to the lamented death of Davies, and expressed his high appreciation of the fact that the club had shown their sympathy with the widow in iL practi- cal form. (Applause.) As their president lie was not istiffied with the finances of the club, hut he was not afraid. He did not like debit balances at the bank and he thought hy a little arrangement they could succeed in having a balance at the bank and also an enclosure on the field to protect the spectators in stormy weather. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>ELICTION OF orribErte. _ _</p>
        <p>The election of officers was then proceeded with. The voting was by ballot, and Messrs. Jno. Clark, G. S.</p>
        <p>Frith, J. Moulton and Joynson, were appointed scru- tineers. W. Faulkner was appointed captain by 75 votes against three received by S. Hougfiton who was elected sub-captain without opposition.—George Robin- son was appointed captain of second team, and Jno. ' Stubbs sub-captain. Mr. J. E. Langshaw was unani- mously re-appointed secretary; Mr. Jno. Ellison, assist- ant secretary ; Mr. C. Pownall, treasurer; Messrs. Edward Key and Edward Lunt, auditors; J. Sherlock umpire first team, and D. Massey umpire second team. The following were elected the Committee: Messrs. Jno. Wilson, 65 votes, Walter Jones 52, W. Beckett 50, J. Gatley 62, W. H. Cawley 57, T. Moores 51, S. Houghton 54, IV. Shore 48, A. Taylor 43, J. E. Goodwell 50, G. Lea 38, J. Evans 40. There were 31 nominated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>MANAGEMENT.</head>
        <p>THE MANAGEMENT.</p>
        <p>Mr. C. Pownall moved a resolution altering rule 2 to read that the management of the club should be en- trusted to a committee, which should consist of twelve members, the captains and vice-captains of the first and second tcams,and the secretary, assi,tant-secretary, and treasurer.—Mr. Shore seconsled.—Mr.John Clark moved a rider that the last three-named officials shall ke allowed to speak but not to vote.—This was accepted, and the resolution as amended was carried. On the proposition of Mr. Harry Wright, seconded by Mr. W. Faulkner, it was unanimously resolved to allow the Sunday schools the use of the field on Whit- Monday.</p>
        <p>NON-PAIING MEMBERS. On the proposition of Mr. H. Wright, it was decided that the members who had not paid their subscriptions be given notice that unless the money was paid within a month their names would be expunged from the roll of the club.</p>
        <p>PATRON 9, The names of Mr. W. S. Callender and Dr. Burton (Frodsham) were added to the list of patrons. It ham previously been resllved that the patrons would be ex- pected to give a donation of 10s. 61. A vote of thank' to the Chairman concluded the meet- ing.</p>
        <p>THE FIXTURES. Mr. J. E. Langshaw, the secretary, stated that he had arranged fixtures for the next season with the following clubs :—Halifax, Bradford, Newport, Swinton, Glouces- ter, Salford, Wigan, Liverpool, Liverpool Old Boys, Burton-on-Trent, Old Edwardian, St. Helens, Birken- head Park, Naw Brighton, Broughton, St. Helens Re- creation, Stockport, Widnes, Sile, and Tuebrook. The latter two will be played on Cheshire county dates.</p>
        <p>COMPETITIONS AT THE GYMNASIUM.</p>
        <p>The following are the results of the gymnastic com- petitions held privately among the members of the gymnasium on Thursday.</p>
        <p>LADIES' COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>Names. 1 I o.°l F-4" 46 1 Miss R. Allen ll 17 n 7 10 12 17 831 2 Miss K. Brundrett 12 15 11 5 11 10 17k 811 3 Miss Simpson lO 151 12 5 8/ 9 14- 74 4 Miss Hartley la, 10 14 4 51 9 7 15 631 5 Miss L. Allen IF 11 4 10 5 9 91 14/ 64 6 Miss Balsliaw lO 131 14 5/ si 9 9 63 7 Miss Crompton</p>
        <p>BOYS' COMPETITION. IC. Boardmsn l2 18 10 11 10 18 181 97/ 2 r'• t-Divward 3 W•AlL(lreadie lO 15 11 71 9 14/ 17/ 841 48. Critmo.w SW. Warren 9/ 111 12 6/ 9 11/ 14; 74/ 6L. Davies 7 8/ n 8 6 101 111 61 There were three medal for each competition, pre- sented by the president, Mr. T. Morris ; Mr. J. E. Warren and Mr. G. Vernon acted aa fudges, and gave every satisfaction. 1</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>FIRST BAND PERFORMANCE IN law</head>
        <p>FIRST BAND PERFORMANCE IN law</p>
        <p>PARK.</p>
        <p>THE ARRANGEMENTS FOR MAY AND JUNE.</p>
        <p>The new band stand in the park was for the first time this season occupied on Sunday afternoon, when the band of the Ist Battalion Prince of Wales Volunteers played in an excellent manner. The programme gone through has already been pub- lished in the "Examiner." The band was under the conductorship of Mr. Armstrong, and the music was listened to by about 2,000 persons. Amongst the spectators present were the Mayor and Mayoress (Alderman and Mrs. Greenall) and several town Councillors. Boxea were placed as usual in various parts of the ground for the reception of subscriptions in aid of the expenscs of the bands for the season, but the response, considering the large assembly of people ! was disappointing. The total amount realised was 12s. Hid. which included 0110 half sovereign and a French halfpenny. The amount was afterwards made to total 13 shillings. The Corporation have decided to contribute £25 towards the expenses, but it must be admitted that the prospects of realising a satisfactory amouat during the season to meet the expenses, are not at the present very bright. The music is to be pro- vided by the'' Warrington Association of Bands for Providing Music in the Park," of which Mr. T. .1. Down is the president, Councillor F. W. Monks treasurer, and Mr. .lE'sepli Taylor secretary, with a committee consist- ing of two representatives from each of the nine bands in the association, who altogether number 300 instru- mentalists. The following is a list of the bands who will : play in the park during May and June :—May 21st, St. Paul's band ; 28, League of the Cross; June 4, Warring- ton United ; 5, St. Paul's, dancing ; 11, Cockhedge Mission ; 15, Warrington United ; 18, Buckley-street Mission ; 19, League of the Cross ; 25, Messrs. Fletcher and Co. ; 28th, Volunteer Band. It is expected that performances will be given until the end of September, and that during the season the bandstand will also be occupied by Mr. Down's band, and by those connected with the Blue Coat School and the Pad,gate Industrial Schools.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>WHITSUNTIDE. ARRANGEMENTS.</head>
        <p>WHITSUNTIDE. ARRANGEMENTS.</p>
        <p>In aid of the funds of the Royal Albert Edward Infirmary and Dispensary, Wigan, a grand gala will be given in the Mesnes Park,-Wigan, on Whit Mon- day. The gates will be open at one a'clolk and stage performances and sports will commense at two o'clock. As will be seen from a lengthy adver- tisement on our front page tbe attractions are of a varied and interesting character. Three brass bands will be in attendance, viz. : Wigan Borough Brass and Reed Band, Pemberton Old Brass Band, and Crooke Brass Baud. Mr. W. E. Royal Murray, of the Empire PARC°, Wigan, has had the arrangements for the supply of a star variety and novelty company of special artistes, and he has brought together a company whose performances will be a treat to witness. Be- sides the stage performances, there ill be a programme of athletic sports, open to all all amateurs from within a radius of nine miles round Wigan, when valuable prizes will be competed for. In the Park pavilion there will be two martinee concerts 'at three and five o'clock, and a special evenieg concert at seven. At dusk there will also be a grand display of fire- works by Messrs. G. I'. Brock and Co., London. Special trains have been arranged by the railway companies. The admissioh is fixed at the low figure of sixpence, and favoured with line weather, the gala promises to be a success. In addition to the events mentioned, there will be a daylight display (in the afternoon) of balloons, grotosquely-shaped to represent animals.</p>
        <p>The London and North Western and Gecat Western are making extensive arrangements for holiday makers dur- ing the Whitsuntide holidays. Their arrangements com- prise trips to Liverpool and Manchester, Llangollen. Bale and Central Wales, South West part of England, Dublin, the watering places round Cardigan Bay, Sou!ii Wales, London, Glasgow and Edinburgh. the Lake The- trict, South West Scotland, Llandudno, Rhyl, and other places in North Wales, and Chester, so intending holiJay makers cannot complain for want of choice.</p>
        <p>Tha Royal Mail and Expreas--steamers to the Isle of Man will no doubt be well patronised as they have Special one day excursions which extent over Virnitsveek at the moderate price of saloon 76., fore cabin 4s. 61. The first-class, full-pawered screw steamier " Bear" leaves Liverpool this afterncym 1.,1r Greenock and Glas- gow. The Tudne and the Bonnie Princess leave each day next week for Llandudno, Beaumaris, Bangor, and Menai Bridge.</p>
        <p>_HUSBY. -</p>
        <p>OUTIIREAIE OF SMALLPDX.—The inhabitants of this quiet village have been somewhat disturbed by the knowledge that a case of smallpox had appeared in their midst. It appears, that a collier, out of work, hailing from Wigan, after visiting Chester, was returning home. He was engaged by a resident to do a few days' work. During his stay he became ill, and on being medically examined it was discovered that he was suffering from the disease named. The officials of the Rural Sanitary Authority were communicated with, and the man was removed to the hospital at Moore on Thursday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>PENKETIL</head>
        <p>PENKETIL</p>
        <p>THE RAINFALL.—The rainfall this week has dose a large amount of good to the crops around here. The .round in many places was beginning to crack with „. the!, intense heat, and the hay grass, which was terribly scorched has now recovered its natural form, and, given fine weather, will prove an abundant crop. The corn is alio much improved by the rain, also the root crops. BAND PARADE.—On Saturday afternoon last the brass hand paraded the village playing some of their liveliest tunes. The band halted at intervals to give the people a better hearing. The musla was very much enjoyed by the inhabitants, and many complimentary remarks wer.! passed on tha players. The parade was in charge of the bandmaster, Mr. J. Bowyer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>THINGS . HEARD__ AND SEEN',</head>
        <p>THINGS . HEARD__ AND SEEN',</p>
        <p>AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>It will be news to a great many people in War- rington that Mr. Percy Davies was at one time tnember of the Warrington first football team, hay- ing held the position of three-quarter back.Pootball was evidently of a very vigiarous character in thoso days, as in playing with Wigan at Warrington, Mr. Davies says, that Mr. Hutchins, the tanner, who was captain of the Warrington team was very badly hurt. In the return match at Wigan, the tables were turned, and the Wigan captaia was placed hors de combat. Mr. Davies also played his last time at this match, as owing to an over-anxiety of him- self and one of his comrades to get the ball, they kicked each other, and Mr. Davies apparently bad had enough of it and retired into a quieter sphere of life. His interest in football, however, has not waned, as he is president, of the Warrington Foot- ball Club, and has also .liis year presented a beau- tiful cup and medals to the winners of the Junior Rugby League of this town.</p>
        <p>I notice that Mr. A. H. Crosfield emerged from the golf handicap at Freslatield on Monday, as the win- ner of the first prize. Very little is known of golf players in Warrington, and it may probably in- terest your raders to learn that a few Warring- tonians have for years taken an active part in help- ing to popularize and extend the gacae in this district.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. 11. Crosfleld is an active member of the 1 lioylake golf club,which includes amongst its mem- bers some of the best golfers in England ; and their link is certAinly the most important in the north. The club, I learn, was started in 1867, and one of the original founders was Mr. Jolla Dan, of Warrington. He still takes an active interest in it,and I think,in- dee‘l,l am pretty sure, it was mainly owing to him that Mr. James Fairclouh, Mr. Charles Hutchings, and other Warringtontans took up the game. Several of the number have achieved success in different competitions, but Mr. Charles Hutchings is considered to be the most brilliant player. in- deed whilst already in the front rank of players he would have been, in the opinion of his friends, second to few, if any, living golfers, had he not been heavily handicapped by indifferent health. Mr A. 11. Crosfi Ad and his cousin, the successful cricketer' Mr. S. M. Crosfield, are very much to the fore in the club. But they are both young golfers and as the player in golf, much more than in any ot her game, requires time and practice to develop what latent skill lie may pessess,it is generally ex- pected that both gentlemen, in another season or so, will be winning conspicuous honours. M</p>
        <p>r. S. M. Crosfield was fortunate in securing an early win this spring at the first two leading annual competitions at floylake. He won the first handicap prize on the first day, and the same for the best aggregate score for the two days, and strange to say he succeeded with precisely the same figure that Mr. A. 11. Croslield scored last year. Freshfield, whete Mr. A. IL Croefield achieved his victory last Monday, is one of those new links which are relieving tile enormous pressure on the older greens, for golf like so many other things now-a-days is getting slightly overdone. There are 700 members in the lloylalse club, and 400 in this new link, which is only one of a number which is springing up on all sides. It is an excellent course, and being near the see shore had conse- quently advantage over the inland links in the mat- ter of turf.</p>
        <p>The Theatre Royal in Scotland-road has had a chequ=red history, and a long one too, as it is the oldest tlit—tre in the town. Since the death of the his widow, Mrs. Sheridan, has blaeteenMtrh.eSiPerrolPliZilv-,ss of the building. The lease of its present manager, Mr. &amp;ape, expired this week, and all at once the " house " eemed to be- come valua'le property in the qyes of many. Snape had commenced to float a ..,mpanv for its purchase, and had already obtained ti promise of several public men to become shareholders, ---,hen it was sold for £1,600 to the proprietors of the L-73,1 Court Theatre. I have heard that it is intended to convert it into a variety theatre; but we must wait until the secret is divulged. By the way next Saturday night a well organised performance is going to be given for the benefit of Mrs. Sheridan, bafore she finally severs her connection with the place. As the late Mr. Sheridan and his wife made many friends in the town, I am expecting to see a crowded audience present.</p>
        <p>Speaking of theatrical matters by the way, makes me inquire " Where is the Warrington Amateur Theatrical Society ? " Surely they are not dead. , but merely sleepeth. In days gone by they used to give such capital performances and assist so thoroughly public charities, that I thought they had behiad them more energy and life than to allow themselves to sink so suddenly int) obscur- ity. The occasion I have Just alluded to would have been a capital chance for making a re-appear- ance.</p>
        <p>A question arose at the nseeting of the Board of Guardians on Wednesday, as to the inconveniences caused by tramps being taken into the ordinary ward at the Workhouse, and allusion was also wade to the recent charges against a Union official who had defended himself against the violence of a tramp suffering from delirium &amp;men&amp; In exonerating the porter, 31r. Davies compared him with a policeman and said: "What policeman in Warrington woold stand quietly by and see his head smashed in." Well, I know that the " bobby" is an ingenious and long- suffAng man,but I think that if be combined both ingenuity and patience to any extent, he could never perform the wonderful feat of stand- ing by watching someone smash his head. All the same Mr. Davies's remarks were well meant, and I agreed with the general opinion that was expressed that the tramp should be kept apart from the wards wherein are confined" the sick and aged and infirm inmates."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>ME RLIN.</head>
        <p>ME RLIN.</p>
        <p>THE NEW WARRINGTON BATHS.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday afternaon next, the new baths- which have just been completed, will he publiely opened. The opening ceremony will be performed by his Worship the Mayor, Alderman E. Greenall, assisted by other public gentlemen. Now that it has been completed the new bath presents a sight which is well deserving of the remark that " it is the finest plunge bath in Lancaahirs." It ia 75 feet long and 43 feet wide, there being a diffetence of, 20 feet alone in length between it and the old bath.. The bed is composed of paddled clay and concrete,. on the top of which have been laid edgeways whiter glazed bricks interspersed with glazed blue bricks: The sides ere lined in a simi!ar manner. The box,s, of which there are seventy are the snuggest things of their kind that an aquatic performer could with for. They are made of pitch pine, with a rear wall of glazed bricks The floor round the bath .is made of concrete on iron joists, and beneath it rune a subway which will facilitate the handling of the supply pipes. Right round the bath, on the top of the boxes is a balcony, with trestle- worked sides. Tbit; balcony, together with certain parts of the ground floor of the building will accammo- ' date no•fewer than 2,000 spectators, and this provision has been made in view of tho fact that swiinnung galas are z.o become sonadva features of the place, The roof of the building k of wood, with iron principles, and con- tains a large number of windows so as to admit ample light. Another boiler has been fitted up, and mains for obtaining a water supply of Appleton Water have been laid. There is also a connection existing with the water supply from Winwick,and it is expected that both waters will be utilized. It will take 112,000 gallons to fill the new bath atone, and a similar amount to supply the two otwbofiwtilfatdhi b acdidoll hhdretudi hhti sixpence. additional silowx, frnopoe beenarhevel inoatcenmt c. Nyvda officestli brhlealr iases,tetn. thb adhi lairotoavbfueitteaenkubr:lhoz atnremoyoe srpeperseaarei rnlcne :sta. oci.et eTnrfooldbaditr.eb.icostoi alteration, greatl nsd,r,esl anlhstelnelaxn:stupwhst nett, oo)nameb enrnn ateksi: icanesggt hii tvi neounn.evrvweehdreynnessadthatiyes,facthiot;n to have the Cor- -130.1) 0.1 ;cat tut, itnoi a-osnft d.s, li Ptul aonf tole same (Onion. _ .</p>
        <p>„ - During the week the Baths Committee lieVe appointed Mr. A. Barloww and Mrs. Barlow, as manager aud 12:11iI• agaress. This is an appointment which cannot but' meet with public approval as Mr. Barlow 'hag tile' reputation of being the best swimmer, ma the ty:st authority on swimming for many miles aroun.l. lie, we understand, is likely to give the aquatic art grat impetus in the town when things have been ut luta ” working order." • - p</p>
        <p>After the opening ceremony has hewn on Wednesday afternoon a well organised gala will be held. pnformed In the first part of the programme Professor Lomax will give a display of ernamental swhnming, including AUCiI feats as smoking under the water, rating under_ the water, peeling an apple, and also a number of instruc- tions of .how to swim when undLr pltygical difficulties undet water. There Will Aie:o b an exhibition by Mirs I a M. Slater (age) 111, and Miss G.irtrude Stater 81. The tst part of the programme will consitit of #t, polo match' between Barhiw's team and " Fair " Till. ner'a team. In the evtming there will be another pi. , match between Warrington and SC. Helens,</p>
        <p>Al</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>A: UNIVERSAL BOON.</head>
        <p>A: UNIVERSAL BOON.</p>
        <p>It is seldom ths.t any discovery of science Is such as will benefit all classes of people. Some of the greatest discoveries of the age, while proving beneficial to thousands, have also at least for a time, injured ether thousands. By n some of the best m-dicinal preparations, however, much they may do to remove one clAss of disease or suit some constitutions, at the same time either deepen the hid of another disease, or leave it entirely untouched, or, if they benefit one class of patients they may injure another. Now,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>GREAT NEED OF THE AGE.</head>
        <p>THE GREAT NEED OF THE AGE.</p>
        <p>Is some scientifically compounded preparation which will effectually cope with the prevalent diseases of this country, which will be certain to do good when fairly tried, which will be equally adapted to the nreds of the merchant, the profes- sional man, and he who wins his bread by the sweat of his brow, the clerk, the factory hand, the miner, the workers in metals, and the roadside labourers. It sh,uld also be such a preparation as contains no injurious ingredients, and which may be taken with impunity by the weak and sickly child or the delicate female, as well as by the stronger constituted man. Such a preparation would indeed be</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>BOON TO HUMANITY.</head>
        <p>A BOON TO HUMANITY.</p>
        <p>After long-continued and diligent research, this much- I needed boon has been found, and repeatedly tested by analysts, who declare it to be pure and harmless. This desirable preparation is that wellknown Vegetable Tonic sailed Gwilym Evaa,' Quinine Bitters, and it has gained such a widespread reputation for efficacy that many unprejudiced doctors, wno have given it a fai: trial, frequently recommend it to their patients in cises of great Weakness, Nervousness, or any disorder of the digestive organs. Ovri:ym Evans' Quinine Bitters is so invariably efficacious and produces such satisfactory resullts, MIA when once tried It is always recomtnended. It has proved successful when all other medicines hadfailed o give relief. It has also in many cases been found to ba permarient'y beneficial when other preparations at best only give temporary relief. Gwilym Evans' Bitters is purely vegetable, and contains neither</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>MERCURY NOR IRON,</head>
        <p>MERCURY NOR IRON,</p>
        <p>or any poisonous or other injurious substances, and thou- sands who b.ive benefitted by its use agree In saying it is a specific for all symptoms of Indigestion, Nervousness. and Weakness. It is also confidently recommended for Low Spirits Liver Disorders, and Chest Affections. The unparalleled success of Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters has induced unprincipled persons to offer base and spurious imitations, somewhat similar in name and appearance, but possessing none of the virtues of this great national remedy. Remember that none are genuine unless the name Gwilym Evans is on !abel. stamp, and bottle. Sold by all Chemists, In bottles, at Is. lid., 2s. 9d. and 4s. 6d. each, or may be obtained, carriage free, direct from the Proprietors i—Quinine Bitters Manufacturing Company, Limited. L'anelly, South Wales.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>BEFORE TIM MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE TIM MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>ASSAULTING • TRAMP WOMAN.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Occasional Court, on Tuesday, be- fore Messrs. J. Tinsley and Edward Greenall, a youth aamed Martin Burns, farmer's labourer, was charged with assaulting a tramp named Emma Perks,on Monday night, at half-past eleven in Thelwall-lane.—From the evidence it appeared that the prisoner followed the woman ?arks and a female companion along Thelwall-lane, and there assaulted her. The attention of a Warnngton police- man was called by the woman's cries for assistance, and he arrested the prisoner.—Fined 203. including costs. SAD TALE OF NEGLECT Eli A MOTHER.</p>
        <p>At Warrington Borough Court on Monday, Mary Tur- ner, 68, St. Mary's-st., was charged with being drunk in Friar's Green on Sunday morning. P.C. Turner said that he saw the prisoner drunk and requested her to go home. He saw her later on, and as she was still wan- dering about, he took her to the police station. Alico Lees said that on Sunday morning shewas standing at her door,and saw prisoner's little boy of two and a half years running about naked. Her baby was lying on a rug in the house, in a pool of water. They had had no break- fast, and she and several neighbours gave them .me food. The husband worked at Runcorn. P.c. Evans confirmed the last witness's statement. Air. Taylor, P.C.M., said that drink was the cause the deplorable condition of prisoner. The magisNates dismissed the c.ase on the prisoner promising tJ go to a home in Liver- pool for six months. Cragoi3 or STEALING A WATCIL At the Warringr.a County Petty Sessions on Wednes- day, Frederic', uramhall was charged with stealing a silver wstai value Ll, the property of Hannah Concan- non. • widow, residing at the old cottages, Rixton. The p,mplainant said she saw the watch safe on the 9th inst. It was hung over the mantelpiece, but she missed it the following day.—Frances Concannon,the daughter of the complainant, said she left the kitchen to go up- stairs, and on coming down a few minutes later she missed the watch.—Mr. P. Kelly, manager for Mr. G. Jackson, pawnbroker, Winwick-st., said the prisoner came to the shop and offered the watch for sale. He gave him ss. for it. He gave the name of Henry Mars- den, and said the watch belonged to him. Prisoner was apprehended by P.C. Pope, and when charged with the theft said he bought it off a man in (he lane. Ile had been employed at Rixto:a for about three months. The roagistrates considering this was his first offence, and as be had been locked up a week, treated the case under the first offenders act, and bound him over to come up when called upon, and ordered the costs amounting to 324. 6d., be paid. ASSLEILT ON A DATIGETEIL -</p>
        <p>John Harrison, 50, Bostock-st., was brought before the magistrates at the Warrington Police Court, on Friday, on the charge of assaulting and beating Harriet Harrison, 59 Bostock-st., on the 15:h inst. Mr. Arthur Browne appeared for the defendant.—Harriet Harrison, said that on the afternoon of the 15th inst. she was com- ing home by the back way, when she met her father. He ran at her with his closed 111, and struck her in the face, lie said that was for hitting her mother. Ile then thumped her again, and knocked her head against a lurry which was near by. A young man came up and prevented her from falling to the ground. She was bleeding from the mouth. 0" had not lived 7;4i:a hor -pro btaor two years, but lived with her married sister. Some time ago her mother had given her a gold ring, and a fortnight before the alleged assault, she came to her sister's house for it, in a temper and lifted a brush at her. Witness never gave her mother a black eye. Immediately after her mother left, witncss broke the gold ring, and straightened the pieces with a hammer, and threw them into her mother's house. Her father threatened to thrash her the next time he met her for giving her mother a black eye.—Mrs. Handles, the married sister, corroborated, and also another witness named Randles.—Mr. Browne said that he would not deny that his client had struck his daughter though not to the extent that she said. He contended that though he was not legally justified he was morally justified in thrashing his daughter for giving her mother a black eye. His client would consent to pay the costs if his daughter would withdraw the case, and on the advice of the magistrates, this course was adopted and the case was dismissed. CHARGE AGAINST A rict DEALER.</p>
        <p>Andrew Quinn, was charged on Wednesday, at the Warrington County Sessions, before Captain Withington (in the chair), and Mr. James Tinsley, with causing two pigs to be removed from Warrington to Poulton, with- out first having obtained the necessary license from the police, authorising him to do so. Mr. W. H. J. Jenkins. solicitor, protecuted on behalf of the police, and Mr. Arthur Browne defended. Mr. Jenkins said the defend- ant was a dealer in pigs and carried on a large business, and attended the Warrington Market every Wednesday. On Wednesday the 3rd May, Mr. Quinn sold two pigs to Mr. Houghton, farmer, Woolston. Quinn and the farmer afterwards engaged a driver, and told him to drive the pigs into a road near the station at Padgate, and then apply at the sergeant's house there for an order. Mr. Jenkins contended it was useless making out this order when the pigs had already been taken from Warrington market without first taking the the order. It was done to save time.—Charles Jackson, labourer, Warrington, deposed to receiving orders from Mr. Quinn and the farmer, to take the pigs to Padgate, and leave them in a lane, until he had got a declaration order from the police. He also admitted being paid by the farmer. After evidence by Sergeant Barnes and I%C. Pope, Mr. A. Browne contended that no case had been made out against his client. The driver had been engaged by Mr. Houghton, the usual declara- tion was made by Mr. Quinn, and the driver was paid by Mr. Houghton, and therefore the driver was not a servant of Mr. Quinn. He also urged that it was a great trouble for dealers to obtain a license, and sometimes they lost several hours in getting one. It waa customary in many towns to have an officer of police attending pig markets in order to give the purchasers the necessary license. The Bench dismissed the case, and asked the Superintendent of police to inform the county authori- ties that the Bench were desirous that a police officer should attend the market to give the necessary license.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>FATURDAY.—Before Mr. John Burgess (in the chair) and Mr. L. Voisey. A DRIINHEN DRlVER.—William Lomas was chai,md with being drunk in charge of a horse and cart in linas- ford-road.—He was fined ss. and costs, or seven days. DRUNK AND DISORDEIILY.—Mary Gibbons was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Orostield-st., on the night previous, about twelve o'clock.--She was fined ss. and costs. AIONDAY.—Bef ore the Mayor (chairman), Aid. Harrison, Messrs. V. Monks, J. W. Hayward, and S.Wright. Bsoomo.—Tvro boys,John N iMagFuairrkeear.nsdt. J, oohnis sfuonstdyany, n were charged with begginit. afternoon. Inspector Uphill, N.5.P.C.0., said that the boys were going from house to house in Parker-st. on Sunday, and called at his house for something to eat. One of them said that he had been sent out by his i parents for food. The parents, who were n court, denied this, and the case,on the application of Inspecto; Uphill, was remanded for a week. DESUATER.—Thomas Trelfa was charged with being a deserter from the South Lancashire Militia, and also with making a wilful false answer in making an attestation, 1 aving said that he had not been sent to prison by a</p>
        <p>-</p>
        <p>civil power. P.C. Phillips proved the case, and prisoner was fined 40s. and costs, or one mouth for the first offence, and two months hard labour for the second. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—CaroIine Bowen, or Tan- ner, 23, Stamford-st.,dismissed on promising to amend ; Wm. Warburton, 378, Winwick-road, ss. and costs, or seven days' imprisonment. ASSAULT ON TRE POLICE.—John Carroll, 23, Rank-st., was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Butter- market-st., and with assaulting P.C.'s Turner and Cun- liffe, on Saturday night. This being prisoner's first offence, he was fined ss. and costs, or seven days. CAanietaviNo.—For this offence in Watkin-st., on May Bth, which was proved by P.C. Clough, Daniel Crook was fined ss. and James Potts 2s. ed. UNJUST CALES.—Elamblett Jackson, Selby-street, was charged with having a weighing machine unjust, on the sth inst.—The magistrates considered the case a rather trivial one, and ordered defendant to pay the costs. TUESDAY.—Mr. R. Garnett (chairman), and Dr. Starkey</p>
        <p>_ Smith. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—James Farrell, 234, Win- wick-road, discharged on promising to reform; Mary Whitfield, last from Hull, discharged on proinhing to leave the town. SCHOOL CASES.—Michael Dempsey, Fennel-street, 53. ; John Fiddler, School Brow, 53. ; Samuel Hiles, 3, Quay Fold, 53. ; Joseph Ireland, School Brow, adjourned ; John Jones, Bewsey-road, 2s. 6d. ; John MacDonald, Bank- stieet, 14th appearance, 2s. 6d. ; Arthur Miles Bank 2 Quay, 2s. 6d. ; Richard Newton, School Brow, 3. 6J.; Thomas Lusly, Fennel-street, 23. 6d. ; Samuel Becket, Silver Square, 2s. 6d. ; Mr. G2orge Riley prosecuted. FnmaY.=Before Alderman Harrison (chairman), Coun- ....</p>
        <p>cillor Roberts, Messrs. J. White and W. Pier Point. Dons WITHOUT LICENSES.—For keeping a dog without a license, Richard Shaw, 2, Union-st., was fined 4.5. in- cluding costs, and James Toole, 8, Foundry-st., 253. and costs.</p>
        <p>A QUEER ASSAULT.—Joseph Pilling, Winwick-st., was summoned by Peter Robinson.horse slaughterer,Church- st., for assaulting him en the 10th inst. Peter Robin- son said that he appeared as a witness against defend- ant about five years ago. Since that time, defendant had shouted after him every time he passed him. On the 10th inst. witness was speaking to a friend in the Market Place when defendant shouted out that he was telling lies. Beyond this defendant did nothing to him, but it was very hard that a man should shout after him every time he passed him in the streets. The case was dismissed as no assault had been committed. ASSAULT.—Patrick Hagan, of Appleton, was charged with assaulting Dennis Noon, of Cuerdley, on the 13th Inst. Complainant said that he and defendant were in a public-house in Bridge-st., when defendant asked him to come into the yard. Complainant went out with him, when defendant without any warning, struck him a blow in the face, and knocked him down. Complainant did not strike back—Thomas Costello corroborated and defendant was fined ss. and costs or seven days impri- sonment.</p>
        <p>..... THE POKER DID IT.—Frederick Jos. Browning and Lily Browning, of Ship Yard, were charged with un- awfully assaulting and beating Harriet Edwards, Ship Yard, on the 15'11 inst. Mrs. Edwards stated that she was standing at her own door on Monday night when the defendants commenced to call her bad names. She retorted, and the male prisoner struck her on the cheek. —Alice Burns was called to support complainant's state- ment, and caused much amusement by her evidence. Among other things she said that " she had never seen defendant before this morning until last night," and "defendant was so drunk that he could not see the hoks through a ladder." The defendant said that some one in Ship Yard struck him in the face with a pJiter, and in trying to save himself he accidently caught com- plainant in the face.—John Massy and Mrs. Massey sup- ported this explanation,and the magistrates accepted it, and dismissed the case. ‘adering complainant to pay the c)sts. THREArs._Agocs Longshaw, Cambridge Place, was charged wRn using threats towards Ann Stafford of the samep:ace, and Ann Stafford and Jane Hogan were sum- moned for using threats towards Agues Longshaw on May 14. The cases were dismissed,each side to pay their own costs.—For using threats towards his wife on the 14th inst., John Atherton, 8, Heaton-st., was bound over to keep the peace for six months in one surety of £5, or 14 days' imprisonment in default.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>WARRINGTON COUNTY PETTY SESSIONS. WEDNESDAY.—Before Captain Withington, aud Mr.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON COUNTY PETTY SESSIONS. WEDNESDAY.—Before Captain Withington, aud Mr.</p>
        <p>James Tinsley. DRUNIE.—The following persons wera dealt with for this offence : Matthew Stewart, in Liverpool-road, Great Sankey, on the 7th inst., 53. and costs.—Thomas Childs, on the same date, at Penketh, was fined 23. fid. and costs.—Wm. Broadhurst, for the same offence at Sankey Bridges, NVarrington, on the sth May, was fined a similar amount.</p>
        <p>A CYCLIST FINED.—Ralph Houghton, was fined is. and costs, for riding a bicycle on the footpath in Cow- lane, Great Sankey, on the 4th inst. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—Benjamin Stokes was charged wih being drunk and disorderly on the 17th March, 1892, and also on the 2nd April, 1892. Prisoner pleaded guilty to the first offence but not guilty to the second. P.C. Dugdale said he was shouting and want- ing to fight a man named Wm. Brown who was fined ss. and costs for the first named offence, and o.' the 2nd of April he threatened to assault him. He was afterwards summoned but did not then appear. He was fined ss. and costs for the first offence and 2s. 6d. and costs for the second offence, or 21 days' imprisonment. ASSAULT.—Wm. Sutton was charged with assaulting Robert tilting ton on the Bth May. From the evidence of the complainant it appeared that he was going to his work at Sutton Colliery when the dcfeniant struck him and knocked him to the floor, and then commenced to kick, Healoo hit him in the face, The only reason for the defendant's conduct was that when the manager asked him where Sutton was, he told him he had seen him drunk and incapable for work. Thomas Barton also gave evidence as to the assault. The defendant admitted striking the complainant once, but also received a blow in return from him, and said thero Oa a quarrel be- tween them. He was finios. and costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>WARRINGTON OCCASIONAL COURT.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON OCCASIONAL COURT.</p>
        <p>THURSDAY.—Befere Mr. James Tinsley. CHABGE OF STEALINO A SPADF.—Oswald Drinkwater was charged with stealing a spade, value 4s 6d, the pro- perty of John Longshaw Jones, at Moore, on the 16th May. He was remanded to Daresbury Petty Sessions,to be held on the 30th inst.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Refore Mr. James llandley and Mr. G. L.</p>
        <p>Davies.</p>
        <p>DOG AT LARGE.—George Davidson, Halton, for allow- ing his dog to be at large in contravention of the Rabies Order, was fined 6d. and costs 9s. 61. DAMAGING GRAS3.—Roger Tickle and Arthur Har- greaves, labourers of Parkside, Ashton by Sutton, were charged with having on the seventh inst. wilfully com- mitted damage to the extent of 3s. by trampling down grass and breaking a fence, of a field belonging to Thos. Walker Challoner, farmer, of Aston Grange. Mr. EhaHomer said he had four footpaths across his fields and only one was level. The people from Parkside were con- stantly crossing his land to get to the publichouse, and did a great deal of damage. lie wanted a stop put to the tiespassing and did not press the case against the defendants. Each defendant was ordered to pay us.</p>
        <p>SCHOOL BOARD CASES.—A number of persons were summoned for neglecting to send their children regularly to school and the usual fine of ss. was imposed in each case.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>WEAT HE It.</head>
        <p>THE WEAT HE It.</p>
        <p>EXTRAORDINARY RAINFALL IN AMERICA.</p>
        <p>NEW YORK, THURSDAY. —The extraordinary rainfall which commenced on Saturday last, over a wide area of the country has now ceased, but the results are in many districts simply appalling, and the amount of the loss in produce, damage to general property, and disturbance of trade, may be said to be beyond calculation. So exten- sively have a number of railway tracks been washed out that even if the floods had already disappeared it would be impossible to repair the damages sufficiently to allow of the resumption of the traffic for several days. , So overcharged have the river and canal, which practically surround Cleveland in Ohio, become, that, with the exception of a small part of the western district, the city is cut off from or- dinary communication with the outer world, and it has becn ascertained that much misery is resulting from the inundated condition of thousands of dwellings. The quantity of flood waters pouring into Lake Eric and the other lakes is so vast and causing such turbulent condi- tions as to bring shipping and general traffic on the lakes to a standstill. It will, therefore, be readily understood that the damage in the States of Pennsylvania, Ohio,and New York is estimated by millions of dollars. Happily the loss of life has not been so serious as was reported, only five persons being drowned by the capsizing of the tugboat which was swept into Lake Eric by the torrent in Conneant Creek, while at Cleveland only four mem- bers of the lifeboat crew which went to the rescue of two men were carried away and drowned. A SCHOOLHOUSE STRUCK BY LIGHTNING. During the heavy storms which burst over Luton ena Wednesday a chimney at St. Matthew's schools was struck by lightning. A large quantity of brickwork fell into the playground and through the roof, and it has been found necessary to close the schools for a week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>EXCITING SCENE AT A TRADES UNION</head>
        <p>EXCITING SCENE AT A TRADES UNION</p>
        <p>MEETING.</p>
        <p>A HOSTILE SPEAKER BEATEN.</p>
        <p>On Thursday, a scene of great excitement occurred in Blackburn Market-place, where a great meeting of trades unionists assembled to protest against the action of some local corn millers in discharging their workmen. A. man named Blackshaw ascended the platform, and at- tacked the trades unionists. Immediately a great tumult arose, and, in spite of the appeals of the unionist leaders, the spectators dashed on to the platform and thrcw Blackshaw violently to the ground. There he was set upon by the crowd, who beat him and drove him off the market and through the streets of the town. Eventually he escaped his persecutors.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 20, 1893.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 20, 1893.</p>
        <p>BOWLING.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGVE.</p>
        <p>HORSE AND JOCKEY v. RED LioN.—A match between the Horse and Jockey and the Red Lion Clubs took place on Saturday. with the following result }WYSE AND JOCKEY. RED LION. Joseph Holbrook Thomas Broadhurst ... 14— 29 John Hesketh ls— 23 Henry Ball l3 42 Fred Broady Henry Atherton Peter Wells (eapt.) ... 15— 72 Henry Broady l2— 55 George Hlgham ls— 87 Wm. Mclntyre William Broadhurst... 15-102 W Rathbone - 68</p>
        <p>G. C Curry Walter lligharn 9-124 Albert Broady.., ...... 15— 98 Thomas Atherton l5-139 Thomas Broady l4-112 Peers Brimelow l5-154 J Gregory 6-118 Thomas Wells</p>
        <p>Total</p>
        <p>Total</p>
        <p>LEGIC CHALLENGE COMPETITION.—The lint league match in connection with the Legh Challenge Cup Corn petition was played on Saturday between Newton-le- Willows Nursery and Park Lane Village Clubs, and re- sulted in a win for the former by 27 points on the two games. The following are the scores on each green. At Newton-le-Willows :</p>
        <p>NRWTEIN. PARK LANE. W Kent R Turner W OwPn 2l 11 Wtight J McDonald l9 C Rimmer 2l G Rylance 2l T Shaw J J Randal T Gee 2l 3 Robinson 23 J Chapman</p>
        <p>1 At Park Lane. 1 NEWTON. PARK LANE. Jos. Aitken D Ilylance 2l J Smalley If lloicroft 2l. W White l2 J Cameron 2l S BiHinge F Singleton K Kelly 9 W Walkden 2l F Foary l4 J Wittingham J Williamson</p>
        <p>124 At Newton 159 -- 283 256</p>
