      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>CRICKET.</head>
        <p>CRICKET.</p>
        <p>"„'• Secretaries of Cric .et Clubs are requested to note Mai reports of matches played on Saturday must be sent to the head office, Bewsey-street. Warrington, not later than ten o'clock on Wednesday morning.</p>
        <p>CRICKETERS. Lawn Tennisers, and Golfers are reminded that GEO. G. Buss 1. &amp; Co. manufacture high-class requisites. Their new catalogue is obtainable from Local Dealers, or the Sports Manutactory, Peckham Rye, S.E. Trade (CCD(C Mark.</p>
        <p>GRAPPENEALL ST. WILFRED'S 2ND V. SANKEY WES- LEYANS 2ND.—This match was played at Sankey on Saturday, and resulted in a win for Grappcnhall by 40 runs to 38.</p>
        <p>ORFORDRECREATION V. CEMETERY WRITE ROSE.—This match was played on Saturday, on the ground of the latter, and resulted in a win for the visitors. Score : Orford Recreation, 47 ; Cemetery White Rose, 42.</p>
        <p>WESTON v. CHESTER ST. MARY'S.—This match was played on Saturday at Chester in favourable weather, and resulted in an easy victory for the visitors. Score : —St. Mary's, 39 ; Weston, 165: •</p>
        <p>RUNCORN BETHESDA GUILD V. LOSTOCK.—This match was played on the Guild ground on Saturday. For the home team S. Houghton, the captain, took seven wickets for 19 runs. Score i—Bethesda, 69 ; Lostock, 55. BUCKLEY-ST. SUNDAY SCHOOL V. ASHCROFT'S TEAM—This match was played on the ground of the former on Satur- day, and resulted in a win for Buckley-st., by 95 runs to 54. Excellent scoring was done by Baker for Buckley- st., whilst Leech did well for Ashcroft's team.</p>
        <p>BETHESDA GUILD 2ND V. RUNCORN WHITE ROSE.— Played on the ground of the latter, and resulted in an easy victory for the visitors, tor whom Owen took six wickets for seven runs. Score :—Runcorn, 35 ; White Rose, 19. BRICK-STREET AND FRIARS GREEN S.S.C.C. IST V. ORFORD BARRACKS C.C.—Played on the ground of the latter and resulted in a win for the visiting team after a very exciting game. Scores :—Brick-street, 38; Orford Barracks, 28. SANKEY BRIDGE WESLEYANS JUNIORS V. BANK QUAY MISSION.—This match was played at Mill-lane, and resulted in a win for Sankey Bridges by 39 runs to 33. J. Dunn batted well, and Welding and Wright bowled well for Sankey Bridges. GOLBORNE-ST. BAPTIST V. ORFORD CRESCENT.—This match was played on the ground of the former on Sat- urday, and resulted in a win for the Baptists by four rune. It was a very evenly contested game in every respect. Ball scored 27 for Orford Crescent, and Woods " knocked" 18 for the Baptists. BEWSEY-ROAD WESLEYANS V. SELECTED TEAM.—This match was played on the ground of the former, and ended in any easy win for the selected team by 97 runs to 35. There was a very poor show of scoring amongst the Wesleyans, their highest being seven, whilst Gibson' for the selected team ran 32, Grimshaw 16, and Reynolds 14. BANK-ST. MISSION V. WIDNES RED R^SE.—A match was played between the above clubs on Saturday,at Widnes, and resulted in a win for Bank-st. by 152 runs to 60. J. Duckworth batted in a grand style for 68. A.Duckworth T. Hibbert and J.Beardsall at,' did well with the bat for the Mission, and W. udgue batted well for the Red Rose. .</p>
        <p>..-.,,. WA lIRINCIToN V. LEYLAND.---This game which was played the ground of the Leyland club, resulted in a very decisive victory for Warrington. Mr.S.M.Crosfield was absent from the Warrington ranks, as were also the two Tinsley's from the Leyland eleven. Leyland's eleven wickets fell for 56 chiefly owing to the excellent bowling of Sugg and Smith. J. J. Crosfield and H. Reynolds opened Warrington's account,and though boundary hits were scarce, the first quarter of an hour's play saw 20 hoisted. At 23 Cresfield was dismissed and Smith came in. The latter hit each bowler repeatedly to the bound- ary. At 44 Reynolds returned one tamely to mid on, and gave way for G. Clarke. Smith and the last corner offered a stubborn resistance and forty minntes' play sufficed for the Leyland score to be passed. Smith was , indeed unmerciful, and although Tongue stayed the run getting at one end, the Warrington captain turned his attention to Silcock and Wood, driving them repeatedly for four. G. Clarke, however, played the patient game, and at 110 he returned one to Wood, who effected a capital catch, the partnership having realised 66 runs. Clark joined Smith, and thc score again rose rapidly. However, at 131 Smith stepped out to Nash and fell a victim to Pike's smartness. His score of 68 runs was made up of eleven fours, one three, five twos, and nine singles. Bell opened his account in a sensatonal man- ner, for after hitting Nash and Hill to the boundary, he lifted both bowlers clean out of the ground for six. Some' fine hitting was seen when Voisey and Poclmore were to- gether, these two adding 59 runs in 25 minutes. The innings closed for 233, Sugg not being able to bat. Score and bowling analysis :</p>
