      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>IM.PERIALD PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>IM.PERIALD PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Monday evening Sir W. flAncounT, answering a question, said that spirit dealers' retail licenses to sell in any quantity not less than one reputed quart bottle would not come within the Local Veto Bill, and the Government did not intend to inter- fere with these licenses.</p>
        <p>Mr. ASQUITH informed Mr. Saunders that, exclusive of Lancashire, thirty-six working men had been appointed justices of the peace in the United Kingdom since July last, and Mr. Bryce added that the number of working- men appointed to the borough benches in Lancashire since August was 33. The House again went into Committee on the Home Rule Bill, and resumed consideration of clause 2, to which Mr. Cavendish had moved an amendment provid- ing that the powers granted to the proposed legislature should be specifically defined. Mr. Chamberlain spoke in support of the amendment, and Mr. Glad:tone against it. On a division, the amendment was rejected by 275 to 228. A number of other amendments were discussed and thrown out.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>TUESDAY.</head>
        <p>TUESDAY.</p>
        <p>_ In the House of Lords on the motion of Lord Hood of Avalon that a further scheme for increasing the strength of the navy should be prepared, considerable discussion took place on the importance of protecting our com- merce, particularly in time of war. Lord Cross moved for a copy of the memorandum made in 1870 in the office of the Chancellor of the Duchy of Lancaster with regard to the appointment of county magistrates.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Tuesday evening, reply- ing to a question as to whether advances under the Irish Land Act of 1891 would still go on,and from what funds, after the passing of the Home Rule Bill, Mr. GLADSTONE said the Government intended that there should be no interference with proceedings under the Land Act. The House again went into Committee on the Home Rule Bill. Mr. Broderick moved an amendment to clause 2 to pi ovide that Her Majesty might, upon the Address to both Houses of the Imperial Parliament, "diminish or restrain the whole or any part of the powers therein granted to the Irish Legislature." Mr. GLADSTONE Strongly opposed the amendment. Mr. BALFOUR sug- gested, in view of the ignorance under which the House laboured as to the resolutions of the Government on the ' question of the retention of the Irish members, that Mr. Broderick should defer any final decision on the amend- ment to a later stage of the bill. Sir E. J. REED, while holding that means should be found for " correcting " objectionable measures of the Irish Legislature, said he should vote against the amendment. The Committee would not allow the withdrawal of the amendment, which on a division was defeated by 303 to 247 ; majo- rity for the Government 56. Sir H. James moved an, amendment adding to the clause wards providing that ,1 the supreme power and authority of the Parliament of the United Kingdom should remain undiminished over all persons and matters within the Queen's dominions. Mr. Gladstone offered to accept the substance of the amend- ment if it was put in the form of a new clause. After some discussion the amendment was agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>WEDNESDAY.</head>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.</p>
        <p>The House of Commons went into Committee again on Wednesday en the Home Rule Bill. 'rhe first question put was that clause 2 as amended should stand apart of the bill. It grants to the Irish Legislature power to make laws for the peace and good government of Ire- land. After a long debate Mr. Fowler pointed out that with autonomy there would be effective supremacy of the Imperial Parliament, but if the House was to be a court of appeal 30 every Irish measure their "last state would be worse than the first." That was not the supremacy advocated by members on the Ministerial side, and there was no machinery in the bill for it. The closure was carried by a majority of 61, and the House then divided on the motion that the clause should stand part of the bill. There were—for the clause,2B7 ; against 225 majority for the Government, 62. An interesting dis- cussion arose on Mr. Goschen's motion to report progress, and it was not devoid of amusing incident. The debate was adjourned. During the sitting Mr. Gladstone stated that the vote on account would be taken on Monday week. The House adjourned at ten minutes to six o'clock.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>THURSDAY.</head>
        <p>THURSDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Thursday,Mr. MUNDELL&amp; replying to Mr. Wm. SMITH, who alleges that the Furness Hallway Company's charges for carrying agricultural produce was 50, 60, and even 100 per cent. above the rates current last December, said he had communicated with the railway company, but had not yet received their reply. _</p>
        <p>Mr. isclorTn replied to Colonel SANDYS regarding an alleged Catholic outrage on a young woman named Mary Breen.</p>
        <p>Mr. MACAIITNISY gave notice on behalf of the hon. member for South Tyrone, that on the vote on account he should call attention to the lawless condition of the counties of Clare, Kerry and Limerick, and move to re- duce the salary of the Chief Secretary. The House went into Committee on the Treasury Chest Fund Bill, and after discussion the Bill passed through Committee. The House went into Committee on the Civil Service Estimates.</p>
        <p>In the House of Lords, on Thursday, the Railway Servants (Hours of Labour) Bill passed through Com- mittee. A discussion took place, on the initiation of the Earl of Meath, as to the finding of employment in Government departments for discharged soldiers. Lord SANDHURST said the matter had passed out of the hands of the War Office, but he was sure that the Secretary for War fully sympathised with the object in view, and would do anything in his power to further it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>WIDNES SCHOOL BOARD.</head>
        <p>WIDNES SCHOOL BOARD.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Widnes School Board was held in the Town Hall on Tuesday after- noon, when there were present: Mr. 11. Wade Deacon (in the chair), Revs. Dean Finegan, J. Clarke, and Messrs. R. Shaw, S. Quinn, C. F. Flynn, B. Brown, Dr. O'Keeffe, and Mr. G. H. Danby (clerk).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>DISTRICT EDUCATION COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>DISTRICT EDUCATION COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>This Committee reported that during the month of April the number of cases before the Committee was as follows :—Applications for free places, 31; for contra- vention of Education Act, 217 ; a number of cases before the magistrates, 40; number of conviction!, 39 ; with- drawn, 1. In proposing the adoption of this report the Rev. Dean Finegan drew attention to what he consi- dered the bad attendance. There had been 217 persons summoned before the magistrates for the past month against 189 the previous month. Ile made inquiries into it and was told that the bad attendance was owing to illness, to measles. He could not believe this was so as at their school the previous morning there were only 104 present whilst in the afternoon there were 144. Forty children absent at a time, and certainly not suffering from illness. Ile was very much dissatisfied with the state of affairs and thought that much of it was owing to the fact that the parents had been taught to know how often they might keep their chil- dren away and then avoid being taken to court. Most of the bad attendance occurred on a Monday when the School Attendance Officers were engaged all day at the Town Hall. He suggested that some arrangement should be made which would enable them to go out to their calls.—The Rev. Father Clarke pointed out that although more cases had been before the Bench that month the attendances had actually been better than ever. At the same time he did admit that the attend- ances were bad.—The Chairman supported Father Clarke and said that the attendance for the year was 864, the previous year MO, in 1883 it was 810, in 1882 it was 850, in 1881 it was 83.0 and in 1880 it was 84 0. He had been told that measles was affecting the schools at Appleton and was spreading to Farnworth.—Dr. O'Keeffe said that was not so.—Tha minutes were passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>FINANCE COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>FINANCE COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>In the accounts submitted for approval by this Com- mittee was one for El 10s. for the Timmis Fund Com- mittee. It consisted of.two months' grants of £1 10s. to John Hughes, William Ashley, William Hague, James redder, and John Hartland. The minutes els) contained a recommendation that in accordance with an invitation made by the Town Clerk, the Board should take a lease from the Corporation of their present offices. The minutes were adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>REV. U. H. GARRETT'S RESIGNATION.</head>
        <p>REV. U. H. GARRETT'S RESIGNATION.</p>
        <p>The Clerk read the following letter from the Rev. G. H. Garrett :—Dear Mr. Deacon,—As I am leaving Wid- nes, I beg to place in your hands my resignation of my position as member of the Widnes School Board. Ido so with regret as I fouLd the duties very interesting and I greatly valued the opportunities afforded of working with men of different schools of thought from my own. Wishing the Board every .11:ccess in its great and impor- tant work, I remain yours very truly, G. 11. Garrett." The resignation was accepted with regret by the Board. Mr. Brown asked whether the Board always returned for a vacancy, a man of the same party as the one who had caused the vacancy.—Several members : Yes of course. —Mr. Brown said he did not think it was so—he was sure it was not so, in fact.—Mr. Shaw said he did not think that that the Board always took the man who was recommended by the party.—After further considera- tion it was explained that several factions of a party often choose different candidates, but the Board always took the officially recognised member. This terminated the business,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>AN EXTRAORDINARY FAMILY.</head>
        <p>AN EXTRAORDINARY FAMILY.</p>
        <p>THIRTY CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>A woman was buried at Kirkham, on Thursday, who died through being confined of her thirtieth child. She was the wife of a local farmer, and had only been married twenty years. The woman had twins six times. Of the thirty children only six are now living. Both husband and wife were of immense proportions, the woman weighing over twenty stone.</p>
        <p>RUPTURE CURED.—S, J. SIIERMAN, Hernia Specialist, 64, Chancery-lane, London, sends his book on ruptnre post free. Branch Chambers, 26, King-st , Manchester.-_-4t.</p>
        <p>DOWN IN A YA.CLIT.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>QUOITS.</head>
        <p>QUOITS.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON LONDON-NORTH-WESTERN V. SEICOMBH. —Played on Saturday. Score :</p>
        <p>- - WARRINGTON. SEACOMBE. J noughton (Cal t.) 25 W T Brown (Capt.) l4 J Brice T Davies 25 It Edwards l5 J Pewter l5 d Preston J Taylor A Hamblett l7 C Arlett J Kinsey 25 T Mason 24 W Burke l3 D Williams J Bate 4 D Morris 25 P Brown 25 B Walten T Wall 25 W G Brown 0 If Ashbrooke 29 W Tarlton II Gardener H Wall</p>
