      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS AND MEDICAL FEES.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS AND MEDICAL FEES.</p>
        <p>At the meeting of the Warrington Board of Guar- dians held on Wednesdaydar.G.Reteliffe in the cI air, Mr.SuAw drew attention to an account from Dr.Wat- kins,of Newton. It was for £9 as fees for attendance upon kirth cases. He believed this was considerably in ex- cess of the general price. In the course of a lengthy discus- sion it was stated by the Clerk that there had been five cases. The Board allowed £2 in cases where there were exceptional difficulties to be mot, and £1 in ordinary cases. Dr. Watkins according to his account, which had been passed by the Committee at Newton had had four out of the five, exceptionally difficult cases. The accounts were signed by Mr. Balbarry, the chairman of the Committee, and he assumed that the Committee had given their consent after assuring themselves of the genuineness of the claitn.—Mr. MEIN said it seemed a very strange thing that four out of the five cases should be special—Several members asked if the committee had investigated the cases before the accounts were signed, and Mr. Ratcliffe said they had not had time ; the account only came in the day of their meeting, and they took it that it was correct.—Mr. A.J. JOLLEY : Were any objections raised :—Mr.CLanan : We were not asked to object to it. —The CLERK, in a further discussion as to who should report upon the genuineness of the cases, said that the Relieving Officer should.—Mr. RATCLIFFE said they did not like to ask him, as it would show a want of confi- dence in their doctor, and bring the two officials to loggerheads probably.—A MEMBER : This is a queer state ef affairs. Are the members of the Newton Com- mittee satisfied themselves ? (" No.")—Mr. JOLLEY proposed, and Mr. J. HOUGFITON seconded, that the ac- counts be rererred back to the Committee.—Mr. HEATON moved an amendment which was seconded by Mr. DAVIES.—The amendment was lost. AVAUNT PROTECTION!</p>
        <p>Mention of San Francisco recalls the fact that iron and steel manufacturers on the western coast of America cannot even attain the moderate amount of success which has of late been the lot of their eastern colleagues; so that it is not surprising to hear that there is a strong feeling in trade circles in San Francisco and district in favour of admitting free such crude articles as iron-ore coal, coke,pig-iron,scrap-iron, and scrap-steel. In fact,a petition embodying these sentiments and signed by 160 iron and steel firms on the littoral mentioned has been addressed to the Chamber of Commerce. This entreaty, in the coursa of its observations, remarks that trade is in a deplorable condition; that, owing to the poor quality of local coal, manufacturers are compelled to import fuel for smelting purposes, a special tax of 75 cents per ton being thereby imposed upon manufactured iron, coal interests at the same time not being bene- fited; and that they are obliged to purchase abroad large quantities of scrap. Whatever may be thought of Major McKinley in Ohio it is manifest that protection- ism, at least in regard of raw iron and steel products, is not looked at in a very favourable light in the Golden West.—lron.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>--•-. THE OYSTER TRADE.</head>
        <p>--•-. THE OYSTER TRADE.</p>
        <p>In the course of evidence on Thursday before the Sea Fisheries Committee of the House of Commons, Mr. Petrie, representing the American oyster trade at Liver- pool, stated that 130 million oysters are received at that port annually from America, and that 100 million of them are relaid on the beds at Fleetwood, Sutton, Cork Harbour, and Sligo. That they improve greatly by this process was shown by his comparing American oysters which had been some time on beds at Carlingford with some East River oysters just brought over by the Cam- phoia. He had never known the American oyster to spawn in British waters. Forty million of them were sold during June and July at a value, as sold to the public, of £150,000. Tho prime cost was 265. for 1,300. They were packed very close in boxes, without any moisture, but with holes for ventilation, aad were food- less for about three weeks. The loss was about 15 per cent. He should not like to see any close time, which would prohibit this American oyster trade.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>VOYAGE ON THE MERSEY.</head>
        <p>A VOYAGE ON THE MERSEY.</p>
        <p>BY LTJKE BLUNT.</p>
        <p>If any yachtsman detects me going on " the wrong tack," in nautical points. I hope he won't heed it. There was only one man out of the four in the yacht who knew anything about sailing a boat, when we pushed into mid-stream. It was Saturday one Whit week. Our intention was to run down to the mouth of the river and cruise about there two days and then come back. There was only a slight fair wind blowing, and Benzen, who was the one who could sail a boat, managed everything with- out much difficulty for we dropped quietly down with the ebb, in long easy tacks. It was a close, dull day ; the sun had never been seen since morn- ing, and when we had rounded the bend at Monks Hall and Co's. Works, it seemed as though we had left all life behind. We slipped along quietly past the green fields o'er the banks, hearing nothing but the " mawing " of the browsing cattle, the songs of the birds, and Williamson's bubbling stream of poetical eulogies of the charms of quiet nature. Fiddlers Ferry was reached,and the passing glimpse of a few boatmen was caught; then lost to us as we drifted by. It was a dreamy, lazy, soul-calming time for us, and we lay about and smoked and di,ln't trouble ourselves any more than four tramps would. At length Runcorn Bridge came in view, and soon we were dropping along past the smoke- bleaching towns of Widnes and Runcorn ; we slipped along under the great, giant bridge, passed the quays on each side, and then we came abreast of Weston Point. It was a stirring spectacle that awaited us. The heavy clouds in the west had broken and the full round, burnish coloured sun had burst forth. The river had suddenly opened out ; its broad waters here and there spotted with gold-grain like sand banks, sparkled in the glow of the sun. Away to the right was the small white- washed light-house of Halehead, bordere I and em- braced by the cluster of trees, and cottages, and dappled by the mellow rays of the golden sun, then to the left there stretched from the mouth of the Weaver a marsh, gradually undulating to a high wood, and then right away was the village of Eastham resting snugly in the cluster of tall trees. It wag a beautiful sight, and Williamson got quite enthusiastic and got dabbling about in some poetry about Sunset," which he repeated so often that it began to jar upun us. But we kept along ; the effects were getting so trying and cruel that we had to threaten to put him ashore if he didn't do what someone called "closing it." He did close it finally.</p>
        <p>A large sand bank lay opposite Eastham, and we steered into the channel which lay between it and '1 the village. Benzon ran as high and dry upon the sandy bank as possible ; the mainsail, foresail and jib were furled ; the anchor was carried on to the bank, for when the tide returned during the night it would float us. We lighted the stove and cooked tea, and with the fresh salt smell of the breeze in our nostrils we ate our viands with a relish and said to ourselves that we were Bohemians now; we could live along like that for ever we said. Then we light our pipes and walked over the sandy shore, to a mossy bank and chatted. The veil of evening was gradually closing upon us ; one by one the stars peeped forth; little by little the faint light in the adjacent wood dimmed into dusk ; away up the river anchored vessels began to hoist their night lamps, and then at last the moon had sailed right overhead, and thrown a shimmering silver light up- on the rippling water. It was night, quiet, and lonely. S 1 e adjourned to the boat to sleep ; but the approach of night had cast a melancholy shadow about us; and after Botizon bad detailed all the. terrible accidents he had heard of down the river, the melancholy became rather ghostly. Tickle- botham said we could get nicer beds at the hotel on shore, and Williamson said so, too, but we over- ruled them. Finally the hatches were battered down ; the stove re-fueled, the lamp put out, and there we all lay upon the cabin floor wrapped in our blankets.</p>
        <p>I couldn't seem te get to sleep anyhow, I heard a deep oppressive silence settle down amongst us ; I heard the silence have its monotony broken by a slow drawling more, which emanated from Benzon, and grew in strength as it went on ; and then I heard Ticklebotham break out in the same way. Up to then my nervousness had lead my brain to think and worry so that 1 thought my head would crack ; but now I had found a respite, a peace. I would referee the race which was going on between those snorers. I decided to let Benzon have the start as he dra ivied the most. This is how they went: Benzon, Tickleboth am : Ilaw-luvo." Off they went, I keeping count. They raced along up to 100, and now I could perceive that Ticklebotham's last snore was gaining ground upon Benzon. I tried to find how much, dropped the counting and after intently listening found I had mislead myself—there was no ground either lost or won. I gave up the task wearily and turned on my side. I fell to speculating about lots of things; then I got into a kind of stupor where my faculties wouldn't speculate any more but only keep on a painful repetition of the two snores " 11-a-w-aw-er," and "Haw-aw." Then these dropped out, and sleep overtook me. I could only have been in its embrace aboat three minutes, altho' it seemed hours, when I suddenly awoke quite oblivious of my whereabouts. Thought I was in bed and finding no pillow jumped up to get it. Bash ! My head had found the hatches above. I rubbed it till the pain went; then I got to pawing about with my leg to see where the land lay. Presently I struck something soft; I placed my foot on it gently and pushed, and then the features of a face seemed to impress themselves against my foot. So I withdrew. Then I pawed right out, but the next moment it was all I could do to surpress a howl ; my foot had been striking a warm friend- ship with the stove. I turned over and dosed a bit more, and then wakened with a start. , There were strange sounds abroad. A rapid, liquid " slip-slip-slip," was gently beating- against the boat ; and then there was a grating sound abou,-, the keel, as though the sand kept getting up and kicking. I found out after that it was the tide beginning to float us. But I knew not then, and as there was a low wailing, sobbing kind of wind loitering about the rigging I felt quite lonesome. I lay a bit and then my nerves were suddenly shat- tered by hearing at my elbow, " flew-hew I don's Oh I save me, Emily I " Then succeeded a series of hysterical sobs. I lay there and listen?d, and suff- ered for five long drawn minutes. Then I rose for a match. I lighted the lamp, and reflected it upon Williamson. Poor fellow I -Like the fitful gust of a worn out storm, so were the jerking sobs of his subsiding grief. Right down his cheeks, and glis- tening on his moustache was a profusion of tears. I softly wakened him up. He stared wildly around for a time, then said " Phew ! what an ugly dream I've had. Thought I was drowning."</p>
        <p>But it did seem a relief to have him waken ; and I kept him waken until the first cold streaks of dawn came Oro' a half open hatchway. Then we awoke the others. The tide had covered the bank and we were riding at anchor some distance from the shore. It was a grey dawn. The light looked grey and the water looked cold and grey ; the ves- sels about the river showed no sign ot life yet; they were just as they had been all night, except that their lamps like weary watchers as it were, were dimming and paling in the growing light, as tho' their work was done. It was chilly. We had breakfast. The ooffee was azalding hot and we couldn't drink it because of the "grounds" floating about. The kippered herrings ' were only half cooked, and we had to pick them with our fingers. No one had been used to cutting new bread, so we broke it in lumps and :dabbed them in a butter dish. After breakfast Benzon said we would go coaling. This is what all yachts from Warrington do when they get down there. They steer for the big bank which we were lying by, and in low water it is a very easy thing to col- lect a cwt. of coal on it. Thus the boats are kept in stove fuel. Well, after a considerable amount of ramshackling, and tieing and untieing ropes at</p>
        <p>Benzon's orders, we got sail set, and thou via, swept down to the bank—a quarter ot a mile 0' sand. We ran high and dry, and then pulled otir shoes and stockings off, and went scrambling ab°ll, in the wet, soft sand, after lumps of stranded cos" Presently, we saw Ticklebotham running at ft speed towards us from one end of the bank shou ing" Come along fellows—found a curiosity—oh a wonderful thing." He seemed so elated and eX' cited that we all set off at a run to the spot where the curiosity was. It was a round jelly lookif thing sucking water in like a sponge, in a puddl He danced round the puddle, and touched tl4e thin with a stick and leaped back, and shouted "Mind."</p>
        <p>"Is this the thing," said Benzon quietly " Yes. Howl we catch it. "Don't you know what a is." _</p>
        <p>" No. i bet its valuable in a museum." " That's just a jily fish, sir, that is," and be walked away with suppressed indignation.</p>
        <p>Well, we got a sack of coal in the bunk and .th.eo steered out into the channel. The wind was ri</p>
        <p>gradually; we were beginning to pitch slightly,e- Benzon had all he could do ,to manage the boat hiel" self, We cruised about until tide time, then th( sky became heavy, the wind gradually rose, rale began to fall, and the boat lurched and pitched worse than ever. Williamson was the first to giv,,e out; he went under the hatches with a bucket ac" a face almost yellew. In a bit Ticklebotham came anxious about Williamson—so anxious that his face became yellow; and he went under the hatches to see after his friend. Things were begin,: fling to look serious now. We were trying to ge' away towards Garton, but the waves were co, mencing to run over our decks, the current gaining strength, and we were beating up in a wind. The wind grew stronger and Benzon we should have to reef the foresail; he could leave the helm to do it; could I. There was no doubt about it, I said, with a heart down in my boots. I had a faint idea what it was to reef a sail and tear be I could have done it if the water had been calta and I had been able to reach the foresail witheo danger. But I assumed a calm exterior and got oe the deck stomach downwards. Benzon eyed ne,ie over; perhaps he thought it didn't mater if I A° drown myself. I set off like a crab, towards thAa bowsprit. A wave came dancing up ; I lay low an' held on to the hatches. Splash lit was all over lee and my hat had gone. I got a few more feet t wards the bow and then another wave leaped OV me. At length I reached the bow; but it Wag dreadful. One minute we were right in the air; the next in the trough of two waves; and the next I and the for'ard part of the boat Wer,,', plunging thro' a wave. I was never called a cower": but, I dareu't venture on the bow sprit to do theu reef. Benzon never even said "It's dangeroll come back." o I said it myself and commence' to claw my way back, like a dressed up lobster a bucket of water. When I got aft again he lookee calmly contemptuous and said, " Well, where's the reef ?' I said I had just found out that my boote let water in and I might catch cold if I went on tile bow sprit.</p>
        <p>Well, what did he do ? Ile just hissed," pow-waw," luffed the boat round ; let out his nal sheet, and commenced to run before the wind a,n with the current up towards Warrington. never exchanged civilities once ; he acted as 1 there was no one else in the boat; we drove PI Runcorn and Widnes with a swampy boat an plenty of rain and reached Warrington a day or two before we were expected, simply because, so ..!.'icklebotharn told our friends, that gooseberrY tart of which we had eaten had created a diarrhca; epidemic amongst us. He and W.illiamson had bee?, very ill thro' it, he said, and then he expressed 121' disappointment that the trip had not been contlf ued round Iloylake and the mouth of the Dee!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>EMPLOYERS' LIAPILITY BILL.</head>
        <p>THE EMPLOYERS' LIAPILITY BILL.</p>
        <p>AN IMPORTANT AMENDMENT.</p>
        <p>The Employers' Liability Bill was discussed in the House of Commons Committee on Law on Thursday, tt considerable length. Mr. Flaldana moved an amen ment declaring that knowledge of a risk should not r. regarded as implying an acceptance of it by the work men. The Home Secretary said the amendment v/oula but carry into effect the law as it had been laid doll° by the House of Lords, and it was agreed to withoa ; division. Mr. Woods moved to strike out sub-sectioa , of the first clause, by whch workpeople were excluda,`. from obtaining compensation if it could be shown On they knew of the negligence which caused the injury le failed to give notice to the employer within a reasonabro time. Mr. Asquith, Mr. Mather, and other !flamhle.. strongly supported the amendment, and the sub-sectio.. was struck out. A long and interesting debate then Cr lowed on Mr. Hunter's proposal to permit exceptions the rule laid down in the bill that workmen shalla "contract themselves out" of the measure. tli;s.' WLaren, Sir C. Dilke, Mr. Woods, and other mealbe,., spoke on the subject, and the opinions of railway se'g vants and others were quoted as to the advisableness ° permitting exceptions. The debatewas adjmraed on after the holidays.</p>
        <p>THE HULL STRIKE. The conference between the shipowners and docksrs Hull on Thursday, failed in its object, and the dispal remains unsettled. The men were adverse to three the Clauses, which set forth that the dockers show return to work as soon as places were vacant, that 01 should not molest any worker who had been emploYe, during the strike, and that the masters should reftls employment to anyone intimidating non-union men., ri was felt that by accepting these conditions the doeil_e,,d would be acknowledging that they had countenanun violence, and it was pointed out that the shipowners il; already power to dismiss or refuse employment to O.' docker intimidating a fellow-worker.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>INTERNATIONAL CONFERENCE OF MINERS.</head>
        <p>INTERNATIONAL CONFERENCE OF MINERS.</p>
        <p>raw It is announced that at the International Miners' gress, to be held at Brussels in Whitsun week, the Prire cipal resolution which will be moved by the gicief Federation of Great Britain will affirm the princiPle oro legal eight hours day from bank to bank, and wi110,11,6 pose that,if necessary, owing to Parliament refusin„ wt:c; legal eight hours day, a universal strike be resorted tb obtain the same. The federation will also propose female labour in and about mines be prohibited.</p>
        <p>WATCHING A WOMAN BURN TO DEATH. so A remarkable statement was made on ThursdaY a,%p. inquest held at Blackburn on a woman named Elic'Zi burned to death by the explosion of a lamp which -01 e, was about to throw at her husband. Police-conStai iry Richards stated that he found the woman envelope" d flames, and a crowd of neighbours standing round Ile staring at her, but making no effort to help her. of put out the flames with his greatcoat. A verdict. "Accidental death" was returned.</p>
        <p>REPORTED STRIKE OF WELS/i QUARRYMEN. • a rrle A strike is reported from ono of the Festiniog citi The action of a manager in dismissing a quarrYina; was resented by 400 men, who at once left the work. 7; EMBEZZLEMENT BY AN ODDFELLn W 0 SECRETARY, Daniel Davies, secretary of the Swansea branch a to Ocidfellows' Society, was reminded on ThursdaY, charge of embezzling about £4OO belonging to the 1°1.'5 The prisoner admits the charge. NEWTON CATTLb FAIR. The annual May cattle fair was held at NO" 1 ton on Wednesday. There was a fair show of c°:r: and horses but the qualities were rather below. • average. Prices also ranged low, and much. culty was experienced in obtaining for m"...,,t0 cows anything above £l. Very little basiness NYin. done in any class although there were a large her of dealers present. There was also a good el of pigs, but prices were too high for buyers.</p>
