      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <p>FRACAS WITH THE POLICE AT LATCHFORD.</p>
        <p>HEAVY SENTENCES ." A LESSON TO THE WHOLE TOWN."</p>
        <p>• At the Warrington Borough Court on Monday, ' William Bradbury and thoas Bradbury, 7 Old , Road, were charged with being drunk and dis- orderly on Saturday night and also with assaulting P.C.'s Leith wood, Newey, and Bruen and P.S. Smith in Old Road on the same date; John Dooley, 16 Old Road, was charged with assaulting P.C.'s Leithwood and Newey and with attempting to rescue the two first prisoners when in custady, at the same time and place; and Cornelius Ward, Old Road, was charged with assaulting P.C.'s Leith- wood and Bruen and P.S. Smith at the same time and place. The Chief Constable gave the facts of the case after which P.C. Leithwood said that he was at Bridge Foot on Saturday night when a young man told him that there was a fight in Ashton's yard. Witness went there, and saw Williarn Bradbury yo into Ashtoa's public-house. Witness remained outside and soon afterwards Bradbury came out of the front door, and commenced to fight with another man, not in custody. Witness separated them, when Thomas Bradbury came up and told him not to interfere. They went away, but when near the crossings, they commenced to fight again. When wit- ness tried to separate them, Thomas Bradbury pulkd him away. The two Bradburys then went into the Norton Arms' and witness followed and told the barman not to serve them as they were drunk. From the violent behaviour of Thomas, witness took him in custody, when William struck him on the head, and Thomas also struck him. P.C. Newey now came up and pulled Thomas away, and the two were brought outside the Norton Arms. William Bradbury then threw himself to the ground and took hold of P.C. Newey's legs, and that officer had great difficulty in getting them loose and P.S. Smith coming up said to Newey "You have no right to kick the man." The prisoner Dooley then kicked P.C. Newey and struck him behind the neck, and Ward also assaulted the officers. Witness never saw P.C. Newey kick any of the prisoners. P.C. Newey said that on being informed of the row in Old Road he went thither and saw P.C. Leithwood struggling with Thomas and William Bradbury, who had him in a corner, and were striking him "right and left." Witness made his way to them and took them in custody. P.S. Smith and P.C. Bruen then came up and ISmith accused him of kicking a prisoner but he (wit- ness) denied doing so. P.C. Bruen was obliged to use his staff. P.S. Smith said that on seeing the crowd of people in Old Road, he proceeded there, and saw the constables struggling with prisoners. Witness on going to their assistance received a blow in the mouth from Ward. He commenced using his stick and he received another blow from Thomas Bradbury. The officers had great diffi- culty in getting prisoners to the police station, and they were all under the influence of drink. He saw P.C. Newey kick William Bradbury in the ribs while he was on the floor, and told him he should report him. P.C. Bruen said that he went to the constables' assist- ance when informed, and received a blow in the back of the neck. He saw all the officers assaulted, and the prisoner Ward came with a poker, and gave witness a blow in the back. Witness drew his staff and struck Ward to make him put the poker away. He also made a way through the crowd with his staff. Ward was arrested in his house and the poker was also found there. George Billington and William Campbell gave evidence and Inspector Spinks said that although he had made inquiries in the neighbourhood of Old Road, he had received no complaints of illusage by the police. Several neighbours were called by the prisoners but did not throw any light upon the affair. The magistrates then retired and on their return the Mayor said that the magistrates were determined to have their police officers protected, and they were going to inflict a sentence which would be a lesson to the whole town of Warring- ton. William and Thomas Bradbury would each be fined 40s. and costs or a month, John Dooley 20s. and costs or a month, and Cornelius Ward for using the poker, would go to gaol for three months without the option of a fine.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>CRUELTY TO HORSES AT</head>
        <p>THE CRUELTY TO HORSES AT</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>APPEAL AGAINST THE SENTENCE.</p>
        <p>On Saturday, in the parade room, of the Borough Court, Mr. John Burgess, J.P., heard an appeal against the sentence passed on Edmond Collins, h trsedealer, the previous Saturday, for travelling horses in an unfit state. Mr. Horner, solicitor, Liverpool, said he applied for the liberation of Col- lins. lie was prepared to give substantial hail by sureties who had been approved of by the Liver- pool police, as responsible persons. The appeal was to be made at Liverpool Quarter Sessions on the nth July. Mr. Burgess said he might tell him that in consultation with Mr. Voisey, his colleague on the Bench, that they had arrived at the conclusion,that when the application was made for Collins' liberation, they would require. not two sureties, but £4O in cash paid into court. Mr. Homer replied that he had never been asked for that. They had come with two respect- able men as bail which was generally preferable, and he hoped they would be accepted. Mr. Burgess said Mr. Voisey had been in Court that morning, and they had come to the conclusion stated. He didn't feel at liberty to vary the decision, that they should require a cash deposit of £4O, for the liberation of Collins. The prisoner was again taken to the cells, but the money was afterwards paid to the magistrate's clerk, and Collins was liberated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>WAGES OF SHOP GIRLS. PLAIN SPEAKING BY MANCIIESTER MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>THE WAGES OF SHOP GIRLS. PLAIN SPEAKING BY MANCIIESTER MAGIS- TRATES.</p>
        <p>At Manchester Police Court on Monday, Ellen Grainger, was .charged on remand with having stolen a quantity of goods, consisting of gloves, laces, ribbons, &amp;c., the property of Messrs. Scarfpin and Co., Oldham-St., by whom she had been employ- ed as an aSsistant.—Mr. Armitage, the presiding magis- trate, asked the gentleman who represented the prosecu- tors, and who was their manager, if it was a fact that the prisoner only received 6s. per week.—The Manager : Yes, with the addition of from ss. to es. commission on the sales she made during the week. She came to us as a junior.—Mr. Armitage: When would she become a senior?—The Manager: After the lapse of twelve months She was practically an apprentice for that length of time.—Mr. Armitage: And you expect a young lady who has been highly educated, and is well dressed, to serve you for twelve months for the munificent sum of is. a day, and to work twelve hours a day P—The Man- ager : I deny your assertion that sne works twelve hours a day.—Mr. Armitage : You must listen to what I say.—The Manager : But not withoutsaying something in my own defence.—Mr. Armitage : As magistrates we sit here day by day and send young persons to prison. We have made inquiries into the causes of such eases— why so many young women are brought before us for theft and prostitution—and there is no doubt in our minds that the most active agent in bringing young women to such a pass is the miserable salaries they receive.—(Loud applause in court.) I have no hesita- tion myself in saying it is a great shame.—(Hear, hear.) In the present instance the prisoner would be bound over in her own recognisance to come up for judgment when called upon. The manager left the court protest- ing against the case having been settled without his being heard.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>DANGERS OF PORK PIES.</head>
        <p>THE DANGERS OF PORK PIES.</p>
        <p>The Deputy Coroner for Manchester, on Monday in- vestigated a remarkable case of accidental poisoning. The deceased, Elizabeth Thorpe, was a dressmaker, and lived at 740, Rochdale-road. On the sth inst. she pur- chased a pork pie at a shop in Hilton-st., of which she and her two sisters ate portions. The next day all three were seized with symptoms of poisoning, and the de- ceased in spite of the most efficient medical assistance, succumbed after a week's illness. It was the opinion of the medical men that death was due to some irritant poison. The pork pie was one of a cons'gnment re- ceived by the retail dealer from Messrs. Taylor and Co., of Leicester, and a gentleman representing that firm stated that it was made from perfectly sound meat, of which both he and members of his family had eaten without experiencing any 11l effects. Pies made at the same town had h cen sent to other towns, and six complaints had been received of people having been affected, but in only one case besides the present had there been a fatal result. The presence of poison could in no way be accounted for. The Deputy Coroner was of opinion that no blame could be attached to the manu- facturers, who were a well-known firm, doing an ex- tensive business ; and the jury returned a verdict of death by misadventure. Several cases of poisoning of a similar character are reported from South Wales.</p>
        <p>EPPS'S COOOl.--GNATEFOL AND OOMFORTINO.—" By a thorough knowledge of the natural laws which govern the operations of digestion and nutrition, and by a careful ap- plication of the fine properties of well-selected COCOA, Mr. Epps has provided our breakfast tables with a delicately flavoured beverage which may save us many heavy doctors' bills. It is by the judicious use of such articles of diet that a constitution may be gradually built up until strong enough to resist every tendency to disease. Hundreds of subtle maladies are floating around us ready to attack wher- ever there is a weak point. We may map.- many a fatal shaft by keeping ourselves well fortified with pure blood and a properly nourished frame."---Civil Seivice Gazette.—Made simplywith boiling water or milk. Sold only in packets, by Grocers, labelled J.uvrEs EPPS, &amp; CO., Hororsorr.thic Chemists London. Also Makers of Epps's Cocoaine or Nib-Extract. Tea-like.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>"JOHN BULL."</head>
        <p>THE "JOHN BULL."</p>
        <p>A striking example of that durability which has ren- dered British manufactures so famous in every clime is seen in the " John Bull," the oldest locomotive in exist- ence in the United States, which has just steamed at a fair rate from Jersey City to Chicago, hauling two equally antiquated passenger-carriages. Doubt had been manifested as to the ability of the engine to main- tain the time which had been scheduled for it ; while some even questioned whether the train would he able reach its destination, the World's Fair, of its own accord. But all qualms have been fully set at rest, for the old English locomotive not only kept time, but it could have been run at a considerably greater speed notwithstanding a heavy and constant rain. Indeed, in , the words of the driver, ' It responded with wonderful alacrity to every demand made." Naturally the engine was furbished up a little before being sent out into the cold world once more, but it is practically the same "John Bull " which left the shops of George and Robert Stephenson at Newcastle-on-Tyne and arrived in Phila- delphia in August, 1831. The circumstances of the im- portation of the locomotive into the United States are nteresting,. A Mr. Robert L. Stevens, was, about the same period mentioned, president and chief engineer of the Camden and Amboy Railroad. In 183 G he visited this country, as did many more, to learn as much as possible concerning railway matters, and here he wit- nessed the trial ot Stephenson's " Planet," the success of which so pleased him that he immediately ordered from the makers a similar engine for his line. That locomo- tive was the" John Bull," which is now the property of the United States Government, and considered one of the greatest treasures of the Washington National Museum collection.—P•tei.</p>
        <p>A FRENCH SYNONYM FOR "ENGLISH-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>MEN."</head>
        <p>MEN."</p>
        <p>For a long time it has been the fashion in Paris to regard all pickpockets as Englishmen, so that the very word has been adopted from our language, and has dis- placed the French word " filou." Cow it seems that every act of vandalim is to be attributed to us. Marie Antoinette's chair having had small pieces cut out of it by relic-hunters, the custodian at the Conciergerie the Queen was imprisoned, and where the chair still remains, recently explained that " English tourists " were to blame, and that he had had the injured relic transferred to a place of safety in the building. A new and still more damaging indictment is now made against visitors in Paris who hail from perfidious Albion. Complaint having been made that the most interesting parts of Notre Dame Cathedral were closed to the public gaze, M. Magnard,the Cute of Notre Dame, has been appealed to on the subject. His only object, he declares, is to protect that precious his- torical structure from the ravages of "snobs." Latterly, he says, there have been a number of pieces of tapestry, enamel, and other objects—including a key—missing, and he adds, almost as a matter of course,that he thinks English tourists are at the bottom of the business. It does not seem to occur to the worthy prelate that there are French" snobs" (we should say thieves) as well as English.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>MR. BRYCE AND THE SOUTHPORT BENCH.</head>
        <p>MR. BRYCE AND THE SOUTHPORT BENCH.</p>
        <p>At a special meeting of the Southport Town Council on Tuesday evening, Mr. Dimond, chairman of the Watch Committee, moved a vote of thanks to Mr. Bryce M.P., Chancellor of the Duchy of Lancaster, for remov- ing the political disparity which until recently existed in the composition of the Southport Borough Bench, and also thanking him for recognising the claims of labour by placing two working-men on the Borough Bench. Ile disavowed Mr. Curzon as a representative of Liberal Southport. He was elected by the rest of the division. —Mr. Pilling described Mr. Curzon as a bantam cock.— Mr. Boocock declared Mr. Bryce to have committed jobbery.—Mr. Watson said the object of the motion was not to compliment Mr. Bryce, but to throw mud at Mr. Curzon,—Pr, Pollard said the Tories and not the Liberals were to blame for forcing a political question on the Council.—Mr. Robson, who spoke amid soma excitement and withdrew a word be had used towards Mr. Threl- fall, leader of the Labour party, said he was prepared to debate the subject all night.—At ten o'clock the motion was carried by a large majority.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>" LABOUR GAZETTE."</head>
        <p>THE " LABOUR GAZETTE."</p>
        <p>The first number of the " Labour Gazette," the journal of the Labour Department of the Board of Trade, was issued on Monday night. It states in its introduc- tion that the " Gazette ' is a journal for the use of workmen and of all others interested in having prompt and accurate information on matters specially affecting labour. It is intended to stand to labour questions in the same relation as the "Board of Trade Journal " to questions of trade and commerce. Among other things, the first number contains reports on the state of trade from the Labour Correspondents in Manchester, Oldham and Burnley.</p>
        <p>The Select Committee of the House of Commons on Corn Sales recommends that the sales of corn through- out the country should be conducted by weight only,and that the hundredweight of 112 lbs. should be the standard.</p>
        <p>A woman named Auckling murdered her son, aged four, at Ipswich on Wednesday night. She borrowed a knife from a man in the street and cut the lads throat. The boy was taken to the hospital, and died soon after admission. The woman did not attempt to escape. She made her way to the hospital, crying " Oh, pity me."</p>
