      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>SCHOOL CHILDREN POISONED: A DANGEROUS PLANT.</head>
        <p>SCHOOL CHILDREN POISONED: A DANGEROUS PLANT.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Under the above caption, you have a paragraph which has been going the round of the papers. In it there is a great amount of confusion as to the name of the very common plant which has caused the calamity, viz., the fool's parsley—./Ethusa tlynapium L. This herb and the hemlock have little in common except inclusien in the same natural order, and in being both dangerous plants. Hemlock—the conium maculatum of botany—is net a local plant, and is easily distinguished from fool's parsle)—which is a common garden weed. My object in writing is to call attention to the fact, thus made evident, that it would be much more conducive to the public weal were children taught more of the com- mon properties of common things ; even at the expense of some of the traditional rubbish with which their little minds are now crammed. A double benefit would thus ensue; the children themselves wyuld be enlightened and some little of this knowledge might percolate up- ward into the region of journalism, and so prevent a repetition of the "scientific" heresy, that feol's parsley " is known to science " as conium maculatum.—Truly yours,</p>
        <p>JOHN PEER.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>Haws, Penketh, 15th May, 1893. GAMBLING IN ADULT AND CHILDREN'S LIVES.</head>
        <p>The Haws, Penketh, 15th May, 1893. GAMBLING IN ADULT AND CHILDREN'S LIVES.</p>
        <p>84,—The above subject is now attracting the atten- tion of local and national institutions,wko will assuredly bring to bear that public opinion which is so much needed, to change the present state of things. It is coming to the front as one of the refonns,to educate the people and check the gambler, whether on the turf or in the gambling club. I read the remarks of your leading article of last week,in connection with Justice Wills' strong comments, at the Liverpool Assizes. The judge stated "That the Longer he sat on the • bench the more he saw that in the insurance of the lives of infants there was a certain appreciable number of cases in which it was an insurance of death rather than insurance of life. That there was an appreciable residuum in which insurance did have a bad effect, no judge who had had a lengthy experience in the criminal court entertained any doubt. The judge's remarks were undoubtedly derived from personal experience, and from what he had observed in the different law courts, and they must be taken as weighty evidence against the practice of insuring inno- cent lives for sums of money by persons who gain by the death, and against the practice of entering these innocents once, twice, and even a third tirrlP in as many offices by either parents, uncles, aunts, or grandparent's to the very full limit allowed by law,namely, six pounds under five years, and ten pounds under ten years. The cases which are allowed to come into court are indeed few, this being a point well guarded against by offices, who know well that the severe strictures passed ' upon them by many judges and magistrates are not con- ' Oucive to the rapid progress of the insurance business. should be pleased to give your readers a few hints re- lating to methods employed to secure contracts, if such they can be named. In order to ventilate this most greivoes question through the columns of your valuable paper, I most earnestly declare that hundreds of thou- sands of policies, and hundreds of thousands of peunds, are issued and paid by a few insurance offices in the United Kingdom on children's policies. Thesn have been issued by offices without asking for, or receiving, the sienatures of the lawful parents (who am the only persons entitled to enter their children), and without many of those children being seen or examined in the slightest, by either the agent, collector, or "district superintendent. Any man, woman, or child is thus entered without any signatures whatever, and all this is treated as being done in accordance with justice. I would ask your readers if this way of doing business is calculated to check and prevent any number of unscru- pulous persons from participating in funds which are,or should be, intended for the honest assurers</p>
        <p>I think the time has arrived to submit these methods to the light of public criticism. Life assurance is fast becoming a vast tradivg institution rather than a bene- volent one—which was intended by its founders. Gamb- ling is a national evil, permeating every class and condi- tion of men, and is encouraged by every subtle device, by persons who are found among the just and the un- just, from the highest to the lowest. The law is power- less to check it : the Church fails to grapple with it. Gambling policies are issued broadcast, contrary to insurance laws now on the Statute Book. It appears to be nobody's business to stop them. All this,l say is done for a purpose. What,then, can the object be in rejecting the legal forms P—Yours faithfully, JOHN 2.IOORFIELD. 44, Parker-st., Warrington, May 18th, 1893.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>IS MAN AN IMMORTAL BRING?</head>
        <p>IS MAN AN IMMORTAL BRING?</p>
        <p>Sir,—The words " Immortal "and " Immortality" are found in the Bible, the former once only ; the latter five times ; and if an unbiassed consideration of the passages in which they occur does not demolish the theory of natural immortality a voice from heaven would fail to do so.</p>
        <p>In Ist Timothy, Ist chapter, 17th verse. Paul writes. " Now unto the King eternal, imnzoPtal, invisible, the only wise God, be honour and glory for everand eve r.' This is sublime language used for a sublime purpose— the glory of God—and the words employed are such as could only apply to the Divine Being. It is a note- worthy fact that in describing the attributes of that Being the sacred writers never use adjectives that elan Could appropriate to himself. No human language can convey an adequate idea of the chlracter of Him who inhabiteth eternity, and the application of any of His at- tributes to human beings is nothing short of blasphemy. In writing the verse I have quoted, Paul had no idea that, far (loan the ages, false prophets would arise and wrest the word immortal from its proper place in his des- cription, and apply it to creatures who may be com- pared to the beasts that perish. But the loose and ir- reverent theology of our day does this, and thus dis- honour the Creator by investing his creature with one of His most inscrutable characteristics. But why have the theologions stormed at this ? If man is immortal why not eternal, invisible, and only wise ? Having endowed him with one of the Divine attributes named by Paul their logical course would be to "crown the edifice "by adding the others, and thus place him on a level with his Maker. But the theologians are too astute for this, as they knew full well that any attempt to turn men in- to Gods would endanger their craft and cover them with ridicule. Men, unfortunately, are only too easily deceived ; but they could hardly he persuaded, even by their priests, to regard themselves as eternal, not created ; invisible, but not visible ; and only wise, and not ignorant and foolish. I will now cite a passage in which there are four simple words which, in my opinion, cempletely des- troy the natural immortality theory. In his first epistle to Timothy, chapter vi. verse 16, the Apostle of the Gentiles, referring to the "'King of kings and lord of lords," uses these remarkable words, " Who only bath Immortality dwelling in the light which no man can ap- proach unto." Here then is a clear and emphatic state- sent that God only hath immortality; and the state- ment being a Divine revelation we are driven to the conclusion that man, in his natural condition, has no part or lot in the endless being which is an essential prerogative of the Almighty. Indeed, his whole history from the beginning until now is a mournful proof that when his breath is taken away he dies, and retivns to his original dust. Adam, the first man, died, anwhat is the world to day but a vast cemetery full of graves of his posterity, who, in all ages and in all climes, have suffered the death penalty! But there is hope that at some future period a portion at least of the human race will become possessed of the power of an endless life. One of the leading tenets of the Christian faith is the doctrine of the resurrection from the dead. In 2nd Thessaloniane, 4 chap. and verse 14, Paul expresses this belief in these words, " For if we believe that Jesus died and rose again, even so them , also which sleep in Jesus will God bring with him." And the question at once suggests itself, what are the qualifications for that glorious destiny? The same</p>
        <p>apostle, in the seventh verse of the second chapter of Romans, answers the question thus," To them who by patient continuance in well doing seek for glory and honour and immortality, eternal life." It is evident, therefsire, that immortality, even in the csse of the saints, pertains not to this life, but must be understood in the future tense. They seek for it now because they have it not ; but it will be bestowed upon them when " The Lord himself shall descend from heaven with a shout, with the voice of the archangel, and the trump of God : and the dead in Christ shall rise first." 2. Thess. iv., 16. Then, and then only, will" this mortal" put on immortality, and " this corruptible put .on incorruption," 1 Cor. xv., 53, 54. " The wages of sin is death ; but the gift of God is eternal life through Jesus Christ our Lord," Romans vi, 23. And yet in the face of all these solemn declarations, which are as immutable as the Being who inspired them, the pulpit goes on deluding the people with the Devil's falsehood, " "Ye shall not surely die," Gen iii, 4. Two reasons for this are, unfortunately, only too obvious. The occupants of the pulpit are not inspired; for men who have recourse to books and manuscripts and other human helps in delivering this message must not be offended if we cannot regard them as speaking with the power and demonstration of the Spirit of God. And then our spiritual guides being reverend men, and masters in arts, doctors in divinity, and so forth, must surely be wise above what is written. Moreover, it might even be permissible to hint at another reason for the heterogeneous theology of the modern pulpit. Preachers appear to be more anxious to spread the shibboleths of the sects whose servants they are, than to search out and make known the simple but -eternal truths of GA for the benefit of perishing men. Such a line of action pays best, is popular among religionists, and brings praise instead of persecution to those who adopt it.—Yours truly, lONA.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>.., ""'",•01r----"" -- '--</head>
        <p>.., ""'",•01r----"" -- '--</p>
        <p>WHAT IS SALVATION?</p>
        <p>Sir,—Salvation depends upon hell, with which it is indissolubly inked;l it is a child of hell, and if you des- troy hell, salvation of necessity disappears. What then is this hell? In the old testament man's troubles ended with this life, and the curses and denunciations of the priest pursued him no farther; he was allowed to rest there, he was hounded no more : "Thou shalt surely die," was his worst sentence, which he could contem- plate with comparative composure ; he was threatened with no unspeakable post-mortem tortures, his troubles stopped at the grave, he slept with his fathers. The advent of Christ ushered in the ghastly dogma of an eternal burning hell, which threw a pall over the lives of men, and clouded with awful gloom their future existence. Christ seems too good to have invented this doom of man, and as he never wrote a line himself, it is more than probable his biographers invented it for him. Be that as it may, the result was a dreadful addition to the woes of man, and the consequences were far-reaching and inestimable. Everlasting burning was the un- believer's doom, and the priest was the way primarily to escape it. What a terrible power was this placed in the hands ot fallible, and often unscrupulous, priests, throughout the ages of superstition which have not yet ended. . . .</p>
        <p>Even now, we see the big brewer erecting his church as a premium on " fire insurance," and the sweater, the shady speculator. or the dishonest trader, attends "divine service," because he cannot quite divest his mind of this dreadful hell; it smy perhaps be truc, and you cannot be too careful ; it's as well to make sure. But it does not for one moment prevent him from sweat: ing, speculating, or trading dishonestly. How many lives and minds have been blasted, and their owners consigmed to madhouses, through this hell. How many poor innocent women and children, the young, the beautiful ,the good ,have been completely undone,have be- come hypochondriacs, monomaniacs, through this detest- able hell. From it were borrowed the flames of the awful auto-da-fe, its lurid glare shone around the faggot at Smithfield, and wrapped itself around the poor devoted body of the witch. It was the strongest plank in the platform of Wesley and Whitefield, and the former be- lieved in it as thoroughly as be (lid in witchcraft, of which he said, to destroy it was to destroy the bible. With how much more truth can we say, to destroy hell is to dretroy the bible ? Says Wesley in his "Journal," of a young girl of nineteen: "I found her on the bed, two or three persons holding her. It was a terrible sight. Anguish horror and despair, above all description, appeared in her pale face. The thou- sand distortions of her whole body, showed how the dogs of hell were gnawing at her heart, the shrieks intermixed, were scarcely to be endured, she screamed out lost for ever . . . . . . _</p>
        <p>. , , I will go with him to hell. I cannot be saved. She then fixed her eyes on the ceiling, and said : There he is, ay, there he is ! Come, good devil, come ! _Take me away " " This is but a sample of thousands of cases that happened all over the kingdom during the Methodist preaching of the eighteenth century, the early part of the nineteenth, and which occasionally happen now. Hell was so painted before them, that it :lied all their minds, which could dweli on nought else ; it :ad always present, and pursued and worried them to the asylum or the grave ; those that stopped short of these became in too many cases morose, ascetic, and bore an eternal gloom about them. • Let us throw this detestable creed of hell, back in the teeth of the priest who would force it down our throats; let us ridicule and condemn tha idea, of countless thou- sands flame-licked for ever, whose only faults perhaps were not believing a man was a God, who lived eigh- teen hundred years ago, in an intensely credulous age, in a small corner of Asia, among a people who then and there rej,cted him, and to whom he was specially sent. But hell is being relegated fast, to the same limbo as witchcraft, astrology, and other expired beliefs. Let them go. Let them never again thrust their grisly beads out of the black oblivion to which they are consigned ; let them never again bury their hideous claws in the hearts of the innocent and lovely,—who are so peculiarly susceptible to their attacks; never let their gorgon stare again petrify the hearts and souls of those who would otherwise be gentle and kind ; and the lives of men cannot fail to be lighter and brighter with the banishment of this last great incubus. Salvation is exemption from hell, hell is a contemptible fallacy, a hideous lie—hence we all have salvation.</p>
        <p>Man needs salvation from many wrongs and crimes but ho does not need it froni. hell. The flames of hell are as extinct as the oracle of Delphi, the fires of the vestals, as the burning faggot of the witch. What then is Salvation ? In a biblical sense it is a meaningless word, without force, use, or gravity, and it is well it is so. _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>SNODGRASS.</head>
        <p>SNODGRASS.</p>
        <p>WHAT IS SALVATION °</p>
        <p>Sir,—The above simple question was recently put to such of your readers as might see their way clear to rush in with an answer. To the great disappointment ef the party who put the question no answer came. I am not likely to crack such a hard theological nut, but will, with your permission, sir, just talk a little at it, always keeping at a safe distance. For near upon thirty years I have been an interested, and, at times, an active looker-on of religious polemics, trying by such hard study as was possible to me,to solve the question of an hereafter, so that at least such con- science as I possess should be at rest, and the conclusion I have come to is, that I know as much as the parson, and, that he knows as much as I do, about the Almighty and His ways, and that both put together, we know nothing. I know that we " know-nothing" people, come in for a pretty good share of ridicule. It is assumed that we are careless about the matter, that we are not honest in our inquiry, that we stifle the still small voice—and so on. No stone is too big to throw at us. We are fitted to bring children into this world, but not fitted to take care of them. Some of us are endowed with great intellectual powers, yet, ere we can gain a seat in Parliament we must go through a slow course of murder. In fact murder and persecution become a fine art in the hands of Christians when a determined op- ponent to traditional Biblical exposition dares to be thorough. My charges are strong, yet the history of the past dozen years will bear them out. I can fancy some say- ing " Oh yes, that's all right, .but why reap all this stale history up, under the heading of your letter ?" I worked for years alongside of Mr. Bradlaugh, and Mrs. Besant. I knew their honesty of purpose. I had heard a leading Unitarian, of all other men, state in the Co- operative Hall that it was " license not liberty these people want." I had repelled base insinuations when I had heard their names bracketed in an unseemly manner together, and yet Mr. Editor, I lived to see, and to know that there were none tao poor whether in press, pulpit, or market place to do reverence to . the. memory of oae who surely knows now what salvation is. Yes, we " know-nothing " folk ask from those who are paid servants of the public, those who are supposed to pass a large portion of their time in prayerful study of the bible what is the salvation we all have to die for before we can have its blessing. Let us coma right down to the fundamental question. Salvation means" to be saved from the bondage of sin, and liability to eternal death." The theological sin, as here given, is the sin we are supposed to have inherited from the Garden of Eden episode. Eternal death so far as I can understand it, must mean simply annihilation when physical death comes to us. It may mean to some theological casuist some kind of spiritual resurrec- tion so that the soul may be fitted to endure the tor- ments of hell. On this latter point I am quite willing to follow any kindly light that may offer to lead me on to truth. I have been much interested in the spirited discus- sion between Mr. Dignum and Harry Esmond, but,after all, I could only ask—well, what matter whether it is T.weedledum or Tweedledee ? Sir Edwin Arnold has given to us a beautiful Indian literature, but not, in my opinion, one that is likely ever to become in touch with the spirit of the coming ages like the ethical portion of the New Testament. It is with the so-called super- natural sides of the later books RS compared with the</p>
        <p>account of Genesis of the creation of man, the tempta- tion and ths fall with the declared prophecies and re- ferences in the following books to a coming Messiah who offers himself as a sacrifice for our sins, that the 'whole question of Salvation rests. Possibly lam wrong in this latter statement, if so. I shall be pleased to have the error pointed out. There can be no doubt, however, that the popular characteristic of Christianity is the con- nection between what I have called the Garden of Eden episodes and the Crucifixion.</p>
        <p>-Scieatific men have, by following their special lines of inquiry, destroyed the astronomy, the geology, and the special creation teachings of the Bible. The philosophers have aided and abetted. Nuw the higher criticism in the church itself is holding up the Bible as a book to be venerated for its age, for its poetry, and for ita moral lessons, but not at all as a book to be relied upon by the student of evolution.</p>
        <p>I have no doubt that the booklets issued by Professor Drummond during the last few years are well known to the readers of the " Examiner." For myself, I have read them with great pleasure, but no where could I percive in them any indication that the Professor had some strong mental reservations touching the account given in Genesis of the creation. And yet this must be so if I understand the point of his present series of lectures on evolutionary problems. "Nothing," says he, "is 1 more certain than that the ancient progenitors of man lived an aquatic life. At one time there was nothing but water life, and the animals are late inventions. The first successful attempt to go ashore was made by the. congo worm, but man did not attempt to go ashore antil after ha had reached the stage of the fish. The four gill slits of the fish are still, represented in man. They are seen in the embryo of the human being." Again the Professor tells us that ears are sometimes found in human embryos bursting out half-way down the neck, at the place which the gill slits would occupy if they still per- sisted. These vestiges appear not only in human beings but also in the lower animals. One more quotation and I have done with the Christian Profession. " Evolution taught us," he said, that a good part of the evil in the world came from the animal. The propensity to evil was a survival &lt;,r vestige of the past, and sin was the self-assertion of the brute. Sin, in tact, was the animal residue, the" Old Adam of the Apostle Paul." I know not whether readers of the " Examiner "will be able to reconcile these statements, which the Professor says" it would be mocking human intelligence to deny "with the usual sermons and :prayers that are heard in a Christian assembly, but lam not. No one should exult at this. I certainly do not, but it is quite evident that the time of the Christian minister, and the money paid him, are so much waste. I say, that I feel no pleasure at this, and why ? because I long myself to know of and to:lope for eternal life, in other words immortality. The Christian religion is certainly the purest state of religious thought yet attained by man, but there are abundant signs that it is making way for ona yet higher and grander, the religion of humanity. As there are decided economic changes going on, so the religious intellectual life is changing. A mighty revolution is working out our salvation. Change all alon., the line. We have looked above for a heaven, and below for a hell and so have missed the cards we trod on. Our salvation lies hero as Burns says,— - - .</p>
        <p>, " To make a happy household clime For weans and wife, Is the true pathos and sublime Of human life." CLIFFORD DI X,</p>
        <p>SUPERSTITION AND SMALLPDX.</p>
        <p>Sir,—What do we know of smallpox 1 Why does it come? Why does it go away? What do we know of its source, and how should it be treated ? There has been a lot said and a great deal written about it ; unfortu- nately we are but little the wiser for these exertions. What is the nature of our ideas respecting it and how have we obtained them ? Our ideas have been obtained by the simple process of superstition, and their nature is of a mJst fearful kind, for superstition declares it to be deadly infectious and this is universal. That small- pox is catching, all the world is as certain of as our forefathers were of the existence of witchcraft ; as the Turks are of the evil-eye, and as thousands of our own country folk are of the dreadful consequences which are sure to follow the seeing of a magpie or the beginning of any enterprise on ,unlucky Friday. Shall we say that those people were silly,superstitious, and ignorant? No, sir, not more so than these of tim present time, who believe that smallpox is infectious. " Everybody knows that it is." "Nothing can be plainer ; " facts speak for themselves." Yes, and I say facts appeared to support the existence of witchcraft. There were always plenty of witnesses to swear to facts ; numbers of persons suf- fered death for being reputed witches who were tried in open court and found guilty on what was thought to be convincing evidence. With regard to seeing a magpie I have heard people tell of many dreadful things which happened after see- ing this poor unlucky bird. Indeed " I could a tale unfold," which would tend to make many credulous persons think it no joke to see a black bird with a patch of white feathers on its wings. I can say that those things were true also, that the persons who gave evid- ence against the supposed witches were not liars! What then ! Do I believe in witchcraft ? Not I. Do I believe that seeing a magpie or the beginning of a new job on a Friday bring bad luck ? Not a bit of it. Nor do I believe that any one person has ever had smallpox simply because some one else had had it. The mistake has been that the saddle has been put on the wrong horse. Thus the reputed witches were blamed for nearly all the evil things that bad ever happened. We have now learnt that bad things will happen without the aid of witehes, that trouble will come if we do not see a magpie, that success does not depend upon the day we begin our affairs but in the ability we display in their management. Neither is it possible for any.one to take , smallpox because acme other body has it. Now, what I want to make clear is, that all the cases of superstition cited above were inseparable from trouble ; also that the superstition of infectious diseases is not only pregnant with trouble but is most persis- tently giving birth to troubles. On the other hand, sound knowledge brightens a man's life and tends to make him more useful and happy. What has our ex- perience been during this last epidemic? Truly fear I has been our portion. I may say fear has been our tyrannical master who has ruled us with a rod of iron. 1 Neighbouring towns' folks looked upon us with appre- hensions of danger. They would not employ our labourers nor would they buy from our tradesmen ; we were not welcome to go anywhere, and no one would come to see us. We were afraid to ride and we thought it risky to walk. Football was fcrbidden for fear of spreading smallpox. Writing was not always to be thought of because joeJple were afraid to receive our letters. We were afraid to read our own books from the library for cruel fear had transformed those blessed friends of ours, these ministers of pleasure and instruc- tion, into possible vehicles of infection. Where, oh where, could infection hide its dirty head? Could it hide behind a comma; or like a mischievous spirit throw a summersault from the top of a note of interrogation down the reader's throat !! No certainly not. The accursed thing we feared was a thing of our own make. A misty indefinite nothing which "nobody knows nothing at all about." Sir I can't understand this quo- tation which is somewhat excusable, seeing that "nobody knows nothing, about it unless it be a concen- trated nothing called infection. This supposed quality of smallpox is the power by which disease multiplies itself.</p>
        <p>Here lies the difficulty. Nothing but living bodies multiply themselves. Smallpox is not a living body, therefore it is impossible for it to multiply itself. Small- pox is not a separate entity, it is only a simple condi- tion of a living body, and a simple condition is immov- able, therefore it cannot of itself leave one body for another. Nor can it move from one part of the same person to another part ot the same person. Thus it hap- pens sometimes that some parts of the body is covered with smallpox, at the same time other parts ef the body are free and they remain s) to the end. I myself have had smallpox severely in the head whilst my body was almost free. Now from these facts arises a most impor- tant question, i.e, If fever or smallpox, being in pos- session of parts of the body, cannot infect the whole of that body, how is it possible for it to infect persons and things at variable distances extending to a thousand yards and under? The immediate cause of small- pox is within, not from without. .Yes, Sir, smallpox comes out of a man, it never moves towards a man, for nature herself directs it frem him. I feel as if someone asks this question: if nature direct it froin him,to whom does it go? To no one. When smallpox leaves a man, that is an end of it. It ceases to be smallpox ere it leaves him.</p>
        <p>Let us suppose for an instant that, i•stead of being panic-stricken, we had felt certain that smallpox was in- noxious, this epidemic that we have scarcely passed through would have fallen upon us but lightly in coin- parison with the dreadful affliction we have had. The sick would have been nursed at home. We should have conducted ourselves as usual,our library would not have been closed, our trade would not have suffered,our foot- ball would not have been stopped. We should have saved abut £l,OOO, which has been spent in vaccina- tion and all the pain attendant, besides the vast sums spent in hospital accommodation. There would have been no great fear,for have we not been afraid of every- thing and everybody ? God has put Us in families in order that we might the better help each other in the time of need ; also we have been very properly taught "that the path of duty was the path of safety." Our duty has been sent to the hospital, and we bravely ran away from the ugly things our ignorant fancies wade.— Yours, am.,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 20, 1898.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 20, 1898.</p>
        <p>461, Kputsford-road, Latchford.</p>
        <p>F. SUMMERS.</p>
        <p>THE PARISH COUNCILS BILL.</p>
        <p>The "Standard ' says: We understand that the Liberal County members have received definite assur- ance from the Government that the Parish Councils Hill will be passed before the present session is concluded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND</p>
        <p>ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>CRICKET.</p>
        <p>Victory in the fallest strength of its moaning crowfeet) the efforts of the Warrington first eleven, in their con- test wits Leyland. The totals were 233 to 56. Orno might well say, with one of lbsen's characters "Fancy that now—eh.". And, when I looked at the scores after the match, and made individual comparisons, I was assuredly struck by the great cont:ast ; by the superior play which was sufficiently tesn,fied for on the part of our eleven. Only three of our players ran under 16 whilst not ons man in the Leyland ranks even scored 14. The highest scores for the resptctive teams were 11. Smith (tor Warrington), 68 ; T. T3ogue (for Leyland) Li.</p>
        <p>Leyland certainly could plead as an extenuatlion for their dire defeat the absence of those brotherly champion Willow switchers," the two Tinsley's ; but,. then, their loss was very little more than the low which Warrington sustained by the absence of S. M. Crnsfield. However, in the way of bowling Sugg and Smith man- aged the absentees' work kr a snug kind of way-. W. Pedmore came out quite brilliantly and showed aidis- position to improve upon his fine play of last year. He was run out for 57. He and Voisey were in together and in 25 minutes their combined scores ran up to 30, just six less than the whole of Leyland made in mere than an hour. Not so bad was it ? Sugg owing to an accident was unable to bat, after doing splendid tied work and bowling.</p>
        <p>As I hinted last week, Sankey Bridges is going in, " neck or nothing " to produce some cricketers—of one sort or the other. Let us hope the other. I Sankey Wesleyan junior team has juet popped up, and on Satur- day it donned—collectively speaking—what cricketing apparel it possessed and laid out to lick the bold juniors of Bank Quay. But unlike the cork in Muldoon's whiskey bottle, " it didn't come off " ; at least not to the extent one of the members had prophesied. They scored 39 against 33. Taking play all round it was very encouraging, and I would advise both teams to keep on battering away, and some day, maybe, they will make that high-flying set—the Warrington first eleven—quail considerably when they cast down the gauntlet before them.</p>
        <p>Grappenhall team were the players whom the Sankey Bridges Wesleyans C.C. "took oia." And when the game ended with 74 for the Wesleyans and 44 for" ye Grapenhollers," I heard.it said that the latter adduced a very good excuse for their failure ; at least one member did. lie said their players wern't much interested in the Willow now—not near so much as they were with the " hash." Fancy that now—eh ? They seem to be meet- ing success at "hashing," anyhow. The Wesleyans were only seven wickets down when the game closed. Grappenhall had eight wickets, five of which Watkins took for 13 runs !</p>
        <p>To-day there is likely to be a crowd with their optics glued down—figuratively—to the play of the Wesleyan C.C. and their dear friends, to whom I alluded last week, the Wire Mill team. I have been asked what will result for this game and I said : Wire (why-er) Mill-ing for one team. And my interrogator said " Thou art a vile punt-ster---and it is not football at all." Seriously, though, I hope that this match will have the effect of throwing " ile on the troubled whart-ers," and that good feeling will again be restored between the two teams who have been rent asunder by over much envy and zeal.</p>
        <p>The Sankey Wesleyan's second had hard lines in their match with Grappenhall secosd, only losing the match by 40 to 38. They began their innings in a very prom- ising way, but fell off towards the end, Booth being top scorer with 11. The fielding of the Wesleyans was not very first class and certainly lost them the game. The Grappenhall captain T. Robinson showed good form and was in the greater part of the innings, and last man out with 15.</p>
        <p>Bewsev-road Wesleyans meta " selected team," who were one too many for them—indeed they were 67 too many for them. Gibson for the selectedlones made 32 towards the total of 97, while no individual score over seven was made towards the 35 for the Wesleyans.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>Bow Ls.</head>
        <p>Bow Ls.</p>
        <p>The green at the Horse and Jockey, has been open for some weeks, but the first League match was played on it on Saturday, when the club was visited by the Red Lion players. Councillor Beaman is the president, and C mncillor Bonnet! the vice-president. The game on Saturday resulted in a win for the Horse and Jockey by 189 to 127. The 15's were well upon on both sides. The ground was in excellent condition.</p>
        <p>With regard to the Legh Challenge Competition Newton Bowling Club i making proverbial tracks for the cup. They met Park-lane on Saturdayand managed to beat them by 159 to 117. That was en the Newton Green, and later on they me., then' on the Park-lane Green and made a tie of it. Etch team consisted of eight bowlers, and the scoring all round was above the average.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>CYCLING.</head>
        <p>CYCLING.</p>
        <p>Members of the Warrington C.C. were ruralising on Saturday. They sought out for a run one of the prettiest snots about. It was to Gaws worth, situated on the Derbyshire border, and reputed ti be resplendent with natural beauty and ancient hotels with old oak furni- ture, and other old things—including fogies. The Peak in Derbyshire was much admired, only I be- lieve the members would be:very much "piqued" if they had to admire it too much.</p>
        <p>A chronicler of the run, after dilating upon the tea, says :—" After gazing in admiration for some time over the beauty of the scene around them, tbs party left," fully imbued with the ectacising sublimity and grandeur of Mother Nature's appearance. Dear me! Would that our itinerant poet had been there, how envious he would have been of the chronicler and his "divine inflatus."</p>
        <p>Warrington wheelers wheeled themselves off in diffe- rent directions on Saturday, notwithstanding the fact that they had a fixed run. I should advise them to keep a little more together if they have the welfare of their club at heart. "Absence makes the heart grow fonder," and the captain is getting anxious to see some of hi! "crowd."</p>
        <p>The captain, by the way, assisted in the Manchester' Wheelers' 50 mile road race which started from Grappen- hall. Schefer came in first, C. Fletcher second, and J. Bennett third. The latter also succeeded in breaking five machines during the race which was breaking the record in one sense. GOLF.</p>
        <p>The Hon. T. W. Legh, M.P., by beating Mr. Beton- Carr, M.P., has secured his entry into the final of the Parliamentary Golf Handicap, but Mr. A. J. Robertsom, of the Press Gallery, and Mr. Howe Browne, of the official staff of the House of Commons, could only half their match and will have to try cenclusions again. The final will not be played until after Whitsuntide.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>FOOTBALL FLASHES.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL FLASHES.</p>
        <p>Although the season has ended, we have had another flash from the circles of the leather kickers. It was their annual meeting on Wednesday night, and a suc- cessful affair too. Thanks are certainly due to Mr. P. Davies for the support he has given the game in War- rington and I was glad to see him re-appointed presi- dent; and I was equally glad to see those two mainstays of the club re-appointed without a thought of opposition —Mr. P. Pemberton treasurer, and Mr. J. E. Warren secretary..</p>
        <p>By the way I was sorry to see that by an unaccount- able freak of the ballot Gilbert was excluded from the committee by an adverse majority of three. This was about the only regrettable incident in connection with the meeting, as Mr. Gilbert, apart from his personal popularity, has during the past season, probably done as much g,muine hard work as any member connected with the club. It was he who arranged and conducted the whole of the Junior League Competition, besides doing other work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>DEAUGIITS.</head>
        <p>DEAUGIITS.</p>
        <p>A draughts' match in which Warrington draughts players will take a deep interest, will take place in St. James's Hall, Leeds, on Whit-Monday, when Lancashire will play Yorkshire. The teams are to consist of the pick ot both counties, and it is interesting to note that War- rington eill supply no less than three players for the Lancashire team, namely, Mr. Frank Dunne, Manchester- road ; Mr. J. Weaver, Winsvick-road ; and Mr. P. Walsh, Kerfoot-at. Mr. Frank Dunne, who has been a promi- nent figure amongst draughts players during the last 20 years, has also the hcnour of captaining the Lancashire team. Three previous matches have been played, in which Lancashire have been victorious twice and York- shire once. The Yorkshire team is captained by Mr. Willie Gardner, of Leeds, who came prominently to the frent in 1884,0 n the occasion of the international match England v. Scotland. Shortly after that he met and de- feated the late William Strickland in a set match for a stake of £5O, and he has been the recognised champion of Yorkshire ever since. I hope Mr. Dunne will be able to bring his team out of the contest victoriously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>ILLNESS OF MR. J. WILSON, M.P.</head>
        <p>THE ILLNESS OF MR. J. WILSON, M.P.</p>
        <p>SERIOUS CONDITION.</p>
        <p>It was stated, yesterday morning that Mr. J. H. Wilson, M.P., who is lying ill at his residence at Cam- berwell, passed a very bad night, and that his condition causes considerable anxiety. There is much hemorrhage and his temperature stands at 102. Mrs. Wilson attri- butes her husband's illness to his exertions during the Hull strike, and says that he refused to give up work until almost prostrate.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>ANN-UAL MEETING OF THE WARRING. TON FOOTBALL CLUB.</head>
        <p>ANN-UAL MEETING OF THE WARRING. TON FOOTBALL CLUB.</p>
        <p>--- The annual meeting of the Warrington it ball Club was held on Wednesday evening in the Wovk- ing-men's Club, the Market Place. There were then present: Mr. Percy Davies, president in tha chair ; Mr. Wm. Pielpoint, vice-president ; Mr. J. Evans, vice-president ; Mr. J. E. Warren, hon. sec.; Mr. T. Pemberton, hon. treasurer, and others. ELECTinT4 OF OFFICERS. The following officers were unanimously re-elected : Mr. Percy Davies, presiAnt ; Mr. J. E. Wierreo, hon. sec. Mr. T. Pemberton, hon. treasurer. The following officieala were elected :—Mr.J. T. Bate, captain Ist team; Mr.T.l4. Teare, captain of A. team ; Mr.H. Gibson, cap- tain setond team ; Mr. J. W. Evans, sub-captain first team ; 191 r. T. Lalley, sub-captain A. team ; iMr. U. Ashton, umpire first team; Mr. J. Boyle, umpiee A. team ; Mr. 'F. Smith, uinpire: second team ; Mr. G. Smith, Mr. A. J. Evans, trustees, and the following Committee :—Messrs. W. Hewitt, T. Bowers, J. Evans, T. Barnes, ...P. Jolley, G. Smithi,. Fair Turner, and W. Speakman.</p>
        <p>PRESENTAT:tIN. The CRAM/SSW said he had now arrived at the triA pleasurable part of his duty, and that was to distribute. the cups and media's. He could only hope that in doinqo that he would be-encourag,ing, as fax as he could, the Rugby game of it‘otball in Warrington. (Hear, hear.)) He had told them on previous eccasions that he pre-1 ferred to be the preisident of the Warrington Football ' Club, much more then the Parliamentary member for Warrington, and he was of the same opinion still. (Applause.) If them was a man in. Warrington who hated politics it waa himself ; as a matter of business he would not dabble in politics and he did not think any solicitor ought to do—unless he made something out of at. (Laughter). He was not an election agent, and therefore he was not going to go into politics. (More Ihughter.) He could tell them that what he had done for their club, had given him- more genuine 21easure than they could imagine_ When he de- cided to commemorate his first presidential year by presenting cups, he did not know what sys- tem to adopt. First it was suggested that he should make the present to the most consiEtent and best be- haved player ; but that struck him as being an awk- ward plan because no Committee however unprejudiced they might be would be able to select satisfactorily the most consistent and best behaved player. Then it was suggested that a cup should be given to the player who scored the most tries, but they would no doubt agree with him that the proposition was absurd because it would have encouraged selfish play, and neither do the Rugby game nor the Warrington Club any good. He thought he had done the best thing under the circum- stances in deciding to present the cup not ta the best player, hut to the captain of the first team. (Applause.) Mr.Asliton had seen the Club through a great many trials and difficulties,and had been captain as long as any other person in the room, and it was he who had led them on to victory the year they won the West Lancashire Cup. (Lcud applause.) That,he thought entitled him to a cup more than anyone else. But then there were others whose services deserved recognition. There was the captain of the A team—(applause)—who bore an excel- lent name, the name he bsre himself. (Laughter.) He was sure they would agree with him that there was not , a more popular, or More beloved captain in Lancashire ' than their Tony Davies. (Applause.) EVeryone seemed to speak highly and admiringly of him: (Applause.) Unfortunately not long ago he did something which per- haps lie ought not to have done, although he had every reason to believe that he had done it under the impres- sion that it was the correct thing to do. (Hear, hear.) But to show them the great integ- rity, honesty, and straightforwardness of the man he could tell them that when Tony Davies got to know that he (the Chairman) was going to present him with a cup, he came to him after he had been sus- pended, and to use his own bluff honest words, lie said : "-Master Davies, you're going to give me a cup I believe." " Yes." " Well, I can tell you this, I should think a great deal more on yo if sou'd give it to some- body else, after what I've been doing." (Laughter.) But the Chairman said he told Tony he should give it to him and hoped he would keep it for ever if he could. (Hear, hear.) Then there was another captain and that was Mr. Rowe, the captain of the second team and he was a useful and hardworking member and he had great pleasure in selecting him a cup winner. (Hear, hear.) Then there were two gentlemen who had also received county honours; he alluded to Surefoot Boscow and Mr. Jolley. (Loud applause.) As regarded Boscow he could say he had never met a more unassuming genuine lad in his life than him and when he saw him play in the match against Yorkshire, he was satisfied, he was the finest full back player they had in the north. And the try which it was said had been made against him he had good grounds for believing had not been made at all. (" That's so.") Then there was Jolley. Well although he had not occupied a position quite so respon- sible, it was one which he had fulfilled successfully, and had scored a try. (Applause.). Mr. DAVIES then presented tho eupo, Qua In a silver medal to the following :—J. T. Massey, F. Turner, Eden, J. T. Bate, J. W. Evans, H. Webster J. Lawless, W. Nevins, James Holmes, Herbert Teare, b. Potts, G. Donohue, A.Molyneux, H. Edwards, Heesom, G. Damp- son, E. Bury, T. Lally, Ashurst, T. Thorniley. A miniature cup was also presented to Mr. J. E. Warren, secretary, and to Mr. T. Pemberton treasurer. Mr. Ashton captain of the team was not present to receive his cup, but Mr. D. Tinnion acted as his delegate and thanked Mr. Davies for the gift in a brief speech.—Mr. T. DAVIES in receiving his cup said that he had not had sufficient opportunity to vindicate himself against the charge made against him at Stockport.—A discussion afterwards followed regarding referees. Mr.-S. WRIGHT. J.P., proposed a resolution that the Union be asked in the next League competition to engage referees from another county except Lancashire.—Mr. J. DIXON seconded this, and it was carried unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>TIE REPORTS.</head>
        <p>TIE REPORTS.</p>
        <p>The reports of the treasurer and secretary which were published in the " Examiner " last week were adopted as read.</p>
        <p>VOTES OF THANKS.</p>
        <p>Mr. WALLINGTON had great pleasure in proposing that the heartiest thanks of the meeting be given to Mr. Percy Davies both for his able services during the year and also for his generosity in presenting the cups and medals.— Mr. D. TINNION seconded the vote of thanks, and in do- ing so alluded to the great impetus which their presi- dent had given to the Rugby football in Warrington.— Mr. Wu. PIERPOINT, J.P., supported the resolution. As an old president he felt, since some one had mentioned old presidents, that eornparisons were odious, for he had never hoped to be able to vie with Mr. Davies in the excellent work which he was doing for the club in War- rington. He saw from his attendances that he had been present at a greater number of meetings than any other president, and besides taking a keen interest in the War- rington Club particularly, he also took a wide interest in the game generally. As -an old president, he (Mr. Pierpoint) was glad to see that year by year the club was growing in strength and reputation. He was very jeal- ous of the club's reputation so far as its play went, for dispite the fact that charges had been made against them of roughness, be had no hesitation in saying that the play of the Warrington men was characterized by scrupulous fairness. True, when they were met with a team who infused vigour into their play they were met with a similar element, and an element which was perfectly justifiable. He urged them to always infuse energy and vigour into their play so that it was consonant with the rules of fairness, and proper style. He knew that their president when he was elected was as proud as though he had been made member for the borough. The many duties which he had been called upon to perform had been discharged in a manner which met with the approval and admiration of the members. In choice which Ile had made for the distribution of the cups and medals he knew he had given entire satisfaction. In conclusion he expressed the hope that all who had received medals and cups would never part with them, and ever remember the circumstances under which they were given, and ever remember the great encouragement and consequent impetus which their respected president had given to their alub, and to football generally in Warrington. I (Loud Applause.)—The vote was then put, and carried with acclamatim. Three cheers were given for Mr. Davies and one for Mrs. Davies. The president after- . _ . .</p>
        <p>wards appropriately responded, and mentioned that he had endeavoured to get Mr. Payne, secretary to the County League, to present the cups and medals.</p>
        <p>THE WARRINGTON VOLUNTEERS.</p>
        <p>The members of the Ist Volunteer Battalion (Prince of Wales Volunteers) South Lancashire Re- giment had a church parade on t'unday morning. There was a good muster at the headquarters at the Drill Hall at half-past nine, and a few minutes be- fore ten o'clock the battalion, headed by the band under the direction of Mr. Armstrong, marched to the Parish Church, the route to which was lined with spectators. The Rector of Warrington, as chaplain to the corps, walked immediately behind the band, accom- panied by Major Bridge, and the other officers present were Captains Baynes, Buckton, Edelsten, Hutchinson, and Chorley,Lieutenant Fairclough,and Surgeon Captain Adams. There was a very large congregation, not only Vr,lunteers, hut many soldiers _being present_ froth</p>
        <p>Orford Barracks. The accompaniments lo the hinVti-s were charmingly played by the Volunteer band, and on the organ by Mr. W. S. Woods. The sermon VMS preached by the Rector (the Rev. F. W. Willis), who preached a homily of advice and encouragement to the men. On Tuesday morning at 95, an advance fatigue party, con- sisting of Captains Baynes, Hutchinson, and Chorley, and 37 non-commissioned officers and men, left by train at Bank Quay station for the camp at Conway to pitch the tent and make other necessary arrangements for the general body of Volunteers, who will reach the camp to-night.</p>
