        <p>LATEST NEWS.</p>
        <p>MR. GLADSTONE AT HAWARDEN,</p>
        <p>Mr. Gladstone, notwithstanding his fatiguil journey yesterday,was up early yesterday morning ana attended divine service in the Parish Church. The right hon. gentleman is enjoying capital health. Mrs. Gladstone, who does not appear to have suffered any ill ejects From the joursey, will take carriage exercise in the course of the day. Mr. and Mrs. Gladstone are staying al the Rectory with the Nev. Stephen Gladstone.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>SINANCIAL CRISIS.</head>
        <p>THE SINANCIAL CRISIS.</p>
        <p>TWO AMERIVAN BANKS SUSPENDED. Nuws—fornr, FRIDA,.-9 despatch from Brunswick (Georgia), states that the Oglethorpe Bank and the Natiorrel,Bank have suspended payment, it is added that Mr. Gilman, the president of the first-named bank, has committed suicide by shooting himself with a revol- ver. This fast-named bank is the first of awl National banks whieh has susponded payment..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>HULL STRIKE.. ' __- A ZETTLEMENT ARRIVErir AT:</head>
        <p>THE HULL STRIKE.. ' __- A ZETTLEMENT ARRIVErir AT:</p>
        <p>A meeting of'the Hull Committee of the federation 'was held yesterdey under the chairmanship of Mr. Arthur Wilson. A discussion took place on the proposed terms of agreement drawn up at an informal confer- ence the previoue night, and it rssulted in a reopening ofthe formal negotiations between the selected represen- tatives of shipowaers and docker°, yesterday afternoon. A special correspondent at Hull subsequently telegraph&amp; : The, terns for the resumption of work have been agreed on by the conference,and,subject to a formal ratification at a•mass meeting of dockers, the strike is ended. A FRIGHTFUL DEITHROLL.•</p>
        <p>A THOUSAND MEN KILLED. A Chicago correspondent telegraphs :—A contractor who is running up a hotel outside the grounds, pay-Mg carpenters 50 cents per hour, and plasterers sdols. per day, told me there have been over 1,000 men killed during the construction of the World's Fair. Asked for authentic returns, he said they were not to be had, as the fatalities were often kept quiet. But he was per- sonally certain that the deathroll was not less than 800, and some placed it as high as 1,500.M05t of the accidents occurred outside the grounds in consequence of the reckless haste with which the hotels and lodging-houses have been built.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>YESTERDAY'S PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>YESTERDAY'S PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>HOUSE OF COMMONS. The Speaker took the chair at five minutes past two. o'clock.</p>
        <p>SECVLAS EDUCATION. Mr. SNAPE cave notice that he would call attention to I the present condition of Secular Education in England,. and move a resolution.</p>
        <p>SCOTCH, 1101 IN RULE.</p>
        <p>Mr. WGREGOR gave notice that he intended to call attention to the backward condition of Scotch business,. and move a resolution in favour of Home Rule for Scot- , land. (Laughter.)</p>
        <p>DETENTION OF TRADE parranne. Mr. JOHNSON FEaousonasked (for Mr. Holland) if the President of the Board of Trade was aware that great inconvenience was often caused to British merchants and manufacturers by the detention at the Custom House of patterns bearing their names, such patterns having previously been sent to their correspondents abroad and returned for the execution of orders. Mr. MUNDELL/ said lie had received complaints such as were indicated in the question. He was most anxious to remove all impediments to the traesaction of business, and if the hon. member would bring to his notice a specific case he would communicate with the Customs authorities on the subject.</p>
        <p>DRINK. FOR DRUNKARDS. Mr. Bell introduced a Bill to prohibit the sale of intox- icating liquors to persona of drunken habits. The Bill was read a first time.</p>
        <p>.83A FISHERIES.</p>
        <p>The House went into Committee on the North Sea Fisheries Bill—Mr.Ginson Bow LES resumed the discussion on clause three of the Bill with a protest against allow- ing the right of search of our fishing vessels to foreign cruisers. He describod the Bill as both Miechieveue and silly, and moved the omission of the clause.— Mr. MUNDELL&amp; explained that the Bill was the result of a convention of the nations (except France) interested in destroying the infamous system of ftoating grog shops and floating brothels in the North Sea. The vile spirit aimed at was not made in or supplied by France,, but by Holland and Scandinavia. He hoped Pt aueff WOuhl noon join tho other nationn, the objections of that country having been raised not by the Uovern-- ment, but by a committee of the French Chamber, prac- tically because of our occupation of Egypt.—Mr. TAMES LOWTITER questioned the propriety of the last remark of till right hon. gentleman.—Mr. Ginsow BOWLES said the objection of Franco was that England had usurped the dominion of the seas.—After some further discus- sion, the Bill passed through committee and was read a third time.</p>
        <p>The House then went into Committee of Supply.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>COMMONS. A REMARKABLE INCIDENT.</head>
        <p>COMMONS. A REMARKABLE INCIDENT.</p>
        <p>MR. GLADSTONE AND THE HOUM.E OF</p>
        <p>Mr. T. P. O'Connor writes in the "Sun " of an inte- resting little incident, which has, says a London corres- pondent, not yet, so far as I know, been brought before the public eye. Everybody knows of Mr. Gladstone's immense capacity for work, and it has long slice become a rare sight in the House to find him sitting idle for five minutes at a stretch. In the division lobbies he is al- ways to be seen writing away as if for dear life, totally oblivious to the moving throng around him. But on Tuesday night (writes Mr. O'Connor), for a moment he allowed the natural man to conquer. Selecting a seat in a quiet corner, he fell into a brief, hurried, but pro- found slumber,and was lost to the world of teeming and shouting life around him. The pallid look on the face told of the fatigue of the day, but the splendid mouth, firm set, was there—with that look of unalterable de- termination which conquers all things. It was a beau- tiful and impressive picture, and by a quick and electric communicativeness all its pathos and splendour and historic significance were gathered by the crowd. The usual noise of the lobby was stilled. Silently,reverently, members paused fora moment as they went by—whis- pered a comment in law accents, and passed on with hearts stirred silently but profoundly to reverence, awe, love.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>RATHER STARTLING.</head>
        <p>RATHER STARTLING.</p>
        <p>It is rather startling to be told that in the entire English army, out of many thousands of officers who carry swords, there are comparatively very few who have the smallest notion of using the weapon they carry with that absolute confidence that habit alone gives. This statement, however, was made by Colonel Gordon Ives before a military audience at the United Service Institution, whore it appears to have been received with general assent. Colonel Ives was going down to hunt near his home in Hampshire one day, knowing nothing of the school at Aldershot, and meeting two officers in the train also going to the same meet of the hounds, he said, " If Sir Evelyn Wood will allow me to come and practise in the summer time, when I am at home, at the Fencing School at Aldershot, I shall be very glad." They said no doubt he would. "Do many officers go there ?" inquired Colonel Ives. The reply, we are told, was : "No, hardly one. You will find some instructors there, and you will find some very fairly trained men, but you will not find any .officers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>SHIP CANAL.</head>
        <p>THE SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>On Sunday morning pumping operations were com- menced to fill the tank of the iron swing-bridge that crosses the Ship Canal at Barton and is to replace the old stone aqueduct built by Brindle y for the late Duke of Bridgewater. The new bridge, which- forms a portion of the new deviation works in connection with the Bridgewater Canal, has been partially turned round, but cannot be turned round its full length until the old aqueduct has been removed. The new bridge will be worked by hydraulic power. The new iron tank bridge, which crosses Barton-lane and connects the deviation works on the northerly and southerly side of the road, was tested with water a few weeks ago, and was found to work satisfactorily. A large number of piles have been driven into the Bridgewater Canal at the entrances to the deviation works, but it is not expected that the arrangements will be so far completed as to allow of the diversion of the water into the new portion of the canal by Whitsuntide. Mr. Parker, the resident engineer of the Ship Canal Company, was present during the pump- ing operations. On Sunday a large number of men were engaged in constructing temporary basks in the Ship Canal on the westerly side of Barton Bridge in order to contract the course of the water and enable the men to blow out the rocks which formed the bank of the river /rwell, and which separates the old bed of the river from the new bed of the canal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-20_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>VLOOD AT BARTON.</head>
        <p>VLOOD AT BARTON.</p>
        <p>The heavy rain on Wednesday caused the river to rise, and the water broke through the bank separating the new from the old cutting of the Ship Canal on the westerly side of Barton Bridge. This cutting has been flooded several times since Christmas. Temporary banks which have been constructed on the north and south side of the river for the purpose of getting out the rock in the old bed of the river are submerged, and a large number of men are temporarily thrown out of work.</p>
